},"1474":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-7-27","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-7","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-7#wording-and-source-details-to-check","content":"09:26–09:37: The speaker explicitly corrects “Vanguard” to “Equifax.” Both the earlier wording and later correction remain below."},"1475":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-7-28","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-7","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-7#wording-and-source-details-to-check","content":"09:47–10:08: A worksheet/QR-code tool is a tentative format idea, not an implemented feature."},"1476":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-7-29","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-7","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-7#wording-and-source-details-to-check","content":"10:22–10:33: “credit reports really don’t matter if you care a lot about borrowing money” may contain a missing or misheard word. A second automated pass did not settle the meaning."},"1477":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-7-30","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-7","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-7#wording-and-source-details-to-check","content":"Rates, fees, minimum payments, the 30% utilization claim, eligibility ages, score ranges, payment/reporting timing and card terms are unverified source claims. They need product, country and date context before becoming guidance."},"1478":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-7-31","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-7","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-7#0000000","content":"So yesterday talked about what to do when you don't have a job and how to sort of reverse engineer your lifestyle that you want. I'm going to leave the debt portion to the end. I think I'm going to leave the debt portion to the end. So the other section I talk about are, I should not move the debt portion, I'm going to talk about that at the end. The other aspects I need to cover are credit cards, investing. Yeah, so let's talk about credit cards. So credit cards are, they can be good and bad, for most people they're bad."},"1479":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-7-32","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-7","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-7#0105000","content":"Because most people, including me, are not great at debt management, but we have been sold. But for most people it's already too late, you're already in sort of a hole. The reason why credit cards are good and bad, they act like a double-edged sword because if you use them responsibly, they give you a lot of leverage. In Alex Hormozi's book, I forget which one it is, I think it's the one where it's 100 million dollar leads, I believe. He essentially talks about how you can use your credit card and sort of front costs for 30 days. And sort of have like this infinite loop of money because you can pay for your ads and stuff and you get paid back in 30 days and you can just sort of keep going back, keep going. I have to research exactly what he said, but you can use them for points, you can use them for like, so for example, you're traveling, you get discounts, free upgrades, hotels, same thing."},"1480":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-7-33","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-7","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-7#0207000","content":"You get credits and there's not really a limit on number of credit cards you can have. That being said, I don't want to say the bad outweighs the good, but for most people the bad does outweigh the good because the problem with credit cards is that they allow you to believe that it's free money because of just how easy it is to spend it. Especially now we got like tap to pay, right? You just tap your phone and you can spend money. And especially in the States, a lot like credit cards and stuff, when you're spending money you don't even need to use a pen, you can just like spend the money. So it's kind of crazy. Now, with our credit card, usually when you're getting a credit card, there's different tiers of credit cards."},"1481":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-7-34","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-7","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-7#0300000","content":"Most credit cards have a general annual interest rate and if you don't know what annual, if you don't know what interest rate is, we can explain that in like a little pop-up thingy. Or maybe a glossary, I don't know how to do this, but they usually have an interest rate between 19.99 and 20, in some extreme cases, 23, 24% per year. Which is insane. Then, yeah, they usually have that kind of interest rate and then if you have a, if you do cash advances where you're using the credit card to withdraw cash, it has an even higher interest rates. And then if you are, yeah, so they have crazy interest rates, the benefits seem so good, but they require you spend money on the card. For example, I recently got an Amex Gold card and, you know, to get the promotion bonus, you got to spend like eight grand over six months."},"1482":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-7-35","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-7","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-7#0403000","content":"And like if you have ways to route those costs, that's fine, but you end up just like spending, spending, spending, and then next thing you know, you're owing them a lot of money. So you have to be very smart about how you plan that. Now, with credit cards, in order to apply for a credit card, you usually can do it at a bank, you do it online. Usually you want to do a referral, if a friend has a credit card that you want, you want to do a referral because you can get some more points or whatever. And once you get the credit card, there's two types of credit cards. I mean, the second one isn't really a credit card, it's more like a, there are two types of card, I guess not two, there's three types of cards that you can use to incur debt. You can do, we should also talk about, leave a note to talk about overdrafts on checking accounts. You can do a traditional credit card, which is where you have a revolving balance, where you have like a credit limit of like $5,000."},"1483":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-7-36","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-7","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-7#0511000","content":"So you can spend up to five grand, and once you spend five grand, you can pay it back and you're good to go as long as you make a minimum payment. You can have a line of credit where it's essentially, it's almost kind of like cash and the interest rate is a lot lower. So usually if you want to, let's say you want to do some home renovations, you might go and get a line of credit, which essentially gives you cash at a lower interest rate, but it's still like debt at the end of the day. Usually you can get that on a card as well. You can then also get what is called a charge card, which is essentially a card that has no limits. Usually American Express is the one to offer these cards, so the American Express gold card, the American Express platinum and the black card, you can spend however. However is relative because they still give you like a spending power thingy, but there's no set limit that you have."},"1484":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-7-37","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-7","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-7#0607000","content":"But the only thing with these cards is that whatever you spend, you have to pay back within 30 days no matter what. And those, yeah, you got to pay back within 30 days. Yeah, so there's different kinds of cards you can get. I would recommend that most people don't get a charge card. And I would say you should only get a line of credit when you absolutely, absolutely need it. And I would actually, actually no, I take that back. I think that if you can, yeah. The nice thing about credit cards is that sometimes some forms of online payments only take credit cards. You can get your first credit card when you turn 18. Again, now we're going to talk about how credit cards affect your credit score, but let's talk about credit score first. What the hell is a credit score? I think we should even have like a section for this. A credit score is some arbitrary number that is used to judge how good you are at borrowing money."},"1485":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-7-38","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-7","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-7#0704000","content":"There's different things that come into play. And then we can go into the details of what makes a credit score a credit score, TransUnion versus Vanguard. We can talk about those things. Let's just do the research and talk about those things. Yeah, so when you get a credit card, usually now today you can apply for a credit card without it. Before you accept a credit card, they didn't do a soft check on your credit, so it's fine. But when you accept it, then it shows up in your credit reports. And ideally you want to be in a position where you're not spending up to 30% of the limit that they give you. So if you have a $10,000 limit, you don't want to spend up to 3 grand on that. You don't want to show you're spending more than 3 grand on that card. If you are, how do you actually pay back the credit card? I would suggest that if you get a credit card,"},"1486":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-7-39","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-7","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-7#0804000","content":"and this is how we're going to go into decisions of how to spend money, I would say that if you don't spend money on a credit card, that you don't have any cash. And I know it's frustrating because it feels like you can leverage and you can just pay back over time, because you enter one purchase and get the next one, the next one, the next thing you know, you have this massive credit card bill that you have to pay. Essentially, you want to try your best to only spend money on a credit card for the sake of getting the points, or if that's the only way for you to pay. The other thing is that when you're doing your credit cards right, what is a minimum payment? A minimum payment is the minimum amount of money you have to pay your credit card per month, no matter what. Usually, this starts off at like $10 a month, depending on the credit card, depending on how much you're owing."},"1487":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-7-40","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-7","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-7#0902000","content":"Also, the other thing with credit cards is that credit cards sometimes have fees. So some cards are free, some are $10 a month, some are $800 a year, $300 a year, depending on the benefits you have. We can list out some different examples. Usually, the higher fee you get, the more \"benefits\" you get, but it's also riskier. You can also get a credit card with a higher limit if your credit score is higher. A good credit score is above 720 or 750, I think, depending on if you're TransUnion or Equifax. Not Vanguard, sorry, TransUnion or Equifax, I believe. So with credit cards, you just want to make sure that you're making minimum payments. And usually, you can see all this stuff. So I'm going to have a worksheet page where you can, or we're going to have a, we're going to, if you, I might do scan QR code because I saw it yesterday. So if you scan this QR code, you can actually go in and like add, just so you can like see it, you can go in and add a credit card that you have, your balance on it, what the minimum payment is and when it's due."},"1488":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-7-41","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-7","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-7#1011000","content":"And you usually want to try and make those, that minimum payment on the day that it's due, preferably. You don't want to like leave it to the last minute. Because if you miss a payment, it shows up in your credit report and it's not good. Again, credit reports really don't matter if you care a lot about borrowing money, if you care, and the primary place where it comes into play is like if you want to buy a house, look at your credit report, blah, blah, blah, blah, blah, blah, blah, blah. Yeah, so that's some credit cards, right? Like they're powerful, but they can also be very, you got to be very careful with them. Another thing. Yeah, so just be careful with credit cards. Now line of credits, you want to use those primarily for like home renovations and stuff like that. You can almost treat it like cash in a way, but again, just got to, again, it's borrowing money, so you got to be careful with that. I'm not going to talk about charge cards in here because if you're reading this book, you probably..."},"1489":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-7-42","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-7","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-7#1109000","content":"I would say the average person probably shouldn't get a charge card. But if you want to, you can. You just got to make sure that you're paying the money back. Yeah, so that's that on credit. Yeah, and also if you have any questions, I'm going to... I think I'm going to... Yeah."},"1490":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-7-43","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-7","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-7#1109000","content":"Return to Raw thoughts."},"1491":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-8","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-8","type":"page","content":"Note 8","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Raw thoughts"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-8"},"1492":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-8-0","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-8","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-8","content":"A tentative idea for reader questions by email and an AI response service."},"1493":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-8-1","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-8","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-8#wording-and-