are not actions taken by this transcript. Financial statements and examples remain the speaker’s unverified working material."},"1538":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9-33","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9#wording-and-source-details-to-check","content":"The savings percentages and the amount each percentage applies to change as the speaker develops the examples. Preserve each figure; do not treat them as one settled calculation or a universal payment rule."},"1539":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9-34","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9#wording-and-source-details-to-check","content":"08:33–09:16: The example balance changes among $500, $300 and $400. The amounts require checking before any worked example is drafted."},"1540":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9-35","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9#wording-and-source-details-to-check","content":"16:29–16:43: The wording is unclear and needs a focused check before use in chapter prose."},"1541":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9-36","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9#wording-and-source-details-to-check","content":"20:29–20:46: The speaker corrects $500 to $250. Both the initial amount and correction are retained."},"1542":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9-37","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9#wording-and-source-details-to-check","content":"The transcript contains “Karna”; the intended brand is uncertain and has not been silently replaced."},"1543":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9-38","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9#wording-and-source-details-to-check","content":"Payment priorities, savings rules, creditor arrangements, borrowing costs and buy-now-pay-later claims are developing source material. They need factual checks and the relevant country/product context before becoming advice."},"1544":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9-39","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9#0000000","content":"OK, so let's talk about what to do when you get paid, right? When you get paid, there's a few different things that you have to worry about. Here's how I would sort of categorize these things, yeah? For me, it's about, at least this is what my mom used to always tell me, it's like, when you get paid, pay yourself first. I think that something that unfortunately is the consequence of the society that we live in, where credit is such a huge, huge, huge thing, we're sort of trained that when you get paid, the first thing to do is that you should go and pay off your debts and your bills and all these different things, and there's nothing, to be honest with you, there's nothing inherently wrong with that. But the issue with that is that what ends up happening is that you don't give yourself the opportunity to be able to build some sort of savings or some sort of buffer for your"},"1545":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9-40","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9#0107160","content":"future or for expenses. You don't get to see any of the money that you're making, which is a huge problem. Because the truth is that, and this, again, some people are not going to like what I'm going to say, but the truth is that all these bills and blah, blah, blah, they're going to be there regardless. The first thing you need to do when you get paid is decide, like, obviously this is a general plan, decide how much of each paycheck, regardless of whatever is happening, except for certain situations, which I'll talk about later, but regardless of whatever is happening, will go towards paying, will go towards some sort of savings or some sort of checking account. Or probably some sort of savings account, right, so a good percentage, if you can afford it, is like 30%, even if it's 5%, like that's fine. But if you can afford to put like 30% or 25%, let's say 25% of whatever money you're getting,"},"1546":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9-41","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9#0203600","content":"just put 25% away, you'll save you and just build that, and the thing is that it's not really about, it's not really about putting away as much as you can, or putting, or wanting to build up some sort of massive savings. It's about a habit that you're developing of putting away money. Because the truth is that if you start doing this when you're not making that much money, it's exponentially easier to do this when you're making a lot more money, and obviously money compounds over time, and in the investments chapter, we'll talk a little bit more about how to actually, now you're putting this money away, where exactly do you need to invest your money? What do you do with that money you're putting away? So first thing you need to do, decide how much you're going to put away for each paycheck, and again, I'm not saying this, I'm not saying that everybody needs to do this, I'm saying"},"1547":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9-42","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9#0302040","content":"that if you have the ability to, do it. If you know that from your entire paycheck, you can pay whatever you're, because the thing is that the truth is that, actually, let's also start with this as well. I think there's a planning phase before we even get to what to do for each individual paycheck. Yeah, there's a planning phase that we need to do before we get to each individual paycheck. You have to figure out, okay, right, you have to look at your life, what exactly are the mandatory bills that you have to pay every single month? Is that your rent? Is that your home bill? Is that your internet bill? What exactly is it? I think that's something that you have to like sit down and just map it all out, and maybe what I'll do is I'll put a, I'll put a work, maybe on the website what I'll do is I'll put a, or on the worksbook or whatever, I'll put like a section where you can write down these bills, yeah, that you have to be able to do, in order for you to be able to"},"1548":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9-43","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9#0401720","content":"survive every single month, or yeah, in order for you to be able to survive every single month, you should, you should, you have to pay, for example, that would include something like rent, that would include something like your car payment, that would include something like your insurance, and your insurance, yes, your insurance, things that you are legally required to pay that if you don't pay them, they have a tangible consequence on your potential quality of life. Netflix is not one of them. Your $90 a month form plan for unlimited data is not one of them, because the truth is that you can just not be a $9 a month plan. So these are the mandatory bills I'm talking about. Things like, for example, like food, right? Your grocery bill, your utilities, these are the bills that you absolutely, no matter what happens every single month, you got to pay them. Some of these are, I think I would also probably, I would also take the liberty of putting things like credit card, minimum payments in here as well, your insurance, student loans, anything"},"1549":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9-44","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9#0506060","content":"that you're, that you're contractually, and there's a massive negative impact if you miss those payments, put those first and then look at it and say, okay, let's say for you, I'm just going to put very, very simple numbers, one of the very, very simple numbers to make this very digestible and easy to understand so that it doesn't feel too overwhelming. But let's say you're making like $5,000 a month, right? $5,000 a month. And again, like this is, when I say making, I'm pretty sure when I say making, I'm talking about money that's like entering your bank now at the end of every single, at the end of, at the end of the month, but this is your post-tax income. So if at the end of, at the end of the month or at the start of the month, you're getting five grand, if at the start of the month you're getting five grand, and let's say your rent"},"1550":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9-45","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9#0604840","content":"is your rent is like $1,500 and all those other bills and everything combined up to like $2,300, right, or $2,500. So that means that after your mandatory bills and expenses before you get to all of your extra subscriptions and stuff, you have $2,500 left, right? You can say, okay, this is great. Now you know exactly how much money you actually are able to spend, now you know exactly how much money you're actually able to spend that doesn't include mandatory, that doesn't include mandatory expenses, yeah? That doesn't include mandatory expenses. Now once you have done this, so you now get your paycheck, you now say okay, you look at that $2,500 a month that you're spending and you look at the $2,500 a month that you"},"1551":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9-46","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9#0702480","content":"have after. Now, you have to decide, the first step in this thing, like I said earlier, is that you have to decide, okay, how much of every single paycheck can I put away no matter what, right? My recommendation, my recommendation to you is that if you can afford to put at least 25 to 30% of your paycheck, but for the sake of an example, at least 25%, if you can afford to put 25 to 30% of your paycheck, including those mandatory expenses away, that's great. If you cannot afford, so in that example, in that example I'm talking about, it would be 25% of $5,000, so that'll be $1,250."},"1552":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9-47","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9#0801280","content":"So that means that you have your mandatory expenses of $2,500 a month and then you now put away another $1,250 a month, if you can afford it, then that leaves you with exactly $1,250 a month to do whatever you want to do with it, right, but you know that you've put money away. That's why you cannot go and do your dinners and blah, blah, blah, blah, blah, blah, blah, all that other extra stuff that we pretend that we all need, right? If you can't afford to do that, let's say like it's just not practical and like your mandatory expenses only leave you with like $500 a month, right? I would just, what I would do is that whatever that balance, whatever that remainder is, so let's say you're working part-time and you're only making two grand a month and after all the mandatory expenses that you have, you only have like $300 a month. What I would recommend that you do is that you just put, again, 25% of whatever is left over, that's what you want to do, 25% of whatever is left over, so you have $400 a month left"},"1553":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9-48","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9#0910920","content":"over, you put $100 away and then you have $300 to do whatever you want to do, right? That's what the best decision that you can make actually is. So I hope, I hope that makes sense now, so you've gotten paid, you've put your percentage, you put your percentage away first, after you've done that, you then pay your bills, you pay all your mandatory expenses, all of your bills, blah, blah, blah, blah, blah. Luckily, most people get paid twice a month, so you're going to have to sit down and figure out how you want to divvy up these payments between throughout the month, how you want to divvy up these payments throughout the month and how you want to orchestrate that. But at the end of the month, you know that whatever happens, you need to put up, you're spending X amount of dollars on mandatory expenses and you're spending, you're putting away at least 25 to 30% of whatever you're, of whatever you can is that number we just"},"1554":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9-49","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9#1003920","content":"talked about. Okay, now again, this is for people who are Christians. The initial step before all of this, and this is what I've done for the longest time, is that as a Christian, again, you guys can debate if you want to debate, that's not the focus of this book. This is, again, like I told you, God has told me to write this book, so this is what I'm putting there. As a Christian, my personal, this is my opinion now, I personally believe that, I personally believe that you should, whatever your salary, your hourly pay is, your salary is, whatever is entering your account at the end of the month, I would just recommend that you just, because things that we have to pay tithes, right?"},"1555":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9-50","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9#1101840","content":"You pay tithes. And the easiest way to deal with that, that I found doing this for many, many, many, many years is that just recalculate like you're making 10% less than what you're actually getting. So, again, sticking to our example, if our take home is five grand a month, again, for my more advanced people, if you want to tithe on your pre-tax income, that's incredible. I've done that as well. It's the heart that counts at the end of the day. So let's say our take home is five grand a month, right? You would, all this math I'm telling you to do, you would do it from the perspective of minus 10% of that. So you would do this from the perspective of someone making $4500 a month, because $500 a month is going straight to your church, or you're using that to pay your tithes, right? And then again, we still do all the math from there, so as it's being transcribed, we should"},"1556":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9-51","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9#1201400","content":"do the math for whatever the case for $4500 is, and I know it seems like a lot, but it's just what's in the Bible, and that is what I believe. If you don't want to pay your tithes, you can go and research and figure out that between you and God, but this is what you're supposed to do. I'm not going to sit down here and argue back and forth with people. So we would do the math from somebody who's making 10%, from pretending as if you're making 10% less. It's just the easiest way to do it. Instead of doing this thing of calculating, hey, I get paid this every paycheck, let me do all my expenses, oh my gosh, I got to take out my tithes, no, you don't actually get paid five grand a month, you actually get paid $4500. If you just switch your mindset to that, doing all these expenses exponentially, it takes a little bit."},"1557":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9-52","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9#1300280","content":"Doing all these expenses gets exponentially easier. And again, I'm not saying this is easy to do, but it's just what you should do, it's what I've done, it's what has helped me in the past. And there's a whole conversation that can be had about, did you pay your tithes, did you not pay your tithes, is there a tangible reward for it? You know, all those different things, that's not the purpose of this book. But if that's something that you do, this is the best and easiest and the most consistent way to do it. This is the best, easiest and most consistent way to do it. And the thing is that if you need to make it more manageable, right? Maybe instead of doing 25% or 30%, you knock it down by 5%. Maybe you're only putting away 20%, right? Like you're only putting away 20%, not the full 25%, not 25%, right? Yeah. So that's what you should do in your paycheck now. So I hope that makes sense. Like you got to take care and the things that, yeah, so that's what you do in how to like distribute the mandatory, like how to bucket your money when you get paid. Now moving into when you have all these bills, right?"},"1558":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9-53","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9#1401600","content":"Let's talk, like the truth is that sometimes you just don't have enough money to pay all of our bills. What happens in that scenario? I'm not going to sit down here and say every single time you should be able to pay. The truth is that sometimes you're just not going to be able to pay. And that's just what it is. This is what it is. So here's how I usually go about giving up these things. I, again, I usually when I don't have enough to cover everything, what I'll end up doing is that I'll look at the things that are absolutely mandatory. Like honestly, because for me, that's like my rent and that is like my credit card minimum payments that I can afford to make. And I'm going to create a priority list of everything that is the super, super, super important to pay. The truth is that I don't have the exact same priority list. So my thing would be to create a party list of the things that you need to 100% pay no"},"1559":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9-54","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9#1504360","content":"matter what is happening. Now when you get your money, if you can't afford to do all of them, what I would do is that you look at that list and I'm also maybe I also add like a worksheet or a page in the whatever web app or app decide to do here, blah, blah, blah of this of this exact same thing as well, where you have to list out your like expenses in terms of like priorities, right? The easiest way to do this stuff is that you pay the things at the top of the list first. That's just being frank and being honest. That's just the easiest way to like, go about handling this stuff. And the truth is that unfortunately, you might not be able to put away your 25% or whatever percent and that's okay. And that's okay. But I would actually know, I would actually know, I would let me say this, I would say"},"1560":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9-55","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9#1603720","content":"that when you're going, I think, do the math to see how much of those things that you can pay that will still allow you to put away some money at the end of the month. And if you can, if you can, it's not a problem, but if you can, that is absolutely amazing. And if you decide not to, that's okay, again, these are just, these are just, this is just like what, it's just that you should pay you to use, but you don't have to read this. But in terms of paying these things, I would make a priority list and then pay them one by one, starting from the top to the bottom. And then go, because, and the reason why I'm saying that you should make this list of what's the most important in terms of the things and the monthly expenses that you have is"},"1561":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9-56","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9#1700480","content":"that you don't want to be in a situation where you go and pay for, for example, like let's say your list contains like things like, okay, you have your, you have your rents, you have your car payment, you got your car insurance, you have your student loan payment and you'll have your phone bill, you'll have your Netflix subscription, your Apple TV subscriptions, your gym membership. Let's say you have your gym membership after your student loans, things like that. But then because you only have like not enough to pay, because you only have, because you look at your bank account and you're like, you're doing the math and you're like, okay, I can use money and pay for my rent, I'm paying for my car rent, I can use money and pay for my what's it called, my gym and my Netflix thing. And then when it all comes to time, you don't have enough money for your rent because rent is later. This is a very stupid thing to do. No. You look at everything month by month. If you can't, if you don't have money for the things that have priority, don't spend"},"1562":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9-57","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9#1800120","content":"money on the things that are lower priority and the things that a lot of times you can shift your payment date if, if that be the case, it's just a function of calling the company and wanting to shift your payment date or canceling it and waiting for later in the month so that your payment date changes. Like those are things that you can do to help yourself. And again, if you don't want to take it, that's okay, but I'm just telling you the reality of how to actually manage these things when like this is, this is about what do you do when you don't have enough money to cover the expenses? What do you need? What do you do? What do you do? Like how do you prioritize bills? Let's like, that's what this section is about. Like how do you prioritize bills? Right. So yeah, I hope that helps you figure that out. Now let's now also talk about, let's not also talk about, okay, so now let's now talk about"},"1563":{"id"