 it, you have to lock in and be absolutely serious with yourself, 'cause there's nothing worse than getting the car and not enjoying driving it because the payments are up, are like, are like, are like fucking with you, you know what I'm saying, and don't, don't say it, don't forbid it, but yeah, and this applies to a bunch of different things as well, right, don't buy a $5,000 camera because of whatever and you're not enjoying it because the payments are so much, so that's just, that's that, I think that's everything for this section."},"1571":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9-66","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/note-9#2604120","content":"Return to Raw thoughts."},"1572":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions","type":"page","content":"Plan decisions","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Raw thoughts"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions"},"1573":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions-0","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions","content":"The author's follow-up decisions about dollars, debt, income groups, and chapter structure."},"1574":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions-1","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions#dollars","content":"Dollars"},"1575":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions-2","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions#debt","content":"Debt"},"1576":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions-3","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions#income-and-debt-maps","content":"Income and debt maps"},"1577":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions-4","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions#the-promise","content":"The promise"},"1578":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions-5","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions#extra-topics","content":"Extra topics"},"1579":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions-6","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions#seeing-the-full-picture-on-paper","content":"Seeing the full picture on paper"},"1580":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions-7","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions#write-what-you-know-for-now","content":"Write what you know for now"},"1581":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions-8","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions#one-next-chapter-for-each-path","content":"One next chapter for each path"},"1582":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions-9","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions#the-book-and-a-companion-workbook","content":"The book and a companion workbook"},"1583":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions-10","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions","content":"Written follow-up from the author on September 27, 2026. This page preserves the direction behind the revised book plan. It is a written note, not an audio transcript."},"1584":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions-11","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions#dollars","content":"> just say a generic dollar amount"},"1585":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions-12","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions#dollars","content":"Use generic $ amounts in examples. This does not select a country's tax or credit rules."},"1586":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions-13","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions#debt","content":"> debt is money that you are owing"},"1587":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions-14","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions#income-and-debt-maps","content":"> Okay, so when this where basically what we want to do is that we want to essentially give people— part of this is going to be giving people a definite and a concrete list of examples for each thing. So when we say income, there is actually, like, a finite amount of different types of income. You either earn hourly, you earn a salary, or you earn dividends. I guess if there's any more, you can also add that as well. Same thing with debt. When we say debt, does that mean good debt? Bad debt? What is good debt? What is bad debt? What are the different types of— what are the actual possible different types of debt you can have, like a finite amounts? Not credit card or line of credit. It means, like, you can have debt from borrowing from a friend. You can have— no, I don't even know. I don't know what the best way to go about that is. And then for the income stuff, you can, like, there's streamers, there's social media, but it still all falls under some generic category. So the goal here is I want to be able to give people some sort of accurate, like— how do I put it— accurate mind map of exactly here, the different options that people have. It's one of these things. The same way in Alex Hormozi's books, he's like, there's only four ways for you to earn customers. It's this, this, this, or this. Everything else, everything falls into these four buckets. That's the kind of goal that we're trying to achieve here."},"1588":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions-15","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions#income-and-debt-maps","content":"Editorial implementation: group income by its source, then show pay methods and concrete examples underneath. Group debts by how the money became owed, then record the terms. These are teaching maps developed for this workbook."},"1589":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions-16","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions#the-promise","content":"> idk yet"},"1590":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions-17","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions#the-promise","content":"The final promise and title remain open."},"1591":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions-18","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions#extra-topics","content":"> I think for now it's not about if we need to make a page or not. Let's just still log it, but then um there could be sections and then we'll explain those things, like as we go. Like, I don't know what the best solution is here actually."},"1592":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions-19","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions#extra-topics","content":"Keep each question in the chapter plan. Place smaller questions inside chapters as sections. Revisit their placement as the writing grows."},"1593":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions-20","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions#seeing-the-full-picture-on-paper","content":"Further direction from the author for Chapter 1:"},"1594":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions-21","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions#seeing-the-full-picture-on-paper","content":"> I think for chapter one, you need to explain why the person has to have to see exactly what the total picture is. It's not to scare them. It's literally just because you can't make a plan if you're not able to see it. You need to actually see it, see it, like look at it. Like you have an amount, how much you owe, look at it. And then let's also to where it also asks them to write exactly how much they have, which I. Just write it on paper so you can actually see it, tangibly see it and get comfortable, like thinking about that number. And then there's also funds in which, like show them a paper, like checking your bank account or something like that, if there's any something we should."},"1595":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions-22","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions#seeing-the-full-picture-on-paper","content":"Editorial implementation: explain why the reader needs visible numbers to plan. Have them check their accounts and records, write what they have and owe, and put the totals beside income on a sheet of paper. Include a worked example of the sheet. Preserve the difference between today's balances and money received over a month."},"1596":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions-23","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions#write-what-you-know-for-now","content":"Further direction from the author for Chapter 1:"},"1597":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions-24","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions#write-what-you-know-for-now","content":"> For chapter one, it's kind of crazy that we're asking people already about interest fees and stuff because we don't. I guess it's not that bad because we can just ask them to not feel worried about if they know it or not. If you know it, write it down. If you don't know it, then it's okay. And if you don't know the next payment, then it's okay. We're gonna review all these things later. It's just to get a full idea of where you're at right now."},"1598":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions-25","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions#write-what-you-know-for-now","content":"Editorial implementation: keep payment dates, interest, fees, and terms in the worksheet but make them optional at this stage. Ask readers to write what they know and leave unknown details blank or mark them “not sure yet.” Point to the later chapters that explain those details. A missing amount stays visible as a gap rather than being treated as zero. Chapter 1 gives the reader a picture of their current position without requiring every answer first."},"1599":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions-26","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions#one-next-chapter-for-each-path","content":"Further direction from the author for Chapter 1:"},"1600":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions-27","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions#one-next-chapter-for-each-path","content":"> Um, we can just get them to pick one here. We don't need to make a plan for both. It's just gonna get people confused. So if you have income and debt, you need to, first of all, I think— actually, I don't know what's the best thing to do here. I think that— gosh, I'm so tired. Okay, so I think that if you have income and debt, then let's go to what to do when you get paid first before we go to make a plan for your debt. Then in the income and no debt, you can go to the life you want. You can go to the life you want. And then for the no income and debt, let's just get them to find a job first, because, like, that's the most important thing, to make a plan for the debt yet. And then for the no income or no debt, we can still just go to the income. Let's go to also find and grow your income as well. That way it gives people a more direct guide on what to go for, on what to go into, on what the next steps are, and then, yeah."},"1601":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions-28","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions#one-next-chapter-for-each-path","content":"Editorial implementation: give each path one next chapter. I-D goes to When you get paid. I-ND goes to The life you want. Both N-D and N-ND go to Find and grow your income. Remove competing next-chapter suggestions from Chapter 1 and use the same routes in the reader-path guide."},"1602":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions-29","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions#the-book-and-a-companion-workbook","content":"Further direction from the author:"},"1603":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions-30","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions#the-book-and-a-companion-workbook","content":"> I think that we need to, for the stuff to fill in, I think that we can also implement some sort of companion workbook as well. So it's two things. It's the book and then the companion workbook, and we can tell people go to this page in the workbook and fill it out there. And if they didn't get the companion workbook, that they can just, like, write it out as well. I feel like that makes sense."},"1604":{"id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions-31","page_id":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/raw-thoughts/plan-decisions#the-book-and-a-companion-workbook","content":"Editorial implementation: keep the book and companion workbook as distinct parts. The book teaches and guides the exercise. The companion holds numbered fill-in pages. Each exercise points to its workbook page and gives plain-paper instructions so the companion stays optional. Start with four pages for Chapter 1. Final printed page references will be set during layout."},"1605":{"id":"/docs/reader-paths","page_id":"/docs/reader-paths","type":"page","content":"Reader paths","breadcrumbs":["Docs"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/reader-paths"},"1606":{"id":"/docs/reader-paths-0","page_id":"/docs/reader-paths","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reader-paths","content":"Four starting situations, with income-source labels and debt details underneath."},"1607":{"id":"/docs/reader-paths-1","page_id":"/docs/reader-paths","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reader-paths#the-four-starting-points","content":"The four starting points"},"1608":{"id":"/docs/reader-paths-2","page_id":"/docs/reader-paths","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reader-paths#what-counts-as-debt","content":"What counts as debt"},"1609":{"id":"/docs/reader-paths-3","page_id":"/docs/reader-paths","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reader-paths#income-source-labels","content":"Income-source labels"},"1610":{"id":"/docs/reader-paths-4","page_id":"/docs/reader-paths","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reader-paths#debt-details","content":"Debt details"},"1611":{"id":"/docs/reader-paths-5","page_id":"/docs/reader-paths","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/reader-paths#symbols","content":"Symbols"},"1612":{"id":"/docs/reader-paths-6","page_id":"/docs/reader-paths","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reader-paths","content":"The four paths follow Note 3. Chapter 1 explains them in the author's voice and guides the first exercise. Readers can use the companion workbook or plain paper."},"1613":{"id":"/docs/reader-paths-7","page_id":"/docs/reader-paths","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reader-paths#the-four-starting-points","content":"ID"},"1614":{"id":"/docs/reader-paths-8","page_id":"/docs/reader-paths","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reader-paths#the-four-starting-points","content":"Starting situation"},"1615":{"id":"/docs/reader-paths-9","page_id":"/docs/reader-paths","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reader-paths#the-four-starting-points","content":"Next chapter after Chapter 1"},"1616":{"id":"/docs/reader-paths-10","page_id":"/docs/reader-paths","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reader-paths#the-four-starting-points","content":"I-D"},"1617":{"id":"/docs/reader-paths-11","page_id":"/docs/reader-paths","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reader-paths#the-four-starting-points","content":"Current income or support, with money owed."},"1618":{"id":"/docs/reader-paths-12","page_id":"/docs/reader-paths","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reader-paths#the-four-starting-points","content":"When you get paid"},"1619":{"id":"/docs/reader-paths-13","page_id":"/docs/reader-paths","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reader-paths#the-four-starting-points","content":"I-ND"},"1620":{"id":"/docs/reader-paths-14","page_id":"/docs/reader-paths","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reader-paths#the-four-starting-points","content":"Current income or support, with no money owed."},"1621":{"id":"/docs/reader-paths-15","page_id":"/docs/reader-paths","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reader-paths#the-four-starting-points","content":"The life you want"},"1622":{"id":"/docs/reader-paths-16","page_id":"/docs/reader-paths","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reader-paths#the-four-starting-points","content":"N-D"},"1623":{"id":"/docs/reader-paths-17","page_id":"/docs/reader-paths","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reader-paths#the-four-starting-points","content":"No current income or support source, with money owed."},"1624":{"id":"/docs/reader-paths-18","page_id":"/docs/reader-paths","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reader-paths#the-four-starting-points","content":"Find and grow your income"},"1625":{"id":"/docs/reader-paths-19","page_id":"/docs/reader-paths","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reader-paths#the-four-starting-points","content":"N-ND"},"1626":{"id":"/docs/reader-paths-20","page_id":"/docs/reader-paths","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reader-paths#the-four-starting-points","content":"No current income or support source, with no money owed."},"1627":{"id":"/docs/reader-paths-21","page_id":"/docs/reader-paths","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reader-paths#the-four-starting-points","content":"Find and grow your income"},"1628":{"id":"/docs/reader-paths-22","page_id":"/docs/reader-paths","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reader-paths#the-four-starting-points","content":"Give each path one next chapter. For I-D, start with incoming money before building a debt plan. Both no-income paths start with finding income. This follows the author's direction on next steps."},"1629":{"id":"/docs/reader-paths-23","page_id":"/docs/reader-paths","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reader-paths#the-four-starting-points","content":"These describe a starting point. They do not measure a person's worth, savings, or whether income covers expenses. A path can change."},"1630":{"id":"/docs/reader-paths-24","page_id":"/docs/reader-paths","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reader-paths#the-four-starting-points","content":"An ongoing source can pay at uneven times. Someone living from savings alone belongs in a no-income path for this workbook, with those savings recorded beside the path. Keep one-time receipts visible without treating them as ongoing income."},"1631":{"id":"/docs/reader-paths-25","page_id":"/docs/reader-paths","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reader-paths#what-counts-as-debt","content":"The author has defined it: **money you owe**. Include amounts owed to people, lenders, providers, and government. A mortgage and money borrowed from a friend both count. A balance does not stop being debt because it is called “good.”"},"1632":{"id":"/docs/reader-paths-26","page_id":"/docs/reader-paths","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reader-paths#what-counts-as-debt","content":"Include balances already owed even when the payment is not late. Mark due dates so routine bills and overdue amounts are distinguishable. Exclude future purchases that have not happened. Record disputed or uncertain obligations separately for checking."},"1633":{"id":"/docs/reader-paths-27","page_id":"/docs/reader-paths","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reader-paths#what-counts-as-debt","content":"The goal of income without debt remains part of the author's direction. A definition alone does not settle the order or speed of repayment; those steps belong in Chapter 5."},"1634":{"id":"/docs/reader-paths-28","page_id":"/docs/reader-paths","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reader-paths#income-source-labels","content":"Use descriptive labels instead of forcing every reader into hourly, salary, or business."},"1635":{"id":"/docs/reader-paths-29","page_id":"/docs/reader-paths","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reader-paths#income-source-labels","content":"Label"},"1636":{"id":"/docs/reader-paths-30","page_id":"/docs/reader-paths","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reader-paths#income-source-labels","content":"Examples on the workbook map"},"1637":{"id":"/docs/reader-paths-31","page_id":"/docs/reader-paths","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reader-paths#income-source-labels","content":"Employer"},"1638":{"id":"/docs/reader-paths-32","page_id":"/docs/reader-paths","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reader-paths#income-source-labels","content":"Hourly pay, salary, tips, commissions, bonuses from employment"},"1639":{"id":"/docs/reader-paths-33","page_id":"/docs/reader-paths","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reader-paths#income-source-labels","content":"Own work"},"1640":{"id":"/docs/reader-paths-34","page_id":"/docs/reader-paths","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reader-paths#income-source-labels","content":"Freelance work, a service, products, a business or creator channel the reader runs"},"1641":{"id":"/docs/reader-paths-35","page_id":"/docs/reader-paths","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reader-paths#income-source-labels","content":"Assets"},"1642":{"id":"/docs/reader-paths-36","page_id":"/docs/reader-paths","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reader-paths#income-source-labels","content":"Interest, dividends, rent, royalties"},"1643":{"id":"/docs/reader-paths-37","page_id":"/docs/reader-paths","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reader-paths#income-source-labels","content":"Support / retirement"},"1644":{"id":"/docs/reader-paths-38","page_id":"/docs/reader-paths","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reader-paths#income-source-labels","content":"Pensions, cash benefits, regular family support, child support"},"1645":{"id":"/docs/reader-paths-39","page_id":"/docs/reader-paths","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reader-paths#income-source-labels","content":"Hourly and salary are pay methods within Employer. Readers can use multiple labels. A later passage could say **I-D · Employer + Own work**. Use **I-D** alone when the source does not change the step. Use **All readers** for shared material."},"1646":{"id":"/docs/reader-paths-40","page_id":"/docs/reader-paths","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reader-paths#income-source-labels","content":"Sort each payment once. Keep timing, reliability, business costs, and tax still to cover beside it. Borrowing, savings withdrawals, sales, and one-time gifts have a separate cash-receipts line in Chapter 1."},"1647":{"id":"/docs/reader-paths-41","page_id":"/docs/reader-paths","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reader-paths#debt-details","content":"The chapter's three groups describe how the amount arose: borrowing or credit, unpaid bills, or taxes, fines, and court-ordered amounts. Then record who is owed, the balance, payment dates, interest and fees, any property pledged, and the purpose."},"1648":{"id":"/docs/reader-paths-42","page_id":"/docs/reader-paths","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reader-paths#debt-details","content":"“Secured,” “unsecured,” “instalment,” and “revolving” describe terms to explain in Chapter 5. “Good” and “bad” describe a judgment to examine. Neither replaces the simple debt yes/no question."},"1649":{"id":"/docs/reader-paths-43","page_id":"/docs/reader-paths","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reader-paths#symbols","content":"The original triangle and square ideas remain available. Text labels keep the paths clear while the visual symbols are undecided."},"1650":{"id":"/docs/reader-paths-44","page_id":"/docs/reader-paths","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reader-paths#symbols","content":"Sources: Note 3, Plan decisions, and the income and debt research."},"1651":{"id":"/docs/research","page_id":"/docs/research","type":"page","content":"Research notes","breadcrumbs":["Docs"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/research"},"1652":{"id":"/docs/research-0","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research","content":"Decisions made, sources checked, and factual work still needed for the chapters."},"1653":{"id":"/docs/research-1","page_id":"/docs/research","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/research#decisions-made","content":"Decisions made"},"1654":{"id":"/docs/research-2","page_id":"/docs/research","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/research#income-map","content":"Income map"},"1655":{"id":"/docs/research-3","page_id":"/docs/research","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/research#boundaries-used-in-the-draft","content":"Boundaries used in the draft"},"1656":{"id":"/docs/research-4","page_id":"/docs/research","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/research#debt-map","content":"Debt map"},"1657":{"id":"/docs/research-5","page_id":"/docs/research","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/research#checking-the-balances","content":"Checking the balances"},"1658":{"id":"/docs/research-6","page_id":"/docs/research","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/research#claims-to-develop-and-check","content":"Claims to develop and check"},"1659":{"id":"/docs/research-7","page_id":"/docs/research","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/research#chapter-2-costing-a-chosen-life","content":"Chapter 2: Costing a chosen life"},"1660":{"id":"/docs/research-8","page_id":"/docs/research","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/research#michelles-rebuilt-example","content":"Michelle's rebuilt example"},"1661":{"id":"/docs/research-9","page_id":"/docs/research","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/research#chapter-3-choosing-an-income-step","content":"Chapter 3: Choosing an income step"},"1662":{"id":"/docs/research-10","page_id":"/docs/research","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/research#what-the-sources-support","content":"What the sources support"},"1663":{"id":"/docs/research-11","page_id":"/docs/research","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/research#author-material-and-editorial-choices","content":"Author material and editorial choices"},"1664":{"id":"/docs/research-12","page_id":"/docs/research","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/research#example-and-exercise-checks","content":"Example and exercise checks"},"1665":{"id":"/docs/research-13","page_id":"/docs/research","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/research#chapter-4-planning-a-payday","content":"Chapter 4: Planning a payday"},"1666":{"id":"/docs/research-14","page_id":"/docs/research","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/research#recording-and-arithmetic-decisions","content":"Recording and arithmetic decisions"},"1667":{"id":"/docs/research-15","page_id":"/docs/research","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/research#chapter-5-making-a-debt-plan","content":"Chapter 5: Making a debt plan"},"1668":{"id":"/docs/research-16","page_id":"/docs/research","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/research#chapter-6-reading-and-paying-a-card-bill","content":"Chapter 6: Reading and paying a card bill"},"1669":{"id":"/docs/research-17","page_id":"/docs/research","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/research#chapter-7-reports-and-scores","content":"Chapter 7: Reports and scores"},"1670":{"id":"/docs/research-18","page_id":"/docs/research","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/research#chapter-8-checking-a-purchase","content":"Chapter 8: Checking a purchase"},"1671":{"id":"/docs/research-19","page_id":"/docs/research","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/research#recording-research-queue","content":"Recording research queue"},"1672":{"id":"/docs/research-20","page_id":"/docs/research","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/research#stories-and-examples","content":"Stories and examples"},"1673":{"id":"/docs/research-21","page_id":"/docs/research","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/research#decisions-still-open","content":"Decisions still open"},"1674":{"id":"/docs/research-22","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research","content":"Keep the author's experience and point of view visible. Check the claims that go beyond that experience. A chapter placeholder is not finished guidance."},"1675":{"id":"/docs/research-23","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#decisions-made","content":"The author answered these in Plan decisions:"},"1676":{"id":"/docs/research-24","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#decisions-made","content":"**Dollar amounts:** use generic $ amounts in examples. There is no need to choose between USD and CAD for a made-up calculation. Real source amounts keep their context."},"1677":{"id":"/docs/research-25","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#decisions-made","content":"**Debt:** money the reader owes. This includes amounts people might call good debt."},"1678":{"id":"/docs/research-26","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#decisions-made","content":"**Income and debt categories:** give a compact map, concrete examples, and a way to handle several sources. Do not use an occupation as an income type."},"1679":{"id":"/docs/research-27","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#decisions-made","content":"**Extra topics:** log each question and place it within a chapter as a section. It does not need a page of its own."},"1680":{"id":"/docs/research-28","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#decisions-made","content":"**Promise and title:** leave them open."},"1681":{"id":"/docs/research-29","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#decisions-made","content":"The book plan assigns all current topics to 12 chapters. Chapter 1 is the first full draft."},"1682":{"id":"/docs/research-30","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#income-map","content":"Checked September 27, 2026. The four groups are an editorial teaching map: Employer, Own work, Assets, and Support / retirement. They regroup common sources found in the official references below; no source establishes exactly these four as a universal taxonomy."},"1683":{"id":"/docs/research-31","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#income-map","content":"Source"},"1684":{"id":"/docs/research-32","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#income-map","content":"What it supports"},"1685":{"id":"/docs/research-33","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#income-map","content":"Scope"},"1686":{"id":"/docs/research-34","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#income-map","content":"BLS Consumer Expenditure glossary, “Income”"},"1687":{"id":"/docs/research-35","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#income-map","content":"Separates employment, net self-employment, property receipts, retirement, benefits, and regular support. Net business income accounts for operating costs."},"1688":{"id":"/docs/research-36","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#income-map","content":"US statistical definitions. The workbook regroups them; it does not use this as a tax guide."},"1689":{"id":"/docs/research-37","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#income-map","content":"Census overview of income sources, April 2, 2025"},"1690":{"id":"/docs/research-38","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#income-map","content":"Shows earnings, asset income, social insurance, government transfers, and other income. Supports looking beyond a job title."},"1691":{"id":"/docs/research-39","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#income-map","content":"Five groups in that research tool, not the workbook's four. No population statistics are copied into the chapter."},"1692":{"id":"/docs/research-40","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#income-map","content":"IRS income overview"},"1693":{"id":"/docs/research-41","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#income-map","content":"Includes royalties, freelance work, and other receipts; its groupings differ from this workbook."},"1694":{"id":"/docs/research-42","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#income-map","content":"Used to check coverage only. US tax treatment is not applied to all readers."},"1695":{"id":"/docs/research-43","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#income-map","content":"CFPB financial glossary"},"1696":{"id":"/docs/research-44","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#income-map","content":"Definitions of dividends and take-home pay."},"1697":{"id":"/docs/research-45","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#income-map","content":"General concepts from a US consumer-education source."},"1698":{"id":"/docs/research-46","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#income-map","content":"Investor.gov: Capital gain"},"1699":{"id":"/docs/research-47","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#income-map","content":"A realized gain concerns profit on a sale, not the whole cash receipt."},"1700":{"id":"/docs/research-48","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#income-map","content":"The $800 / $1,000 example is invented arithmetic, excludes fees and tax, and promises no return."},"1701":{"id":"/docs/research-49","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#boundaries-used-in-the-draft","content":"Income source, payment method, and timing answer different questions. Hourly and salary describe pay methods. A streamer may receive several kinds of payment. Chapter 1 sorts the payment and counts it once."},"1702":{"id":"/docs/research-50","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#boundaries-used-in-the-draft","content":"Borrowed money and transfers from the reader's own savings are not new earnings. One-time gifts and sale receipts are tracked separately so they are not assumed to repeat. Gain on a sale stays with that sale record to avoid double counting. A business's sales and its payment to its owner must not both be counted as separate personal income."},"1703":{"id":"/docs/research-51","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#boundaries-used-in-the-draft","content":"The cash worksheet records costs or tax still to cover. Noncash support is noted separately. The source groups are for planning; tax definitions and reporting rules need the relevant country when those subjects are drafted."},"1704":{"id":"/docs/research-52","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#debt-map","content":"Checked September 27, 2026. The author defines debt as money owed. The draft sorts it by how the amount arose: borrowing or credit; unpaid bills; and taxes, fines, and court-ordered amounts. Creditor, purpose, security, and repayment terms remain separate details."},"1705":{"id":"/docs/research-53","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#debt-map","content":"Source"},"1706":{"id":"/docs/research-54","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#debt-map","content":"What it supports"},"1707":{"id":"/docs/research-55","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#debt-map","content":"Scope"},"1708":{"id":"/docs/research-56","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#debt-map","content":"CFPB financial glossary"},"1709":{"id":"/docs/research-57","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#debt-map","content":"Debt is money owed; collateral, loans, and secured/unsecured lending describe distinct features."},"1710":{"id":"/docs/research-58","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#debt-map","content":"Definitions only. No universal collection procedure or legal outcome is asserted."},"1711":{"id":"/docs/research-59","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#debt-map","content":"CFPB debt worksheet, pages 1–2"},"1712":{"id":"/docs/research-60","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#debt-map","content":"Includes friends/family, bills, taxes, fines, loans, and support obligations. Records creditor, balance, payment, rate, and terms."},"1713":{"id":"/docs/research-61","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#debt-map","content":"Supports coverage of the inventory. The three-group arrangement and the book's worksheet are editorial choices."},"1714":{"id":"/docs/research-62","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#debt-map","content":"CFPB Consumer voices on financial rules to live by, March 2016, section 3.3"},"1715":{"id":"/docs/research-63","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#debt-map","content":"Participants disagreed on which debts were good or bad."},"1716":{"id":"/docs/research-64","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#debt-map","content":"Qualitative focus groups. This is not proof that a particular debt is safe or harmful."},"1717":{"id":"/docs/research-65","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#debt-map","content":"The workbook includes amounts already owed even if not overdue. It separates future spending plans, disputed amounts, and business obligations that may not be the reader's own. Repayment priority still needs researched guidance and concrete examples."},"1718":{"id":"/docs/research-66","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#checking-the-balances","content":"The author asked readers to see their numbers on paper so they have a starting point for a plan. Chapter 1 now includes current money, income for a stated month, and total money owed. A made-up sheet shows the three totals without adding balances and income together. Other assets stay on a separate list with dates and estimates marked."},"1719":{"id":"/docs/research-67","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#checking-the-balances","content":"Checked September 27, 2026: CFPB's overdraft guidance, updated June 4, 2025, explains that transactions may not update balances at once. The worksheet therefore records the displayed balance labels, dates, and pending items. The CFPB glossary supports the distinction between owned funds and credit limits or overdrafts. No US-specific fees or account rights are generalized to every reader."},"1720":{"id":"/docs/research-68","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#checking-the-balances","content":"The sheet is a record of balances, not a calculation of spendable money or net worth. It uses one current balance per account, keeps available figures as notes, and records negative account balances as debts once. An old statement stays marked for checking; incomplete current totals are labelled partial. Income and support have their own subtotal, separate from other cash receipts. The reason for writing and looking at the figures comes from the author's follow-up direction; no psychological outcome is claimed."},"1721":{"id":"/docs/research-69","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#claims-to-develop-and-check","content":"Chapter"},"1722":{"id":"/docs/research-70","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#claims-to-develop-and-check","content":"What still needs evidence or a clearer scope"},"1723":{"id":"/docs/research-71","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#claims-to-develop-and-check","content":"2 · The life you want"},"1724":{"id":"/docs/research-72","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#claims-to-develop-and-check","content":"A complete cost example, taxes, saving, debt payments, and the mindset discussion."},"1725":{"id":"/docs/research-73","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#claims-to-develop-and-check","content":"3 · Find and grow your income"},"1726":{"id":"/docs/research-74","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#claims-to-develop-and-check","content":"Concrete options by starting point and the costs, constraints, and tradeoffs of each."},"1727":{"id":"/docs/research-75","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#claims-to-develop-and-check","content":"4 · When you get paid"},"1728":{"id":"/docs/research-76","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#claims-to-develop-and-check","content":"Payment priorities, variable income, bank comparisons, and the specific criticism of budgeting apps."},"1729":{"id":"/docs/research-77","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#claims-to-develop-and-check","content":"5 · Make a plan for debt"},"1730":{"id":"/docs/research-78","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#claims-to-develop-and-check","content":"Repayment approaches, collections, tax bills, lending to friends, and terms of different borrowing."},"1731":{"id":"/docs/research-79","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#claims-to-develop-and-check","content":"6 · Credit cards"},"1732":{"id":"/docs/research-80","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#claims-to-develop-and-check","content":"Current agreements, interest calculations, minimum payments, fees, and statement examples."},"1733":{"id":"/docs/research-81","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#claims-to-develop-and-check","content":"7 · Credit scores"},"1734":{"id":"/docs/research-82","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#claims-to-develop-and-check","content":"Each system's country, history, factors, and uses."},"1735":{"id":"/docs/research-83","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#claims-to-develop-and-check","content":"8 · Before you buy"},"1736":{"id":"/docs/research-84","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#claims-to-develop-and-check","content":"Full-cost comparisons and current BNPL payment, fee, and reporting terms."},"1737":{"id":"/docs/research-85","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#claims-to-develop-and-check","content":"9 · When life changes"},"1738":{"id":"/docs/research-86","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#claims-to-develop-and-check","content":"Reserves, insurance, and actions around a loss of income or a major life change."},"1739":{"id":"/docs/research-87","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#claims-to-develop-and-check","content":"10 · Fast money"},"1740":{"id":"/docs/research-88","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#claims-to-develop-and-check","content":"Specific offers, documented claims, costs, and outcomes."},"1741":{"id":"/docs/research-89","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#claims-to-develop-and-check","content":"11–12 · Investing"},"1742":{"id":"/docs/research-90","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#claims-to-develop-and-check","content":"Risks, costs, liquidity, mechanisms, and worked-example assumptions."},"1743":{"id":"/docs/research-91","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-2-costing-a-chosen-life","content":"Checked October 1, 2026 for the first draft of Chapter 2, its spoken adaptation, and workbook pages 5–7. The desired-life exercise and voice come from Notes 3–4. The household worksheet is an editorial arrangement, not a method claimed to be prescribed by an agency."},"1744":{"id":"/docs/research-92","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-2-costing-a-chosen-life","content":"Primary source"},"1745":{"id":"/docs/research-93","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-2-costing-a-chosen-life","content":"Supports"},"1746":{"id":"/docs/research-94","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-2-costing-a-chosen-life","content":"Limits"},"1747":{"id":"/docs/research-95","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-2-costing-a-chosen-life","content":"CFPB: Assess your spending, page modified December 12, 2024"},"1748":{"id":"/docs/research-96","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-2-costing-a-chosen-life","content":"Checking records, less frequent costs, saving contributions, and comparison with take-home pay."},"1749":{"id":"/docs/research-97","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-2-costing-a-chosen-life","content":"US home-buying context; used here only for general cost coverage. No mortgage rules or behavioral statistics are adopted."},"1750":{"id":"/docs/research-98","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-2-costing-a-chosen-life","content":"Consumer.gov: Making a Budget, August 2024"},"1751":{"id":"/docs/research-99","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-2-costing-a-chosen-life","content":"Listing income and expenses, comparing monthly amounts, and revisiting a plan."},"1752":{"id":"/docs/research-100","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-2-costing-a-chosen-life","content":"The chapter adapts the comparison to a desired-life estimate. Its result does not establish affordability on each payment date."},"1753":{"id":"/docs/research-101","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-2-costing-a-chosen-life","content":"CFPB financial glossary, “Gross income” and “Net income”"},"1754":{"id":"/docs/research-102","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-2-costing-a-chosen-life","content":"The distinction between pay before deductions and take-home pay after deductions."},"1755":{"id":"/docs/research-103","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-2-costing-a-chosen-life","content":"No fixed tax rate or gross-salary conversion is supplied. Costs or tax still to cover are deducted once or marked unknown."},"1756":{"id":"/docs/research-104","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-2-costing-a-chosen-life","content":"CFPB savings-plan tool, pages 1–2"},"1757":{"id":"/docs/research-105","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-2-costing-a-chosen-life","content":"Dividing a savings amount by the time available."},"1758":{"id":"/docs/research-106","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-2-costing-a-chosen-life","content":"The book adapts weeks to months and first subtracts money already assigned to that goal. It assumes no returns; already-funded goals have zero still needed."},"1759":{"id":"/docs/research-107","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-2-costing-a-chosen-life","content":"CFPB goal-setting tool, pages 1–3"},"1760":{"id":"/docs/research-108","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-2-costing-a-chosen-life","content":"Connecting a goal to priorities, an amount, and a date."},"1761":{"id":"/docs/research-109","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-2-costing-a-chosen-life","content":"Used as planning support, not evidence that writing a goal guarantees an outcome."},"1762":{"id":"/docs/research-110","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#michelles-rebuilt-example","content":"All amounts are explicitly fictional generic-dollar assumptions. The chapter retains the source's desired work, family time, and chosen comforts, then settles a customer-service role for the example. It does not assert an actual school, product price, household, or outcome."},"1763":{"id":"/docs/research-111","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#michelles-rebuilt-example","content":"Monthly rows total $8,400: $5,900 costs/payments plus $2,500 saving."},"1764":{"id":"/docs/research-112","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#michelles-rebuilt-example","content":"The assumed partner contribution is $2,400, leaving a $6,000 personal target. Michelle's assumed current take-home pay is $3,000, so the gap is $3,000. Equivalently, $8,400 minus $5,400 current household income is $3,000."},"1765":{"id":"/docs/research-113","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#michelles-rebuilt-example","content":"The $6,000 moving goal less $1,200 assigned savings leaves $4,800. Over 24 months that is $200 per month, already included in $2,500 saving. The remaining $2,300 is for other saving goals, including a cash reserve."},"1766":{"id":"/docs/research-114","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#michelles-rebuilt-example","content":"The car payment appears inside transport only. Other debt payments exclude it. The example assumes no further up-front purchases and no additional costs or tax still to cover in the take-home figures."},"1767":{"id":"/docs/research-115","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#michelles-rebuilt-example","content":"The $200 monthly provision for less frequent costs represents $2,400 per year. Actual due dates remain separate."},"1768":{"id":"/docs/research-116","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#michelles-rebuilt-example","content":"The raw $6,500-before-tax/$600 target and $20/$200 weekly cleaning ambiguity are not resolved. The draft avoids treating them as settled facts: it chooses an $800 monthly cleaning allowance without asserting frequency and uses a new, fully labeled household calculation. Local costs, taxes, and future prices need new estimates for a real reader. The script uses the same figures and refers listeners to the full table."},"1769":{"id":"/docs/research-117","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#michelles-rebuilt-example","content":"Chapter 3 develops the earning routes and adviser story. It replaces the uncertain sales/app figures with a separate, explicitly fictional editing calculation. Existing Chapter 1 print proofs do not include Chapters 2–8 or workbook pages 5–18; their layout remains future work."},"1770":{"id":"/docs/research-118","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-3-choosing-an-income-step","content":"Checked October 1, 2026 for Chapter 3, its spoken adaptation, and workbook pages 8 and 9."},"1771":{"id":"/docs/research-119","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#what-the-sources-support","content":"Source and scope"},"1772":{"id":"/docs/research-120","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#what-the-sources-support","content":"Use in this draft"},"1773":{"id":"/docs/research-121","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#what-the-sources-support","content":"Limit"},"1774":{"id":"/docs/research-122","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#what-the-sources-support","content":"CareerOneStop: Negotiate your salary, sponsored by the US Department of Labor"},"1775":{"id":"/docs/research-123","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#what-the-sources-support","content":"Check comparable local pay; support a request with work and skills; consider schedule, travel, and benefits."},"1776":{"id":"/docs/research-124","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#what-the-sources-support","content":"The sample conversation is editorial, not an author event. No right to a raise, salary estimate, or hiring result is asserted."},"1777":{"id":"/docs/research-125","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#what-the-sources-support","content":"US Small Business Administration: Plan your business"},"1778":{"id":"/docs/research-126","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#what-the-sources-support","content":"Ask possible customers about demand and pricing."},"1779":{"id":"/docs/research-127","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#what-the-sources-support","content":"The small editing test is an editorial exercise. The source does not establish demand or earnings for Michelle."},"1780":{"id":"/docs/research-128","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#what-the-sources-support","content":"IRS: Manage taxes for your gig work, US federal scope"},"1781":{"id":"/docs/research-129","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#what-the-sources-support","content":"Keep income/expense records and check tax payments."},"1782":{"id":"/docs/research-130","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#what-the-sources-support","content":"No tax percentage, deductible-cost ruling, or universal worker classification is given. Generic dollars do not make US rules apply everywhere."},"1783":{"id":"/docs/research-131","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#what-the-sources-support","content":"USAGov: Find government benefits and financial help, US scope"},"1784":{"id":"/docs/research-132","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#what-the-sources-support","content":"A place to find potential programs and application information."},"1785":{"id":"/docs/research-133","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#what-the-sources-support","content":"No reader is assumed eligible, approved, or certain to receive a payment."},"1786":{"id":"/docs/research-134","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#what-the-sources-support","content":"CFPB: Creating a cash-flow budget, US consumer education"},"1787":{"id":"/docs/research-135","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#what-the-sources-support","content":"Record receipts and expenses by week and carry forward balances."},"1788":{"id":"/docs/research-136","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#what-the-sources-support","content":"Chapter 3 collects dates; Chapter 4 owns the full payment plan. The workbook distinguishes cash, noncash help, and uncertain receipts."},"1789":{"id":"/docs/research-137","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#author-material-and-editorial-choices","content":"Note 3 supplies the goal: income serves the chosen life. Chapter 3 does not require every reader with existing income to increase it."},"1790":{"id":"/docs/research-138","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#author-material-and-editorial-choices","content":"Notes 5–6 supply the direct push to seek work and consider temporary roles. The student and stay-at-home-parent qualifications stay beside the advice. The chapter adds practical checks for health, access, care costs, and permission to work; these are editorial guidance, not new personal stories."},"1791":{"id":"/docs/research-139","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#author-material-and-editorial-choices","content":"Note 4 at 04:11–08:22 supports the Starbucks conversation, vehicle analogy, and founder-versus-product reflection. The chapter retells it without a direct adviser quote, uncertain company name, or claim that a career change has already succeeded."},"1792":{"id":"/docs/research-140","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#author-material-and-editorial-choices","content":"Note 4 at 15:02–20:03 supplies the idea of exploring writing/business routes. Audience-growth guarantees, speculative conversions, guessed tax, and inconsistent subscription calculations are excluded. They remain raw source material."},"1793":{"id":"/docs/research-141","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#author-material-and-editorial-choices","content":"No claim is made that a temporary job, pay request, business test, or benefits check will produce income. Actual pay, availability, terms, and eligibility require current local information."},"1794":{"id":"/docs/research-142","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#example-and-exercise-checks","content":"Michelle remains fictional. Her Chapter 2 household target is $8,400; the partner contribution is $2,400; her personal target is $6,000. Current take-home pay is $3,000, leaving a $3,000 monthly gap. Her editing test uses two hours, no spending, a Friday action deadline, and a Sunday review. It has no assumed earnings."},"1795":{"id":"/docs/research-143","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#example-and-exercise-checks","content":"The separate fictional paid editing batch has four jobs at $150 = $600 received. Assumed total business costs of $120 leave $480 before tax. Eight delivery hours plus four customer-finding/admin hours = 12 hours; $480 / 12 = $40 before tax per recorded hour. All customers are assumed to have paid within the reviewed period. These are not market prices, employee wages, Michelle's results, or a forecast of repeating income. With tax unknown, the $480 cannot be treated as take-home money toward her gap."},"1796":{"id":"/docs/research-144","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#example-and-exercise-checks","content":"Pages 8–9 mirror the chapter's five steps: starting point, limits, options, action, and review. They keep possible income separate from receipts, include payment dates and remaining costs/tax, and work on plain paper. All four reader paths point next to Chapter 4, while no-income readers retain their immediate income/support action and shortfall needs."},"1797":{"id":"/docs/research-145","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-4-planning-a-payday","content":"Checked October 1, 2026 for Chapter 4, its spoken adaptation, and workbook pages 10–12."},"1798":{"id":"/docs/research-146","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-4-planning-a-payday","content":"Primary source"},"1799":{"id":"/docs/research-147","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-4-planning-a-payday","content":"Supported use and scope"},"1800":{"id":"/docs/research-148","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-4-planning-a-payday","content":"CFPB: Adjusting your cash flow"},"1801":{"id":"/docs/research-149","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-4-planning-a-payday","content":"US educational guidance on a dated calendar and contacting creditors about payment timing. The draft requires confirmation; it does not promise a change or repeat the recording's cancel/restart suggestion."},"1802":{"id":"/docs/research-150","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-4-planning-a-payday","content":"CFPB: Creating a cash-flow budget"},"1803":{"id":"/docs/research-151","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-4-planning-a-payday","content":"US educational method of adding receipts, subtracting uses, and carrying balances between periods. The workbook distinguishes reservations from actual bank transactions to avoid duplicate counting."},"1804":{"id":"/docs/research-152","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-4-planning-a-payday","content":"CFPB: Emergency fund guide"},"1805":{"id":"/docs/research-153","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-4-planning-a-payday","content":"Savings amounts depend on circumstances. Watch balances when automating transfers. No source-endorsed 25% rule is claimed."},"1806":{"id":"/docs/research-154","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-4-planning-a-payday","content":"CFPB: Prioritizing bills"},"1807":{"id":"/docs/research-155","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-4-planning-a-payday","content":"Weigh consequences and contact providers when money cannot cover obligations. The chapter gives no universal payment hierarchy or jurisdiction-specific consequence timetable."},"1808":{"id":"/docs/research-156","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-4-planning-a-payday","content":"IRS: Manage taxes for your gig work"},"1809":{"id":"/docs/research-157","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-4-planning-a-payday","content":"US independent contractors may need estimated tax payments. No guessed tax rate is used."},"1810":{"id":"/docs/research-158","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-4-planning-a-payday","content":"CFPB: Monthly account fees"},"1811":{"id":"/docs/research-159","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-4-planning-a-payday","content":"US account fees and waiver conditions vary; compare actual terms."},"1812":{"id":"/docs/research-160","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-4-planning-a-payday","content":"FDIC: Is my money insured?"},"1813":{"id":"/docs/research-161","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-4-planning-a-payday","content":"Pass-through coverage through nonbank arrangements has requirements. The indexed official text was available; the page fetch was blocked. No specific app's coverage or insurance limit is asserted."},"1814":{"id":"/docs/research-162","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#recording-and-arithmetic-decisions","content":"Note 9's correction at 03:02 puts bill planning before choosing a saving percentage. The draft preserves the mother's advice, habit-building aim, conditional 25–30% suggestion, Christian tithing practice, and admission of shortfalls. It excludes unclear speech near 16:29, assumed universal pay frequency, guessed phone-plan prices, and claims about guaranteed returns from giving. No biblical text is quoted or interpreted."},"1815":{"id":"/docs/research-163","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#recording-and-arithmetic-decisions","content":"All examples use fictional generic dollars and are separate from Michelle:"},"1816":{"id":"/docs/research-164","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#recording-and-arithmetic-decisions","content":"Main month: $5,000 take-home − $2,500 required costs − $1,250 saving = $1,250 left to allocate. Saving is 25% of $5,000. Required costs include later known-bill provisions; no additional tax, business costs, or giving are assumed."},"1817":{"id":"/docs/research-165","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#recording-and-arithmetic-decisions","content":"Small surplus: $2,000 − $1,600 = $400. Saving 25% of $400 = $100, leaving $300. This is 5% of initial take-home, not 25%. The source's changing $500/$300/$400 remainder is not treated as one settled figure."},"1818":{"id":"/docs/research-166","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#recording-and-arithmetic-decisions","content":"Separate giving case: $5,000 − $500 giving − $2,500 required costs − $1,125 saving = $875. Giving is 10% of take-home; saving is 25% of the $4,500 after giving. This completion is editorial, not a quoted source calculation or prescribed split."},"1819":{"id":"/docs/research-167","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#recording-and-arithmetic-decisions","content":"Timing: first $2,500 receipt − $2,000 costs = $500 carried. Add the second $2,500; subtract $500 required costs, $1,250 saving, and $1,250 allocated elsewhere = $0 unassigned. Attempting $625 saving in the first period leaves a $125 gap. The illustration assumes the next month's receipt precedes the next month's costs; readers must check their own dates."},"1820":{"id":"/docs/research-168","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#recording-and-arithmetic-decisions","content":"Shortfall: $1,500 needs/required payments − $1,200 available = $300 uncovered. Saving, giving, optional purchases, expected assistance, and unconfirmed arrangements do not silently close it."},"1821":{"id":"/docs/research-169","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#recording-and-arithmetic-decisions","content":"Pages 10–12 record the allocation, date-by-date plan, shortfall consequences and contacts, and payday review. Projected receipts stay distinct from cash available now. Transfers and reservations must not be counted twice. Actual consequences, assistance, due-date changes, fees, tax, and coverage remain dependent on country and provider."},"1822":{"id":"/docs/research-170","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-5-making-a-debt-plan","content":"Checked October 1, 2026 for Chapter 5, its spoken adaptation, and workbook pages 13–14."},"1823":{"id":"/docs/research-171","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-5-making-a-debt-plan","content":"Primary source"},"1824":{"id":"/docs/research-172","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-5-making-a-debt-plan","content":"Supported use and scope"},"1825":{"id":"/docs/research-173","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-5-making-a-debt-plan","content":"FCAC debt guidance"},"1826":{"id":"/docs/research-174","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-5-making-a-debt-plan","content":"Canadian explanation of debts, interest, fees, and records."},"1827":{"id":"/docs/research-175","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-5-making-a-debt-plan","content":"FCAC personal loans, lines of credit, overdraft protection"},"1828":{"id":"/docs/research-176","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-5-making-a-debt-plan","content":"Canadian product structures and terms, collateral, rate changes, and linked funds. No universally cheaper product is claimed."},"1829":{"id":"/docs/research-177","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-5-making-a-debt-plan","content":"CFPB bill priorities, repayment methods"},"1830":{"id":"/docs/research-178","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-5-making-a-debt-plan","content":"US education distinguishing immediate consequences from extra-payment strategies. The second is an archived educational article, not a current product rule."},"1831":{"id":"/docs/research-179","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-5-making-a-debt-plan","content":"IRS tax-debt help, Topic 202"},"1832":{"id":"/docs/research-180","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-5-making-a-debt-plan","content":"US federal filing/payment options and continuing charges; eligibility remains individual."},"1833":{"id":"/docs/research-181","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-5-making-a-debt-plan","content":"CFPB collector checks, validation information"},"1834":{"id":"/docs/research-182","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-5-making-a-debt-plan","content":"US verification and covered-collector validation process, including the timely written-dispute condition. Not all creditors or other countries are covered."},"1835":{"id":"/docs/research-183","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-5-making-a-debt-plan","content":"CFPB family lending"},"1836":{"id":"/docs/research-184","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-5-making-a-debt-plan","content":"Clarify whether money is a gift or loan, record terms, and discuss changed circumstances. No family-loan tax threshold is asserted."},"1837":{"id":"/docs/research-185","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-5-making-a-debt-plan","content":"Note 3 supplies the author's admission of debt after job loss and moving; no recovery is invented. Notes 7 and 9 supply the borrowing and shortfall questions. Uncertain exchange-rate amounts and the unfinished line-of-credit recommendation remain excluded."},"1838":{"id":"/docs/research-186","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-5-making-a-debt-plan","content":"Fictional Sam: $3,000 take-home − $2,400 non-debt needs/planned costs − $100 reserve = $500 for debt. Required payments $80 + $220 + $50 = $350, leaving $150 extra. Card payment becomes $230. For one explicitly simplified monthly interest calculation: $2,000 × 24% / 12 = $40; $3,000 × 12% / 12 = $30. Closing balances $1,810 + $2,810 + $350 = $4,970. Total reduction is $430, not the $500 paid. The same first-month total under the alternative allocation does not establish equal lifetime interest. With income $2,700, only $200 remains for $350 required payments: $150 short."},"1839":{"id":"/docs/research-187","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-5-making-a-debt-plan","content":"Workbook page 14 starts before bills and debt payments, then subtracts each commitment once. The primary integration review checked the source admission, calculations, worksheets, chapter routes, and the material line-of-credit and validation-notice claims against their primary pages."},"1840":{"id":"/docs/research-188","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-6-reading-and-paying-a-card-bill","content":"Checked October 1, 2026 for Chapter 6, its spoken adaptation, and workbook pages 15–16. The examples use US guidance and clearly invented generic-dollar terms."},"1841":{"id":"/docs/research-189","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-6-reading-and-paying-a-card-bill","content":"Primary source"},"1842":{"id":"/docs/research-190","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-6-reading-and-paying-a-card-bill","content":"Supported use and scope"},"1843":{"id":"/docs/research-191","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-6-reading-and-paying-a-card-bill","content":"CFPB card types, Consumer.gov cards"},"1844":{"id":"/docs/research-192","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-6-reading-and-paying-a-card-bill","content":"Debit/credit distinctions, borrowing limits, and minimum-payment implications."},"1845":{"id":"/docs/research-193","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-6-reading-and-paying-a-card-bill","content":"Capital One balance explanation"},"1846":{"id":"/docs/research-194","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-6-reading-and-paying-a-card-bill","content":"Statement versus current balance, posted activity, and dates. This is an issuer explanation, not a universal product agreement."},"1847":{"id":"/docs/research-195","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-6-reading-and-paying-a-card-bill","content":"CFPB card interest, grace periods, card terms"},"1848":{"id":"/docs/research-196","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-6-reading-and-paying-a-card-bill","content":"Transaction-specific rates, daily calculations, conditional purchase grace periods, and fees."},"1849":{"id":"/docs/research-197","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-6-reading-and-paying-a-card-bill","content":"Amex AutoPay terms, Pay Over Time"},"1850":{"id":"/docs/research-198","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-6-reading-and-paying-a-card-bill","content":"Specific US issuer illustration of due-in-full and flexible balances, plus spending capacity. The PDF is dated October 22, 2020 and was available when checked; it is not presented as every reader's current agreement."},"1851":{"id":"/docs/research-199","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-6-reading-and-paying-a-card-bill","content":"FTC card payments, CFPB payment difficulty"},"1852":{"id":"/docs/research-200","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-6-reading-and-paying-a-card-bill","content":"Plan and check funded payments; contact the issuer if the required payment does not fit. An inquiry is not approval for changed terms."},"1853":{"id":"/docs/research-201","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-6-reading-and-paying-a-card-bill","content":"Note 7 supplies the author's admission and rewards concern. The uncertain personal offer, remembered book, generic rate range, garbled spending line, and universal 30-day language are excluded. New examples do not reproduce an actual card statement."},"1854":{"id":"/docs/research-202","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-6-reading-and-paying-a-card-bill","content":"Arithmetic: $800 statement + $100 later purchase = $900 posted. A $30 payment leaves $870 before later activity, including $770 from the earlier statement. The simplified separate interest model is $1,000 × 0.24 / 365 × 30 = $19.73 rounded; after a $30 payment, $989.73 remains, a $10.27 reduction of the original amount. Assumptions exclude compounding and changing balances. The rewards examples give $10 on $500 at 2%, $2 on an extra $100, and a $20 deficit when $80 redeemed rewards are compared with a $100 fee before other costs/benefits."},"1855":{"id":"/docs/research-203","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-6-reading-and-paying-a-card-bill","content":"The integration review compared scripts and blank worksheets with the chapter, checked these calculations, and reopened the grace-period and issuer-terms sources. Own-card terms and processing times still need checking by the reader."},"1856":{"id":"/docs/research-204","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-7-reports-and-scores","content":"Checked October 1, 2026 for Chapter 7, its spoken adaptation, and workbook page 17. The worked system is explicitly the United States."},"1857":{"id":"/docs/research-205","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-7-reports-and-scores","content":"Primary source"},"1858":{"id":"/docs/research-206","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-7-reports-and-scores","content":"Supported use and scope"},"1859":{"id":"/docs/research-207","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-7-reports-and-scores","content":"CFPB credit reports, credit scores"},"1860":{"id":"/docs/research-208","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-7-reports-and-scores","content":"Records versus model outputs, differing scores and report uses."},"1861":{"id":"/docs/research-209","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-7-reports-and-scores","content":"FICO history"},"1862":{"id":"/docs/research-210","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-7-reports-and-scores","content":"Its US FICO Score introduction in 1989 only. The 2018 page is not used for current model-version or market-share claims."},"1863":{"id":"/docs/research-211","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-7-reports-and-scores","content":"FICO amounts owed, CFPB building credit"},"1864":{"id":"/docs/research-212","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-7-reports-and-scores","content":"Revolving-credit use and no need to carry a balance merely to build a score. No universal utilization cutoff or point forecast is asserted."},"1865":{"id":"/docs/research-213","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-7-reports-and-scores","content":"CFPB credit booklet"},"1866":{"id":"/docs/research-214","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-7-reports-and-scores","content":"Thin histories can lack a score; this differs from a low score."},"1867":{"id":"/docs/research-215","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-7-reports-and-scores","content":"CFPB inquiries"},"1868":{"id":"/docs/research-216","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-7-reports-and-scores","content":"Soft versus hard checks; an application process needs its own disclosure review."},"1869":{"id":"/docs/research-217","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-7-reports-and-scores","content":"Consumer.gov report access, CFPB disputes"},"1870":{"id":"/docs/research-218","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-7-reports-and-scores","content":"Weekly online access through AnnualCreditReport.com as checked, plus evidence-based disputes with the bureau and information supplier. Access terms remain date-specific."},"1871":{"id":"/docs/research-219","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-7-reports-and-scores","content":"Note 7's Equifax correction is retained. Its unclear report/application language and 720/750 assertions are not endorsed. Fictional calculations: $600 / $2,000 = 30% on one card; $600 / ($2,000 + $3,000) = 12% combined, not the average of two percentages. A $450 proposed payment against $200 available leaves a $250 gap. The paid-account $400 dispute example and follow-up date do not promise a correction or score change."},"1872":{"id":"/docs/research-220","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-7-reports-and-scores","content":"The primary integration review checked all three worksheet/script relationships and arithmetic, and independently reopened the weekly-report and FICO-history sources. No particular score, lender approval, or improvement date is promised."},"1873":{"id":"/docs/research-221","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-8-checking-a-purchase","content":"Checked October 1, 2026 for Chapter 8, its spoken adaptation, and workbook page 18. The coordinator reviewed the draft against Note 9 and opened all three primary sources below."},"1874":{"id":"/docs/research-222","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-8-checking-a-purchase","content":"Primary source"},"1875":{"id":"/docs/research-223","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-8-checking-a-purchase","content":"Supported use and scope"},"1876":{"id":"/docs/research-224","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-8-checking-a-purchase","content":"CFPB: What is a BNPL loan?"},"1877":{"id":"/docs/research-225","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-8-checking-a-purchase","content":"US description of instalment borrowing and differing plans; check the actual agreement. No named provider offer is assumed."},"1878":{"id":"/docs/research-226","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-8-checking-a-purchase","content":"CFPB: BNPL fees"},"1879":{"id":"/docs/research-227","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-8-checking-a-purchase","content":"Possible late and insufficient-funds bank fees depend on provider/account terms."},"1880":{"id":"/docs/research-228","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-8-checking-a-purchase","content":"CFPB: Compare auto loans"},"1881":{"id":"/docs/research-229","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-8-checking-a-purchase","content":"US educational comparison of amount, APR, term, payments, interest, and ownership costs. No current price or loan offer is recommended."},"1882":{"id":"/docs/research-230","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-8-checking-a-purchase","content":"Note 9's threshold correction to $250, saved-for exception, admissions, expected freelance-payment pattern, and enjoyment of cars are retained. “Afford it twice” remains the author's personal check, not an established affordability formula. Editorial steps protect money already assigned elsewhere and distinguish urgent needs. The $10,000/$2,000 freelance telling is illustrative, not asserted as a checked transaction. The unclear BNPL brand is omitted."},"1883":{"id":"/docs/research-231","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#chapter-8-checking-a-purchase","content":"Fictional generic-dollar arithmetic: $1,800 cash − $1,100 commitments = $700 free. Buying $350 headphones leaves $1,450 total cash, including $1,100 still committed and $350 free. A $250 option leaves $450 free. Four $87.50 instalments total $350; after the first, $262.50 remains owed. The first instalment plus existing $60 and $90 payments totals $237.50 before the next instalment. The zero-fee example is not a product offer. The obligation is recorded once and reduced by payments, not added again to the payment schedule as new spending."},"1884":{"id":"/docs/research-232","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#recording-research-queue","content":"Added October 1, 2026 from Notes 4–9. This is the original queue for the incorporation plan. The Chapter 2–8 sections above record completed checks, rebuilt examples, and excluded claims. The rows below preserve the original questions. Unused uncertain wording and named offers still need verification if introduced in a later revision; their omission does not block these first drafts."},"1885":{"id":"/docs/research-233","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#recording-research-queue","content":"Source and destination"},"1886":{"id":"/docs/research-234","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#recording-research-queue","content":"Work required before drafting"},"1887":{"id":"/docs/research-235","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#recording-research-queue","content":"Note 4, Chapters 2–3"},"1888":{"id":"/docs/research-236","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#recording-research-queue","content":"Settle Michelle's hypothetical background and household assumptions. Recheck cleaning cost at 13:23–13:47: local transcription passes disagree between $20 and $200 per week. Resolve the $6,500/$600 target change. Rebuild the book-sales and app examples with consistent units, conversion assumptions, costs, and tax treatment. Do not claim that posting for two or five years guarantees a target income or audience."},"1889":{"id":"/docs/research-237","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#recording-research-queue","content":"Notes 5–6, Chapter 3"},"1890":{"id":"/docs/research-238","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#recording-research-queue","content":"First draft now includes temporary-work and job-search options, with costs, eligibility, schedule, ability, care duties, and support checks. Keep the author's urgency and his student/stay-at-home-parent qualifications; do not turn them into an exhaustive list of who can be unable to work. No earnings or hiring outcome is established by these recordings."},"1891":{"id":"/docs/research-239","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#recording-research-queue","content":"Note 7, Chapters 5–6"},"1892":{"id":"/docs/research-240","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#recording-research-queue","content":"Use current issuer agreements for APRs, cash advances, minimum-payment formulas, fees, grace periods, charge-card spending limits and repayment terms, and lines of credit. Distinguish minimum due from the statement balance and the payment needed to avoid interest. Research overdrafts separately. Check country, product, date, and terms for the personal Amex $8,000/six-month offer. Verify the remembered Hormozi book/passage or leave it out."},"1893":{"id":"/docs/research-241","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#recording-research-queue","content":"Note 7, Chapter 7"},"1894":{"id":"/docs/research-242","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#recording-research-queue","content":"Use primary regulator, bureau, and scoring-provider sources for reports versus scores, bureaus versus score models, utilization, score ranges, application checks, eligibility, and late-payment reporting. Preserve the speaker's Vanguard-to-Equifax correction but do not present those companies as interchangeable score models. Do not make “30%,” “720/750,” “18,” or “30 days” universal rules."},"1895":{"id":"/docs/research-243","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#recording-research-queue","content":"Note 9, Chapter 4"},"1896":{"id":"/docs/research-244","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#recording-research-queue","content":"Separate take-home income, the amount after giving, and the remainder after bills. Rebuild the low-surplus example that changes from $500 to $300 to $400. Evaluate saving-versus-bill priorities for a shortfall using primary consumer guidance. Research payment timing and whether a provider permits due-date changes; do not promise that canceling/restarting a service changes its date without consequences. Treat 25–30% as the author's proposal, not an established rule for every reader."},"1897":{"id":"/docs/research-245","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#recording-research-queue","content":"Note 9, Chapter 4 faith passage"},"1898":{"id":"/docs/research-246","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#recording-research-queue","content":"Preserve the author's first-person tithing practice and belief. The example reserves $500 from $5,000, then plans from $4,500. If the draft quotes or interprets a biblical passage, identify and check the text; do not add a promised financial return for giving."},"1899":{"id":"/docs/research-247","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#recording-research-queue","content":"Note 9, Chapter 8"},"1900":{"id":"/docs/research-248","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#recording-research-queue","content":"Keep the correction from $500 to $250 and label “afford it twice” as a personal rule for unplanned purchases. Check the distinction between cash in an account and cash already committed to bills. Verify BNPL terms by provider/product, total car ownership costs and depreciation claims. Treat $350 headphones, the $1,500 camera, and illustrative freelance figures as source examples rather than current prices or fully documented transactions."},"1901":{"id":"/docs/research-249","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#recording-research-queue","content":"Source leads for the later research pass: issuer agreements and official offer terms; relevant consumer regulators; credit bureaus and scoring providers; official BNPL agreements; and the original cited book. Record the actual source, date, scope, and supported passage when checked. Naming a source lead is not verification."},"1902":{"id":"/docs/research-250","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#recording-research-queue","content":"Local automated rechecks of Note 4's numerical passages, Note 6's closing job-choice wording, and Note 7's application/spending/report passages left uncertainty. Preserve those flags. Note 9's shortfall wording around 16:29–16:43 also needs checking before quotation."},"1903":{"id":"/docs/research-251","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#stories-and-examples","content":"Personal stories come from the recordings. Confirm uncertain dates, words, and amounts before developing them. Chapter 1 uses the job-loss story without the recording's uncertain expense figures. Its named people and dollar examples are labelled as hypothetical."},"1904":{"id":"/docs/research-252","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#stories-and-examples","content":"For each new fact, keep the source link, publisher, date, applicable country, and the passage or calculation that supports it. A source lead does not count as a completed check."},"1905":{"id":"/docs/research-253","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#decisions-still-open","content":"The title and final words of the book's promise."},"1906":{"id":"/docs/research-254","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#decisions-still-open","content":"Visual symbols for the reader paths."},"1907":{"id":"/docs/research-255","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#decisions-still-open","content":"The exact worked examples and any legal or tax systems needed in later chapters. Generic dollars do not make those rules universal."},"1908":{"id":"/docs/research-256","page_id":"/docs/research","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/research#decisions-still-open","content":"The definition of debt and the use of generic dollars are settled. The smaller questions have chapter homes in the book plan."},"1909":{"id":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy","page_id":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy","type":"page","content":"8. Before you buy — spoken script","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Spoken scripts"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy"},"1910":{"id":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy-0","page_id":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy","content":"A spoken draft about wanted purchases, money still to come, and payments that stack up."},"1911":{"id":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy-1","page_id":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy#the-script","content":"The script"},"1912":{"id":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy-2","page_id":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy#exercise-and-source-notes","content":"Exercise and source notes"},"1913":{"id":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy-3","page_id":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy","content":"**Spoken adaptation · First draft.** Based on Chapter 8. This is developed prose, not a raw transcript."},"1914":{"id":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy-4","page_id":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy#the-script","content":"You want the thing. The headphones, the camera, the car. Okay. What happens to the rest of your money if you buy it?"},"1915":{"id":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy-5","page_id":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy#the-script","content":"I love cars. They make me happy. I don't think you have to pretend a purchase is going to earn money before you're allowed to want it. Your quality of life matters too."},"1916":{"id":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy-6","page_id":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy#the-script","content":"But I don't want you to buy something you wanted and then hate the payments. So let's think about it now."},"1917":{"id":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy-7","page_id":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy#the-script","content":"For an unplanned purchase over two hundred and fifty dollars, I try to have enough to pay for it twice. If the headphones cost three hundred and fifty, I want seven hundred available."},"1918":{"id":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy-8","page_id":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy#the-script","content":"That's my personal rule. I don't get it right every time. Something goes on sale, and suddenly I'm explaining to myself why I need it. So the rule gives me a pause."},"1919":{"id":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy-9","page_id":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy#the-script","content":"I'm talking about an unplanned purchase. If you've been saving for something, check that plan and what the purchase leaves behind. I'm not saying you need to save for the same thing twice. An urgent need is a different decision too. My rule isn't a reason to leave an essential need unmet."},"1920":{"id":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy-10","page_id":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy#the-script","content":"And we need to check what “available” means. If you've got seven hundred in the account and the rent needs seven hundred, you don't have seven hundred for headphones."},"1921":{"id":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy-11","page_id":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy#the-script","content":"Here's an invented example from the chapter. You have eighteen hundred in cash. Eleven hundred is already assigned to bills, debt, tax, saving, giving, and other commitments. That leaves seven hundred free."},"1922":{"id":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy-12","page_id":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy#the-script","content":"Buy the three-hundred-and-fifty-dollar headphones, and you have three hundred and fifty free afterward. The account still has fourteen hundred and fifty in it, but eleven hundred of that still has a job. Don't spend it twice."},"1923":{"id":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy-13","page_id":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy#the-script","content":"You could buy a suitable two-hundred-and-fifty-dollar pair and keep four hundred and fifty free. You could keep what you have and leave all seven hundred alone. Those are choices. Write down which one you're making and why."},"1924":{"id":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy-14","page_id":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy#the-script","content":"Now, let's talk about money that hasn't come in yet."},"1925":{"id":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy-15","page_id":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy#the-script","content":"I've fallen into this doing software work on the side. You have a conversation about a job. You expect a payment. In your mind, you've already started spending it. Then the money doesn't arrive when you expected. The expense is still there."},"1926":{"id":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy-16","page_id":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy#the-script","content":"Let's say you're expecting ten thousand from a job and you spend two thousand on a computer before you're paid. Those numbers illustrate the pattern. The money still has to arrive, while the purchase has already happened."},"1927":{"id":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy-17","page_id":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy#the-script","content":"If the purchase can wait, wait for the payment. Then count the costs, tax, and other commitments it still has to cover. Next week's paycheck isn't money in the account today either."},"1928":{"id":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy-18","page_id":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy#the-script","content":"And don't use a card limit as the answer. A two-thousand-dollar limit doesn't tell you whether a fifteen-hundred-dollar camera fits your own plan. Go back to the money you can use and how you'll pay the bill."},"1929":{"id":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy-19","page_id":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy#the-script","content":"Buy now, pay later needs the same attention. It's borrowing. One small payment might seem fine. Then you get another, and another. They stack up."},"1930":{"id":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy-20","page_id":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy#the-script","content":"Write every payment down. The amount today. The later amounts and dates. The full amount you owe. The terms and any costs. Then put those dates beside the payments you've already agreed to make."},"1931":{"id":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy-21","page_id":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy#the-script","content":"The chapter invents a plan for the three-hundred-and-fifty-dollar headphones: four payments of eighty-seven fifty, two weeks apart, with no interest or fees assumed. It's a maths example, not a product offer."},"1932":{"id":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy-22","page_id":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy#the-script","content":"After the first payment, you still owe two hundred and sixty-two fifty for the headphones. If another sixty-dollar payment and a ninety-dollar payment fall before the next instalment, those three payments need two hundred and thirty-seven fifty altogether. And your other bills are still there."},"1933":{"id":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy-23","page_id":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy#the-script","content":"Check the actual agreement. Fees and automatic-payment arrangements differ. A small instalment doesn't tell you the full cost."},"1934":{"id":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy-24","page_id":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy#the-script","content":"If you're already struggling with required payments, I'd go back to that problem before taking on another loan for something optional."},"1935":{"id":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy-25","page_id":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy#the-script","content":"The same thinking applies to a car. Yes, it can be something you enjoy. But count the whole cost and the whole loan. Insurance, fuel, maintenance, the things you'll have to pay to keep using it. Then look at what would happen in a lower-income month."},"1936":{"id":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy-26","page_id":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy#the-script","content":"Would you still enjoy the car if its payments took away something else you care about?"},"1937":{"id":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy-27","page_id":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy#the-script","content":"Okay, now pick one purchase and use workbook page eighteen, or plain paper. Name it and say why you want it. Work out the money available after commitments. Compare the choices and their full costs. Put any payments on the calendar."},"1938":{"id":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy-28","page_id":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy#the-script","content":"Then make a decision: buy, save first, choose something else, wait, or check a missing detail. Give yourself a next action and a date."},"1939":{"id":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy-29","page_id":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy#the-script","content":"You can want the thing. I just want you to see what getting it would mean for the rest of your life."},"1940":{"id":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy-30","page_id":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy#exercise-and-source-notes","content":"Use 18. My purchase check or the plain-paper instructions in Chapter 8. The chapter includes the filled tables, urgent-needs qualification, and separate next chapter for each reader path."},"1941":{"id":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy-31","page_id":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/before-you-buy#exercise-and-source-notes","content":"Voice and personal material come from Note 9, 19:02–end. The script preserves the correction to $250 and the saved-for exception. Added cash and instalment examples are fictional and match the chapter. US factual support: CFPB BNPL explanation, BNPL fees, and auto-loan comparisons, checked October 1, 2026. Product terms and country rules need their own checks."},"1942":{"id":"/docs/scripts/credit-cards","page_id":"/docs/scripts/credit-cards","type":"page","content":"6. Credit cards 101 — spoken script","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Spoken scripts"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/scripts/credit-cards"},"1943":{"id":"/docs/scripts/credit-cards-0","page_id":"/docs/scripts/credit-cards","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/credit-cards","content":"A spoken adaptation of the card statement and payment exercise."},"1944":{"id":"/docs/scripts/credit-cards-1","page_id":"/docs/scripts/credit-cards","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/scripts/credit-cards#script","content":"Script"},"1945":{"id":"/docs/scripts/credit-cards-2","page_id":"/docs/scripts/credit-cards","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/scripts/credit-cards#exercise-and-next-page","content":"Exercise and next page"},"1946":{"id":"/docs/scripts/credit-cards-3","page_id":"/docs/scripts/credit-cards","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/credit-cards","content":"**Draft spoken adaptation.** Based on Chapter 6 and Note 7, especially 01:05–04:25 and 08:04–11:09. This is edited prose, not a transcript. The example is invented and uses generic dollars; the linked financial guidance is US guidance."},"1947":{"id":"/docs/scripts/credit-cards-4","page_id":"/docs/scripts/credit-cards","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/credit-cards#script","content":"Have you ever paid with your phone and barely thought about it? You tap, take your stuff, and leave. Then the bill comes."},"1948":{"id":"/docs/scripts/credit-cards-5","page_id":"/docs/scripts/credit-cards","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/credit-cards#script","content":"That's what worries me about credit cards. Spending is easy. Keeping track of what you've borrowed takes more work. And I'm including myself in that. I'm still working on managing debt too."},"1949":{"id":"/docs/scripts/credit-cards-6","page_id":"/docs/scripts/credit-cards","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/credit-cards#script","content":"There can be benefits. You might get points from something you were already going to buy. But are you buying what you need, or are you buying more to get the points?"},"1950":{"id":"/docs/scripts/credit-cards-7","page_id":"/docs/scripts/credit-cards","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/credit-cards#script","content":"Let's say a made-up offer pays 2% back. You spend an extra $100 on things you didn't want and earn $2. You still spent $98. That's why I want us to look at the bill before the rewards."},"1951":{"id":"/docs/scripts/credit-cards-8","page_id":"/docs/scripts/credit-cards","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/credit-cards#script","content":"Okay, here's a fictional bill. It closes on April 30 with an $800 statement balance. The minimum is $30, due May 25. Then you make a new $100 purchase on May 3. Nothing else changes. Now the posted balance is $900."},"1952":{"id":"/docs/scripts/credit-cards-9","page_id":"/docs/scripts/credit-cards","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/credit-cards#script","content":"If you pay $30, you haven't cleared the bill. You have $870 left before any later interest or activity. If you pay $800, you've covered the April statement. The later $100 is still owed. Paying $900 covers both amounts in this example."},"1953":{"id":"/docs/scripts/credit-cards-10","page_id":"/docs/scripts/credit-cards","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/credit-cards#script","content":"Those figures come from a made-up card with no earlier carried balance, no fees, and an active purchase grace period. Your card may be different. On US cards, a purchase grace period can depend on paying in full under the card's terms. Carrying a balance can remove it, and cash advances generally start accruing interest right away. Check your own agreement. CFPB: grace periods."},"1954":{"id":"/docs/scripts/credit-cards-11","page_id":"/docs/scripts/credit-cards","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/credit-cards#script","content":"Now let's turn that bill into a plan. Where will the payment come from? What else does that money need to cover? Which date will you send it? And how will you check that it went through?"},"1955":{"id":"/docs/scripts/credit-cards-12","page_id":"/docs/scripts/credit-cards","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/credit-cards#script","content":"For the example, you might have $900 already reserved. You pay the $800 statement in time and keep $100 for the later purchase. You don't spend that $100 again. It already has a job."},"1956":{"id":"/docs/scripts/credit-cards-13","page_id":"/docs/scripts/credit-cards","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/credit-cards#script","content":"If you can't make the minimum, write that down too. Contact the issuer and explain what you can pay. Ask what help is available and what the terms would be. Don't assume help has been agreed just because you've asked. CFPB: payment difficulty."},"1957":{"id":"/docs/scripts/credit-cards-14","page_id":"/docs/scripts/credit-cards","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/credit-cards#script","content":"Use workbook page 15, My card statement, and page 16, My card payment plan. Plain paper works. Write your balances, the rate and fees, the minimum, and the due date. Then write the funded payment and your next check."},"1958":{"id":"/docs/scripts/credit-cards-15","page_id":"/docs/scripts/credit-cards","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/credit-cards#script","content":"The useful result is a bill you understand and a payment you can explain. Start there."},"1959":{"id":"/docs/scripts/credit-cards-16","page_id":"/docs/scripts/credit-cards","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/credit-cards#exercise-and-next-page","content":"Use page 15 and page 16, or the same headings on paper. Readers with income go next to Chapter 7. Readers without income go next to Chapter 3, while following through on any urgent payment contact."},"1960":{"id":"/docs/scripts/credit-scores","page_id":"/docs/scripts/credit-scores","type":"page","content":"7. Credit scores 101 — spoken script","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Spoken scripts"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/scripts/credit-scores"},"1961":{"id":"/docs/scripts/credit-scores-0","page_id":"/docs/scripts/credit-scores","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/credit-scores","content":"A spoken guide to the record behind the number."},"1962":{"id":"/docs/scripts/credit-scores-1","page_id":"/docs/scripts/credit-scores","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/scripts/credit-scores#script","content":"Script"},"1963":{"id":"/docs/scripts/credit-scores-2","page_id":"/docs/scripts/credit-scores","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/scripts/credit-scores#exercise-and-next-page","content":"Exercise and next page"},"1964":{"id":"/docs/scripts/credit-scores-3","page_id":"/docs/scripts/credit-scores","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/credit-scores","content":"**Draft spoken adaptation.** Based on Chapter 7 and the questions in Note 7, around 06:47–07:45 and 09:20–10:33. This is edited prose, not a transcript. The system described is the United States; the worked figures are invented."},"1965":{"id":"/docs/scripts/credit-scores-4","page_id":"/docs/scripts/credit-scores","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/credit-scores#script","content":"What is a credit score? And what are you supposed to do with it?"},"1966":{"id":"/docs/scripts/credit-scores-5","page_id":"/docs/scripts/credit-scores","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/credit-scores#script","content":"If you're getting ready to borrow for a home, you probably want to know what a lender sees. But if you need money for food this week, checking a number again and again won't fix that. Let's understand what it does."},"1967":{"id":"/docs/scripts/credit-scores-6","page_id":"/docs/scripts/credit-scores","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/credit-scores#script","content":"A US credit report holds information about your credit accounts and payment history. A score uses report information to estimate credit behavior. The report is the record. The score is a calculation based on it. CFPB: reports, scores."},"1968":{"id":"/docs/scripts/credit-scores-7","page_id":"/docs/scripts/credit-scores","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/credit-scores#script","content":"So start by reading the record. Do you know these accounts? Are the balances and payments right? If something looks wrong, compare the dates and your own statements before deciding it's an error."},"1969":{"id":"/docs/scripts/credit-scores-8","page_id":"/docs/scripts/credit-scores","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/credit-scores#script","content":"Here's an example. One card reports $600 owed with a $2,000 limit. That's 30% of the limit. Another card reports zero owed with a $3,000 limit. Together, that's $600 out of $5,000, or 12%."},"1970":{"id":"/docs/scripts/credit-scores-9","page_id":"/docs/scripts/credit-scores","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/credit-scores#script","content":"That calculation is called credit utilization. It describes how much available credit is being used. It doesn't tell us whether you can afford the $600. It also doesn't give you permission to spend until you reach some percentage. FICO: amounts owed."},"1971":{"id":"/docs/scripts/credit-scores-10","page_id":"/docs/scripts/credit-scores","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/credit-scores#script","content":"And you don't need to carry a card balance and pay interest just to build a good score. Keep the payment plan in view. CFPB: building credit."},"1972":{"id":"/docs/scripts/credit-scores-11","page_id":"/docs/scripts/credit-scores","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/credit-scores#script","content":"Say you open a report and find a paid account still showing $400. That's our invented example. Check the report's date against your payoff receipt. If it still appears wrong, use the provider's inquiry or dispute process with the records that support you. In the US, the CFPB explains how to contact both the bureau and the business that supplied the information. CFPB: disputes."},"1973":{"id":"/docs/scripts/credit-scores-12","page_id":"/docs/scripts/credit-scores","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/credit-scores#script","content":"Now you've got an action: check this account, send the record if needed, and follow up. That's something you can do. “Get a perfect score” doesn't tell you what to do today."},"1974":{"id":"/docs/scripts/credit-scores-13","page_id":"/docs/scripts/credit-scores","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/credit-scores#script","content":"Use workbook page 17, My credit check. You can use paper instead. Write why you're checking, where the report came from, its date, and what you need to check. If you have a score, record which model it is and when it was calculated. If you don't, keep going with the report."},"1975":{"id":"/docs/scripts/credit-scores-14","page_id":"/docs/scripts/credit-scores","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/credit-scores#script","content":"Then pick one action and a date. If the record is accurate but you can't cover a payment, the action belongs in your debt and income plan. If it's wrong, work on the error. You don't have to solve every money problem by moving one number."},"1976":{"id":"/docs/scripts/credit-scores-15","page_id":"/docs/scripts/credit-scores","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/credit-scores#exercise-and-next-page","content":"Use page 17 and Chapter 7's country-specific access steps. Readers with income go next to Chapter 8. Readers without income go next to Chapter 3, while following up on urgent debt or report issues."},"1977":{"id":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income","page_id":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income","type":"page","content":"3. Find and grow your income — spoken script","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Spoken scripts"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income"},"1978":{"id":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income-0","page_id":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income","content":"A spoken draft about getting income now and choosing a route for later."},"1979":{"id":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income-1","page_id":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income#the-script","content":"The script"},"1980":{"id":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income-2","page_id":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income#exercise-and-source-notes","content":"Exercise and source notes"},"1981":{"id":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income-3","page_id":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income","content":"**Spoken adaptation · First draft.** Based on Chapter 3. This is developed prose, not a transcript of a recording."},"1982":{"id":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income-4","page_id":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income#the-script","content":"What could bring money in from where you are today?"},"1983":{"id":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income-5","page_id":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income#the-script","content":"If you have no income, need money coming in, and can work, I think you need to widen the search. Apply for work you can do. Don't leave a job out just because it isn't at the company you had in mind."},"1984":{"id":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income-6","page_id":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income#the-script","content":"It can be temporary. You can still work toward something else."},"1985":{"id":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income-7","page_id":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income#the-script","content":"Now, that has to fit your actual life. Being a stay-at-home parent is a responsibility. Studying takes time. Health, care duties, transport, and permission to work can limit your options. So start with what you can do. If you need support, make checking that part of the plan. If essentials aren't covered today, a possible job next month doesn't close that gap today."},"1986":{"id":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income-8","page_id":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income#the-script","content":"But if the only thing stopping you from applying is that the job doesn't look like the one you imagined, I'd ask you to look again."},"1987":{"id":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income-9","page_id":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income#the-script","content":"I had a conversation with someone at Starbucks about choosing how to get to a goal. Think about trying to get to the top of a sand dune in a Ferrari. You've decided on the car. But does it suit where you're trying to go?"},"1988":{"id":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income-10","page_id":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income#the-script","content":"For me, I had this idea of being a tech founder. Then I thought about what I wanted from that. I wanted a say in how a product works, how it's designed, and how it's built. That helped me look at the goal differently."},"1989":{"id":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income-11","page_id":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income#the-script","content":"So what do you want from your work?"},"1990":{"id":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income-12","page_id":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income#the-script","content":"If your job gives you the money and the time you need, you don't have to start a business. If it doesn't, we can look at what might change. The hours. The pay. The role. Or something you do yourself."},"1991":{"id":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income-13","page_id":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income#the-script","content":"Let's go back to Michelle. She's our fictional example. Her household plan costs eight thousand four hundred dollars a month. Her partner covers two thousand four hundred. That leaves six thousand for her. Her take-home pay is three thousand, so there's a three-thousand-dollar gap."},"1992":{"id":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income-14","page_id":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income#the-script","content":"She wants more time for writing and research. She also wants to be there for school pickup. So we're looking at money and time together."},"1993":{"id":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income-15","page_id":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income#the-script","content":"She could look at a writing job. She could test an editing service. She could work on a book. Those are options to check. None of them is money coming in yet."},"1994":{"id":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income-16","page_id":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income#the-script","content":"For this example, she keeps her job and starts with one small check. Two hours this week. No spending. She makes a sample with text she owns and asks three possible customers about their needs by Friday. On Sunday, she reviews what she learned."},"1995":{"id":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income-17","page_id":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income#the-script","content":"That's the step. She hasn't closed the gap. She's finding out whether this is worth more time."},"1996":{"id":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income-18","page_id":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income#the-script","content":"And when money does come in, count it properly. In the chapter, we invent a batch of four editing jobs at a hundred and fifty dollars each. That's six hundred received. We assume a hundred and twenty in business costs, leaving four hundred and eighty before tax. We count the unpaid admin and customer-finding time too."},"1997":{"id":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income-19","page_id":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income#the-script","content":"That isn't take-home pay. It isn't a promise of more jobs next week. It's a way to look at what one batch actually gave you."},"1998":{"id":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income-20","page_id":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income#the-script","content":"Okay, now do this for yourself. Use workbook pages eight and nine, or plain paper. Write down your starting point and what limits your options. Compare two possible routes if you have two. What do you know, and what do you need to check?"},"1999":{"id":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income-21","page_id":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income#the-script","content":"Then pick one action. Give it a date and a limit on the time and money you'll use. Pick a day to look at what happened."},"2000":{"id":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income-22","page_id":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income#the-script","content":"If your first step is a support application, write that. If it's a job application, write that. If it's a conversation about pay, write that."},"2001":{"id":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income-23","page_id":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income#the-script","content":"You don't need to figure out your whole career today. You do need to know what you're going to do next."},"2002":{"id":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income-24","page_id":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income#exercise-and-source-notes","content":"Use 8. My income options and 9. My next income step. The full Chapter 3 exercise works on plain paper and covers costs, tax, uncertainty, and payment dates."},"2003":{"id":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income-25","page_id":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/grow-your-income#exercise-and-source-notes","content":"Voice and ideas: Notes 5–6 for the opening; Note 4, 04:11–08:22 for the adviser retelling; Note 4's Michelle and income-route discussion for the fictional example. The spoken adaptation uses the same settled assumptions as Chapters 2–3. It adds no claim that Michelle has earned from editing. See Chapter 3 research for the supporting checks."},"2004":{"id":"/docs/scripts","page_id":"/docs/scripts","type":"page","content":"Spoken scripts","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Spoken scripts"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/scripts"},"2005":{"id":"/docs/scripts-0","page_id":"/docs/scripts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts","content":"Spoken adaptations of the book drafts, with the same examples and source notes."},"2006":{"id":"/docs/scripts-1","page_id":"/docs/scripts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts","content":"These are draft scripts for speaking aloud. The full explanations, exercises, and research remain in the linked chapters. They are separate from the original recordings in Raw thoughts."},"2007":{"id":"/docs/scripts-2","page_id":"/docs/scripts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts","content":"The chapters have since received the October 1 voice revision described in the writing plan. These scripts retain their earlier wording; their examples and workbook references still match."},"2008":{"id":"/docs/scripts-3","page_id":"/docs/scripts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts","content":"Chapter 2: The life you want — a first spoken draft with Michelle's example and workbook pages 5–7."},"2009":{"id":"/docs/scripts-4","page_id":"/docs/scripts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts","content":"Chapter 3: Find and grow your income — a first spoken draft with one income step and workbook pages 8–9."},"2010":{"id":"/docs/scripts-5","page_id":"/docs/scripts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts","content":"Chapter 4: When you get paid — a first spoken draft covering bills, affordable saving, giving, and shortfalls."},"2011":{"id":"/docs/scripts-6","page_id":"/docs/scripts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts","content":"Chapter 5: Make a plan for debt — a first spoken adaptation with its chapter exercise."},"2012":{"id":"/docs/scripts-7","page_id":"/docs/scripts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts","content":"Chapter 6: Credit cards 101 — a first spoken adaptation with its chapter exercise."},"2013":{"id":"/docs/scripts-8","page_id":"/docs/scripts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts","content":"Chapter 7: Credit scores 101 — a first spoken adaptation with its chapter exercise."},"2014":{"id":"/docs/scripts-9","page_id":"/docs/scripts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts","content":"Chapter 8: Before you buy — a first spoken adaptation with its chapter exercise."},"2015":{"id":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt","page_id":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt","type":"page","content":"5. Make a plan for debt — spoken script","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Spoken scripts"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt"},"2016":{"id":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt-0","page_id":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt","content":"A spoken adaptation of Chapter 5 about the next payment and the next action."},"2017":{"id":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt-1","page_id":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt#script","content":"Script"},"2018":{"id":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt-2","page_id":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt#source-and-recording-notes","content":"Source and recording notes"},"2019":{"id":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt-3","page_id":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt","content":"**Draft adaptation of Chapter 5.** This is a speaking script, not a transcript. The chapter holds full product explanations and country-specific sources."},"2020":{"id":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt-4","page_id":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt#script","content":"What do you do when you owe money and every dollar coming in already has somewhere to go?"},"2021":{"id":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt-5","page_id":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt#script","content":"I've been there. I lost my job during my honeymoon. My wife and I moved to New York with wedding debt, and I spent a lot of time living on credit. When I recorded these thoughts, I was still trying to work my way out of that hole."},"2022":{"id":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt-6","page_id":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt#script","content":"So let's start with what you actually owe. The card. The loan. The unpaid bill. The money your friend lent you. Even the thing someone told you was good debt. If you owe it, put it on the page."},"2023":{"id":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt-7","page_id":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt#script","content":"Now add the details. Who gets paid? How much is due? When? What rate and fees apply? Is anything already overdue? Is there a car or home pledged as backup?"},"2024":{"id":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt-8","page_id":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt#script","content":"A credit limit isn't your income. An overdraft isn't extra pay. If the money came from borrowing, you still need a way to pay it back. Check your agreement rather than guessing from the name on the product."},"2025":{"id":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt-9","page_id":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt#script","content":"Before choosing which balance to pay down faster, deal with right now. What needs paying so you can eat, stay housed, and keep working? Are there notices or deadlines you need help with? Put those on the page."},"2026":{"id":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt-10","page_id":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt#script","content":"Sometimes there's a shortfall. I know. If there isn't enough, don't write a repayment plan using money you don't have. Find the gap. Check what can change. Contact the people you owe and find out what options they actually offer. Write down anything they agree to."},"2027":{"id":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt-11","page_id":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt#script","content":"Okay, let's say the required payments fit and you have something extra. You could aim it at the highest rate. Or aim it at the smallest balance to get that one finished. Keep the other agreed payments in the plan."},"2028":{"id":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt-12","page_id":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt#script","content":"Here's a made-up example. Sam brings home $3,000 this month. His non-debt needs and planned costs take $2,400. He keeps $100 for unexpected needs. That leaves $500 for his debts."},"2029":{"id":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt-13","page_id":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt#script","content":"His card requires $80. His personal loan requires $220. He agreed to pay his sister $50. That's $350 required, leaving $150 extra."},"2030":{"id":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt-14","page_id":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt#script","content":"He puts the extra toward his card. So the card gets $230, the loan gets $220, and his sister gets $50. All together, $500."},"2031":{"id":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt-15","page_id":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt#script","content":"In the chapter's simplified example, $70 of interest is added before those payments. His total debt starts at $5,400 and ends at $4,970. He paid $500, but the balance went down by $430. The statement matters. Some of the payment covered interest."},"2032":{"id":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt-16","page_id":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt#script","content":"Now if his income drops, this plan changes. We can't just keep the same extra payment because it looked good on paper last month."},"2033":{"id":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt-17","page_id":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt#script","content":"And if money is owed to someone you know, talk clearly. Is it a gift or a loan? What did you agree? If you need more time, have that conversation. Don't quietly treat their money as the payment that doesn't count."},"2034":{"id":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt-18","page_id":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt#script","content":"Use workbook pages 13 and 14, or two sheets of paper. First, your debt details. Then, your plan. Write the amount you can pay, the dates, and the next action. If the next action is a call or finding income, write that. It still counts as work you need to do."},"2035":{"id":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt-19","page_id":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt#script","content":"Then choose a date to look again. What got paid? What was added? What do you owe now?"},"2036":{"id":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt-20","page_id":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt#script","content":"You don't have to solve every balance today. But you do need to know what happens next."},"2037":{"id":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt-21","page_id":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt#source-and-recording-notes","content":"Personal experience: Note 3. Borrowing distinctions: Note 7. Shortfall voice and conditions: Note 9, 14:01–18:42. The borrowing point is supported by FCAC's line-of-credit explanation and overdraft explanation. Repayment approaches: CFPB comparison. Family agreements: CFPB guidance."},"2038":{"id":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt-22","page_id":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/make-a-plan-for-debt#source-and-recording-notes","content":"Sam is fictional. His figures match the chapter's stated assumptions. For tax notices or collections, use the chapter's scoped guidance rather than treating this shorter script as a legal process."},"2039":{"id":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want","page_id":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want","type":"page","content":"Chapter 2: The life you want","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Spoken scripts"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want"},"2040":{"id":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want-0","page_id":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want","content":"A first spoken adaptation of the lifestyle exercise and Michelle's example."},"2041":{"id":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want-1","page_id":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want#where-are-you-trying-to-go","content":"Where are you trying to go?"},"2042":{"id":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want-2","page_id":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want#put-numbers-beside-it","content":"Put numbers beside it"},"2043":{"id":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want-3","page_id":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want#your-numbers","content":"Your numbers"},"2044":{"id":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want-4","page_id":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want#choose-a-next-step","content":"Choose a next step"},"2045":{"id":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want-5","page_id":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want#source-and-delivery-notes","content":"Source and delivery notes"},"2046":{"id":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want-6","page_id":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want","content":"*Spoken draft adapted from Chapter 2. Read with the chapter's worked table and workbook pages 5–7 available. This is edited prose, not a transcript or a recording of the author.*"},"2047":{"id":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want-7","page_id":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want#where-are-you-trying-to-go","content":"Okay, so you've put your money on paper. You know what you have. You can see what comes in and what you owe."},"2048":{"id":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want-8","page_id":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want#where-are-you-trying-to-go","content":"Now I want you to think about the life you're trying to get to. What does that actually look like?"},"2049":{"id":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want-9","page_id":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want#where-are-you-trying-to-go","content":"Let's say you want to get up early, go to the gym, take your kids to school, and be home for dinner. Great. Let's write that down. Maybe you don't want kids. Maybe you want to travel. Maybe your priority is knowing the bills are covered. Write your own version."},"2050":{"id":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want-10","page_id":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want#where-are-you-trying-to-go","content":"I want you to get into the details. Where do you live? What do you spend your day doing? What do you want time for?"},"2051":{"id":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want-11","page_id":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want#where-are-you-trying-to-go","content":"Take a minute and put that on workbook page 5, My chosen life. Or write it on paper. Then mark what matters most and what you could change."},"2052":{"id":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want-12","page_id":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want#put-numbers-beside-it","content":"Now let's look at Michelle. She's the fictional person in this chapter. She wants to spend her working day thinking, researching, and writing. Being available for her kids matters to her. She also wants a nicer car, cleaning help, and money going into savings."},"2053":{"id":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want-13","page_id":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want#put-numbers-beside-it","content":"We've made up a complete set of amounts to show the calculation. These aren't quotes for what your life will cost."},"2054":{"id":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want-14","page_id":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want#put-numbers-beside-it","content":"Her household plan comes to $8,400 a month. That includes $2,500 in saving. It also includes the bills, the car costs, care, and payments on existing debt."},"2055":{"id":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want-15","page_id":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want#put-numbers-beside-it","content":"There's a moving goal too. It costs $6,000 in this example. She already has $1,200 set aside, so she needs another $4,800. Over 24 months, that's $200 a month. That $200 is already inside her saving total. Count it once."},"2056":{"id":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want-16","page_id":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want#put-numbers-beside-it","content":"For this example, her partner contributes $2,400 a month. So Michelle needs to bring in $6,000. We're talking about money after deductions and any costs or tax still to cover."},"2057":{"id":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want-17","page_id":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want#put-numbers-beside-it","content":"Right now, we're giving her $3,000 a month in take-home pay. So there's a $3,000 gap."},"2058":{"id":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want-18","page_id":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want#put-numbers-beside-it","content":"But remember what she wanted her day to look like. If a job gives her the money but means she can never do the school pickup, she still has something to work through."},"2059":{"id":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want-19","page_id":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want#your-numbers","content":"Use workbook page 6, What that life costs. Or write a list with the cost, how often you pay it, its monthly share, and where the number came from. Keep one-time goals separate, then include their monthly saving amount once."},"2060":{"id":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want-20","page_id":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want#your-numbers","content":"You don't know a number yet? Write that down too. We can come back to it."},"2061":{"id":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want-21","page_id":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want#your-numbers","content":"Then use page 7, My target and next step. Compare your target with income for the same person or household, after costs and tax still to cover. Keep the period the same. If your income changes, show the range. Keep gaps in the information visible."},"2062":{"id":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want-22","page_id":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want#your-numbers","content":"The full example and the steps are in the chapter. Pause here if you want to work through them."},"2063":{"id":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want-23","page_id":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want#choose-a-next-step","content":"Pick one thing to check or explore. Give it a date. You don't have to solve your whole life on this page."},"2064":{"id":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want-24","page_id":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want#choose-a-next-step","content":"If you have income and debt, go to Chapter 4, When you get paid. For the other three paths, go to Chapter 3, Find and grow your income. If you have no income or support, finding income comes first; you can come back to the future estimates."},"2065":{"id":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want-25","page_id":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want#choose-a-next-step","content":"Keep the life you described beside your current money picture. Now you have a starting point and somewhere you want to go."},"2066":{"id":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want-26","page_id":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want#source-and-delivery-notes","content":"The voice and goal exercise come from Note 3 and Note 4, chiefly 01:00–04:25 and 08:45–15:23. Michelle's figures are the rebuilt fictional example in Chapter 2, not claims about the author's income or current market prices. The chapter links the primary sources for the planning method and defines the example's limits."},"2067":{"id":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want-27","page_id":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/the-life-you-want#source-and-delivery-notes","content":"Keep the pauses natural. Show the chapter's full table when reading the numbers; do not present this short script as the entire costing exercise."},"2068":{"id":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid","page_id":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid","type":"page","content":"4. When you get paid — spoken script","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Spoken scripts"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid"},"2069":{"id":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid-0","page_id":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid","content":"A spoken draft about planning bills, saving, giving, and a shortfall."},"2070":{"id":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid-1","page_id":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid#the-script","content":"The script"},"2071":{"id":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid-2","page_id":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid#exercise-and-source-notes","content":"Exercise and source notes"},"2072":{"id":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid-3","page_id":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid","content":"**Spoken adaptation · First draft.** Based on Chapter 4. This is developed prose, not a raw transcript."},"2073":{"id":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid-4","page_id":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid#the-script","content":"Okay, so you've been paid. What happens next?"},"2074":{"id":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid-5","page_id":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid#the-script","content":"My mom used to tell me to pay myself first. And I think there's value in that. You want to get into the habit of putting something away, instead of spending everything and hoping there's money left at the end."},"2075":{"id":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid-6","page_id":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid#the-script","content":"But before we choose a percentage, we need to look at your bills."},"2076":{"id":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid-7","page_id":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid#the-script","content":"What does this money have to cover? Rent? Food? Getting to work? Debt payments? When does each one need to be paid?"},"2077":{"id":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid-8","page_id":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid#the-script","content":"Write it down. Look through the whole month and check for bigger bills coming later. Don't spend on a subscription just because its payment comes out before rent. That rent money is already needed, even while you can see it sitting in your account."},"2078":{"id":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid-9","page_id":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid#the-script","content":"Let's use an example. This is a fictional plan with generic dollars. Five thousand comes in after tax. We're assuming no more business costs or tax to take out. All the required costs and payments, including money needed for later known bills, come to two thousand five hundred."},"2079":{"id":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid-10","page_id":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid#the-script","content":"If you can afford to save twenty-five percent of the full five thousand, that's one thousand two hundred and fifty. You have one thousand two hundred and fifty left to allocate after those costs and saving."},"2080":{"id":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid-11","page_id":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid#the-script","content":"That can go toward chosen spending, extra debt payments, or more money kept aside. It doesn't mean you have to use it all."},"2081":{"id":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid-12","page_id":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid#the-script","content":"And if you can't save that amount, don't force the number."},"2082":{"id":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid-13","page_id":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid#the-script","content":"Here's a smaller example. Two thousand comes in after tax. Required costs are sixteen hundred. There's four hundred left. If you choose to save a quarter of that four hundred, you save a hundred and have three hundred left to allocate."},"2083":{"id":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid-14","page_id":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid#the-script","content":"A quarter of the remainder. That's a different amount from a quarter of your whole pay. Write down which one you mean."},"2084":{"id":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid-15","page_id":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid#the-script","content":"Now, giving. I'm a Christian, and tithing is part of how I handle my money. What has helped me is to treat that ten percent as already set aside and plan with the rest. That's my faith and my practice."},"2085":{"id":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid-16","page_id":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid#the-script","content":"From five thousand take-home, that's five hundred for giving and forty-five hundred to plan with. In this separate example, say the required costs are still twenty-five hundred. If you save twenty-five percent of the forty-five hundred after giving, that's eleven hundred and twenty-five. You have eight hundred and seventy-five left to allocate."},"2086":{"id":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid-17","page_id":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid#the-script","content":"The chapter has the full table. The point is to do the math again. You can't add giving to the old plan and leave every other amount where it was. And a percentage doesn't fix a shortage."},"2087":{"id":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid-18","page_id":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid#the-script","content":"The dates matter too. If your money comes in twice a month but most of your bills come out after the first payment, splitting everything exactly in half may leave you short. Put the receipts and bills on a calendar. Keep a running balance. Future money stays marked as expected until it arrives."},"2088":{"id":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid-19","page_id":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid#the-script","content":"If the dates don't fit, ask the provider about your options. A request isn't a confirmed change. Find out what you still need to pay and when."},"2089":{"id":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid-20","page_id":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid#the-script","content":"Now, what if there just isn't enough?"},"2090":{"id":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid-21","page_id":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid#the-script","content":"I'm not going to pretend I've always been able to cover everything. Sometimes you can't."},"2091":{"id":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid-22","page_id":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid#the-script","content":"Write down the gap. If you have twelve hundred available and fifteen hundred in needs and required payments, you're three hundred short. There's no new saving or optional spending in that example. We can't make the gap disappear by putting it in a different column."},"2092":{"id":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid-23","page_id":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid#the-script","content":"Look at what could happen if each bill is late. What protects your basic needs, housing, and ability to earn? Who do you need to contact? What help can you check? Use the actual dates and consequences in your situation. My priority list won't be exactly the same as yours."},"2093":{"id":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid-24","page_id":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid#the-script","content":"Record any arrangement that's confirmed. Keep the unpaid amount visible. If nothing has changed, the gap is still there."},"2094":{"id":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid-25","page_id":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid#the-script","content":"Use workbook pages ten to twelve, or plain paper. Make the payment plan. Put it on a calendar. Use the shortfall page if you need it."},"2095":{"id":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid-26","page_id":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid#the-script","content":"And when payday comes, check what actually arrived. Then use the plan. Change it when the facts change."},"2096":{"id":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid-27","page_id":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid#exercise-and-source-notes","content":"The full chapter exercise covers current versus expected money, costs and tax, date checks, transfers, and all four reader paths. Workbook pages: 10. My next payment plan, 11. My bill calendar, 12. When money falls short."},"2097":{"id":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid-28","page_id":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/scripts/when-you-get-paid#exercise-and-source-notes","content":"Voice and personal passages come from Note 9, 00:00–18:42. The shortfall and completed calculations use the same labeled assumptions as Chapter 4. Giving is presented as the author's practice without a financial-return promise. Chapter 4 research records the supporting sources."},"2098":{"id":"/docs/topics/big-purchases","page_id":"/docs/topics/big-purchases","type":"page","content":"Buying something expensive","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Topic notes"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/topics/big-purchases"},"2099":{"id":"/docs/topics/big-purchases-0","page_id":"/docs/topics/big-purchases","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/big-purchases","content":"Build a process for deciding whether a purchase fits."},"2100":{"id":"/docs/topics/big-purchases-1","page_id":"/docs/topics/big-purchases","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/topics/big-purchases#the-readers-question","content":"The reader's question"},"2101":{"id":"/docs/topics/big-purchases-2","page_id":"/docs/topics/big-purchases","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/topics/big-purchases#exercise-to-build","content":"Exercise to build"},"2102":{"id":"/docs/topics/big-purchases-3","page_id":"/docs/topics/big-purchases","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/big-purchases","content":"**Chapter destination:** 8. Before you buy. The destination chapter now has a first draft and a spoken adaptation. These remain supporting source notes; the chapter holds the developed exercise and scoped research."},"2103":{"id":"/docs/topics/big-purchases-4","page_id":"/docs/topics/big-purchases","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/big-purchases#the-readers-question","content":"How do I decide whether to buy something expensive?"},"2104":{"id":"/docs/topics/big-purchases-5","page_id":"/docs/topics/big-purchases","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/big-purchases#the-readers-question","content":"The book should give the reader a process they can repeat. It needs to connect the purchase to what they want from life, what it costs, and their current income and debt."},"2105":{"id":"/docs/topics/big-purchases-6","page_id":"/docs/topics/big-purchases","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/big-purchases#exercise-to-build","content":"Ask the reader to name the purchase and explain what it adds to their life. Then build a worked comparison of the available choices and their full costs. The final result should be a decision with reasons."},"2106":{"id":"/docs/topics/big-purchases-7","page_id":"/docs/topics/big-purchases","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/big-purchases#exercise-to-build","content":"Use the life you want exercise for context. Put financing details in Debt 101 and Buy now, pay later."},"2107":{"id":"/docs/topics/big-purchases-8","page_id":"/docs/topics/big-purchases","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/big-purchases#exercise-to-build","content":"Sources: Note 3 and General plan."},"2108":{"id":"/docs/topics/bnpl","page_id":"/docs/topics/bnpl","type":"page","content":"Buy now, pay later","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Topic notes"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/topics/bnpl"},"2109":{"id":"/docs/topics/bnpl-0","page_id":"/docs/topics/bnpl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/bnpl","content":"Research and explain the decision to use a BNPL app."},"2110":{"id":"/docs/topics/bnpl-1","page_id":"/docs/topics/bnpl","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/topics/bnpl#the-readers-question","content":"The reader's question"},"2111":{"id":"/docs/topics/bnpl-2","page_id":"/docs/topics/bnpl","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/topics/bnpl#what-the-chapter-needs","content":"What the chapter needs"},"2112":{"id":"/docs/topics/bnpl-3","page_id":"/docs/topics/bnpl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/bnpl","content":"**Chapter destination:** 8. Before you buy. The destination chapter now has a first draft and a spoken adaptation. These remain supporting source notes; the chapter holds the developed exercise and scoped research."},"2113":{"id":"/docs/topics/bnpl-4","page_id":"/docs/topics/bnpl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/bnpl#the-readers-question","content":"Should I use a buy-now-pay-later app?"},"2114":{"id":"/docs/topics/bnpl-5","page_id":"/docs/topics/bnpl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/bnpl#the-readers-question","content":"This is a topic from the general plan. There is no developed answer in the current recordings."},"2115":{"id":"/docs/topics/bnpl-6","page_id":"/docs/topics/bnpl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/bnpl#what-the-chapter-needs","content":"Explain how a chosen service works using its current terms. Compare the payment schedule, fees, consequences of missed payments, and any relevant credit reporting. Those details can differ by provider and country."},"2116":{"id":"/docs/topics/bnpl-7","page_id":"/docs/topics/bnpl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/bnpl#what-the-chapter-needs","content":"Build a purchase example that connects to the reader's income and existing payments. Give the reader a decision exercise and link it to Buying something expensive."},"2117":{"id":"/docs/topics/bnpl-8","page_id":"/docs/topics/bnpl","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/bnpl#what-the-chapter-needs","content":"Record sources and the date checked in Research notes."},"2118":{"id":"/docs/topics/budgeting-apps","page_id":"/docs/topics/budgeting-apps","type":"page","content":"Budgeting apps","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Topic notes"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/topics/budgeting-apps"},"2119":{"id":"/docs/topics/budgeting-apps-0","page_id":"/docs/topics/budgeting-apps","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/budgeting-apps","content":"Examine the author's view that an app alone does not solve the problem."},"2120":{"id":"/docs/topics/budgeting-apps-1","page_id":"/docs/topics/budgeting-apps","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/topics/budgeting-apps#working-angle","content":"Working angle"},"2121":{"id":"/docs/topics/budgeting-apps-2","page_id":"/docs/topics/budgeting-apps","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/topics/budgeting-apps#material-to-draw-from","content":"Material to draw from"},"2122":{"id":"/docs/topics/budgeting-apps-3","page_id":"/docs/topics/budgeting-apps","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/topics/budgeting-apps#what-the-reader-should-leave-with","content":"What the reader should leave with"},"2123":{"id":"/docs/topics/budgeting-apps-4","page_id":"/docs/topics/budgeting-apps","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/budgeting-apps","content":"**Chapter destination:** 4. When you get paid. These are supporting notes for the manuscript."},"2124":{"id":"/docs/topics/budgeting-apps-5","page_id":"/docs/topics/budgeting-apps","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/budgeting-apps#working-angle","content":"The written plan says, “Why budgeting apps don't really help you.”"},"2125":{"id":"/docs/topics/budgeting-apps-6","page_id":"/docs/topics/budgeting-apps","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/budgeting-apps#working-angle","content":"Chapter 4's tool section develops a narrower practical question: does the tool help the reader see current money, future commitments, and dates? It does not claim budgeting apps generally fail. Specific app comparisons would need current product terms and evidence."},"2126":{"id":"/docs/topics/budgeting-apps-7","page_id":"/docs/topics/budgeting-apps","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/budgeting-apps#material-to-draw-from","content":"In Note 1, the author says he has worked at fintech companies and built financial apps. That experience can explain how he sees these tools. It does not by itself establish how well every budgeting app works."},"2127":{"id":"/docs/topics/budgeting-apps-8","page_id":"/docs/topics/budgeting-apps","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/budgeting-apps#material-to-draw-from","content":"Note 3 asks when someone needs a budget and how to set one."},"2128":{"id":"/docs/topics/budgeting-apps-9","page_id":"/docs/topics/budgeting-apps","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/budgeting-apps#what-the-reader-should-leave-with","content":"A way to identify the help they need and a practical process they can follow. Add examples that show where a tool helps and where the reader still has to make a decision."},"2129":{"id":"/docs/topics/budgeting-apps-10","page_id":"/docs/topics/budgeting-apps","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/budgeting-apps#what-the-reader-should-leave-with","content":"Source plan: General plan."},"2130":{"id":"/docs/topics/credit-cards","page_id":"/docs/topics/credit-cards","type":"page","content":"Credit cards 101","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Topic notes"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/topics/credit-cards"},"2131":{"id":"/docs/topics/credit-cards-0","page_id":"/docs/topics/credit-cards","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/credit-cards","content":"Explain interest, minimum payments, and the tradeoffs of using a card."},"2132":{"id":"/docs/topics/credit-cards-1","page_id":"/docs/topics/credit-cards","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/topics/credit-cards#the-readers-question","content":"The reader's question"},"2133":{"id":"/docs/topics/credit-cards-2","page_id":"/docs/topics/credit-cards","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/topics/credit-cards#what-to-cover","content":"What to cover"},"2134":{"id":"/docs/topics/credit-cards-3","page_id":"/docs/topics/credit-cards","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/topics/credit-cards#exercise-to-build","content":"Exercise to build"},"2135":{"id":"/docs/topics/credit-cards-4","page_id":"/docs/topics/credit-cards","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/credit-cards","content":"**Chapter destination:** 6. Credit cards 101. The destination chapter now has a first draft and a spoken adaptation. These remain supporting source notes; the chapter holds the developed exercise and scoped research."},"2136":{"id":"/docs/topics/credit-cards-5","page_id":"/docs/topics/credit-cards","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/credit-cards#the-readers-question","content":"How does a credit card work, and what can go wrong?"},"2137":{"id":"/docs/topics/credit-cards-6","page_id":"/docs/topics/credit-cards","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/credit-cards#the-readers-question","content":"The plan calls credit cards a double-edged sword. The chapter needs to explain the useful parts and the risks with a worked example."},"2138":{"id":"/docs/topics/credit-cards-7","page_id":"/docs/topics/credit-cards","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/credit-cards#what-to-cover","content":"Interest and the meaning of an annual interest rate."},"2139":{"id":"/docs/topics/credit-cards-8","page_id":"/docs/topics/credit-cards","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/credit-cards#what-to-cover","content":"Minimum payments and what they do to the balance."},"2140":{"id":"/docs/topics/credit-cards-9","page_id":"/docs/topics/credit-cards","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/credit-cards#what-to-cover","content":"The difference between using credit and carrying debt."},"2141":{"id":"/docs/topics/credit-cards-10","page_id":"/docs/topics/credit-cards","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/credit-cards#what-to-cover","content":"When the author would use a credit card or a debit card."},"2142":{"id":"/docs/topics/credit-cards-11","page_id":"/docs/topics/credit-cards","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/credit-cards#what-to-cover","content":"Use an actual card agreement or official guidance for the selected country. Define the example's rate, fees, payment timing, and assumptions."},"2143":{"id":"/docs/topics/credit-cards-12","page_id":"/docs/topics/credit-cards","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/credit-cards#exercise-to-build","content":"Have the reader find the relevant numbers on their own statement, then walk through a payment decision. Add notes for I-D and N-D where the situation changes the next step."},"2144":{"id":"/docs/topics/credit-cards-13","page_id":"/docs/topics/credit-cards","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/credit-cards#exercise-to-build","content":"Sources: Note 3 and General plan."},"2145":{"id":"/docs/topics/credit-scores","page_id":"/docs/topics/credit-scores","type":"page","content":"Credit scores 101","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Topic notes"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/topics/credit-scores"},"2146":{"id":"/docs/topics/credit-scores-0","page_id":"/docs/topics/credit-scores","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/credit-scores","content":"Explain what a credit score is and when it matters."},"2147":{"id":"/docs/topics/credit-scores-1","page_id":"/docs/topics/credit-scores","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/topics/credit-scores#questions-from-the-plan","content":"Questions from the plan"},"2148":{"id":"/docs/topics/credit-scores-2","page_id":"/docs/topics/credit-scores","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/topics/credit-scores#research-before-drafting","content":"Research before drafting"},"2149":{"id":"/docs/topics/credit-scores-3","page_id":"/docs/topics/credit-scores","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/topics/credit-scores#exercise-to-build","content":"Exercise to build"},"2150":{"id":"/docs/topics/credit-scores-4","page_id":"/docs/topics/credit-scores","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/credit-scores","content":"**Chapter destination:** 7. Credit scores 101. The destination chapter now has a first draft and a spoken adaptation. These remain supporting source notes; the chapter holds the developed exercise and scoped research."},"2151":{"id":"/docs/topics/credit-scores-5","page_id":"/docs/topics/credit-scores","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/credit-scores#questions-from-the-plan","content":"What is a credit score?"},"2152":{"id":"/docs/topics/credit-scores-6","page_id":"/docs/topics/credit-scores","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/credit-scores#questions-from-the-plan","content":"When was it created?"},"2153":{"id":"/docs/topics/credit-scores-7","page_id":"/docs/topics/credit-scores","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/credit-scores#questions-from-the-plan","content":"Does it matter, and in which situations?"},"2154":{"id":"/docs/topics/credit-scores-8","page_id":"/docs/topics/credit-scores","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/credit-scores#research-before-drafting","content":"Choose the country and scoring system before writing the history or explaining the factors. Different systems should not be treated as one universal score."},"2155":{"id":"/docs/topics/credit-scores-9","page_id":"/docs/topics/credit-scores","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/credit-scores#research-before-drafting","content":"Use primary sources to explain where a score is used and what the reader can learn from it. Keep score questions distinct from the credit card and debt explanations."},"2156":{"id":"/docs/topics/credit-scores-10","page_id":"/docs/topics/credit-scores","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/credit-scores#exercise-to-build","content":"Create a task that helps the reader understand their own credit information. The exact steps and links depend on the selected country."},"2157":{"id":"/docs/topics/credit-scores-11","page_id":"/docs/topics/credit-scores","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/credit-scores#exercise-to-build","content":"Source: General plan."},"2158":{"id":"/docs/topics/debt","page_id":"/docs/topics/debt","type":"page","content":"Debt 101","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Topic notes"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/topics/debt"},"2159":{"id":"/docs/topics/debt-0","page_id":"/docs/topics/debt","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/debt","content":"Plan the guide to understanding, managing, and getting out of debt."},"2160":{"id":"/docs/topics/debt-1","page_id":"/docs/topics/debt","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/topics/debt#what-to-cover","content":"What to cover"},"2161":{"id":"/docs/topics/debt-2","page_id":"/docs/topics/debt","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/topics/debt#build-around-the-starting-situation","content":"Build around the starting situation"},"2162":{"id":"/docs/topics/debt-3","page_id":"/docs/topics/debt","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/topics/debt#story-and-exercise","content":"Story and exercise"},"2163":{"id":"/docs/topics/debt-4","page_id":"/docs/topics/debt","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/debt","content":"**Chapter destination:** 5. Make a plan for debt. The destination chapter now has a first draft and a spoken adaptation. These remain supporting source notes; the chapter holds the developed exercise and scoped research."},"2164":{"id":"/docs/topics/debt-5","page_id":"/docs/topics/debt","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/debt#what-to-cover","content":"The written plan asks how to get out of debt, avoid new debt, control debt, and balance payments."},"2165":{"id":"/docs/topics/debt-6","page_id":"/docs/topics/debt","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/debt#what-to-cover","content":"Note 3 adds questions about collections, debt collectors, tax bills, loans, and lines of credit. These are planned sections of Chapter 5, alongside borrowing from and lending to friends."},"2166":{"id":"/docs/topics/debt-7","page_id":"/docs/topics/debt","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/debt#build-around-the-starting-situation","content":"I-D and N-D need distinct guidance. In the recording, the author puts finding income first for someone who has none. That idea needs a researched sequence which also accounts for bills and obligations that cannot wait."},"2167":{"id":"/docs/topics/debt-8","page_id":"/docs/topics/debt","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/debt#build-around-the-starting-situation","content":"The author defines debt as money owed. The Chapter 1 map includes borrowed money, unpaid bills, and taxes, fines, and court-ordered amounts. The author's target is income without debt; the repayment sequence still needs evidence and context."},"2168":{"id":"/docs/topics/debt-9","page_id":"/docs/topics/debt","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/debt#story-and-exercise","content":"The author's experience of living on credit after losing a job is source material for this section. Keep it tied to Note 3."},"2169":{"id":"/docs/topics/debt-10","page_id":"/docs/topics/debt","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/debt#story-and-exercise","content":"The first exercise can ask the reader to list each debt, balance, rate, payment, and due date. The later steps for choosing a repayment order need evidence and worked examples."},"2170":{"id":"/docs/topics/debt-11","page_id":"/docs/topics/debt","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/debt#story-and-exercise","content":"Source plan: General plan."},"2171":{"id":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle","page_id":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle","type":"page","content":"The life you want","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Topic notes"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle"},"2172":{"id":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle-0","page_id":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle","content":"A draft exercise to work out what the reader's chosen life costs."},"2173":{"id":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle-1","page_id":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle#start-with-a-day","content":"Start with a day"},"2174":{"id":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle-2","page_id":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle#put-a-cost-on-it","content":"Put a cost on it"},"2175":{"id":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle-3","page_id":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle#compare-it-with-where-you-are","content":"Compare it with where you are"},"2176":{"id":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle-4","page_id":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle#why-this-sits-near-the-start","content":"Why this sits near the start"},"2177":{"id":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle-5","page_id":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle","content":"**Chapter destination:** 2. The life you want. These are supporting notes for the manuscript."},"2178":{"id":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle-6","page_id":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle","content":"Early source exercise based on Note 3, retained for reference. The developed version is now in Chapter 2, with a worked example, research notes, and workbook pages 5–7. Use those pages for the current exercise."},"2179":{"id":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle-7","page_id":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle#start-with-a-day","content":"Think about the life you want. Get specific."},"2180":{"id":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle-8","page_id":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle#start-with-a-day","content":"What time do you wake up? Where do you live? What work do you do? Think about your family, your home, your travel, and the things you choose to spend money on."},"2181":{"id":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle-9","page_id":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle#start-with-a-day","content":"You might want to get up at 5:30, go to the gym, and take your kids to school. You might want to do your job and get home in time for dinner. That is one of the lives described in the recording. Yours can look different."},"2182":{"id":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle-10","page_id":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle#start-with-a-day","content":"Write out your own day before you put a number on it."},"2183":{"id":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle-11","page_id":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle#put-a-cost-on-it","content":"Use your own estimates in one currency. Worked examples will use generic $ amounts. Note which costs you have checked and which are still guesses."},"2184":{"id":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle-12","page_id":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle#put-a-cost-on-it","content":"Part of your life"},"2185":{"id":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle-13","page_id":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle#put-a-cost-on-it","content":"What you want"},"2186":{"id":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle-14","page_id":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle#put-a-cost-on-it","content":"Estimated monthly cost"},"2187":{"id":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle-15","page_id":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle#put-a-cost-on-it","content":"Where the estimate comes from"},"2188":{"id":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle-16","page_id":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle#put-a-cost-on-it","content":"Home"},"2189":{"id":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle-17","page_id":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle#put-a-cost-on-it","content":"Food and household costs"},"2190":{"id":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle-18","page_id":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle#put-a-cost-on-it","content":"Getting around"},"2191":{"id":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle-19","page_id":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle#put-a-cost-on-it","content":"Family and care"},"2192":{"id":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle-20","page_id":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle#put-a-cost-on-it","content":"Health"},"2193":{"id":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle-21","page_id":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle#put-a-cost-on-it","content":"Travel and time off"},"2194":{"id":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle-22","page_id":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle#put-a-cost-on-it","content":"Clothes, hobbies, and other choices"},"2195":{"id":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle-23","page_id":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle#put-a-cost-on-it","content":"Other costs from your own list"},"2196":{"id":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle-24","page_id":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle#put-a-cost-on-it","content":"For a cost that happens once a year, record that annual amount and its monthly share. Keep one-time purchases on a separate list so you can see what is recurring."},"2197":{"id":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle-25","page_id":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle#compare-it-with-where-you-are","content":"Write down your current income and debt beside your estimate. Identify your reader path."},"2198":{"id":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle-26","page_id":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle#compare-it-with-where-you-are","content":"The gap gives the rest of the workbook a job. It helps you see which questions to work on: earning income, changing expenses, handling debt, or revisiting the life you described."},"2199":{"id":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle-27","page_id":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle#compare-it-with-where-you-are","content":"This early worksheet is not a complete income target. The developed Chapter 2 exercise accounts for saving, payments, costs or tax still to cover, and one-time goals without counting them twice. Unknowns keep the comparison provisional."},"2200":{"id":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle-28","page_id":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle#why-this-sits-near-the-start","content":"In Note 3, the author first talks about maximizing income and reducing debt. He then corrects himself: the larger goal is to define the life you want and calculate what it takes."},"2201":{"id":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle-29","page_id":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle#why-this-sits-near-the-start","content":"The book plan follows that correction. The exercise gives the reader a direction; their current situation determines the next step."},"2202":{"id":"/docs/topics/get-rich-quick","page_id":"/docs/topics/get-rich-quick","type":"page","content":"Get-rich-quick schemes","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Topic notes"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/topics/get-rich-quick"},"2203":{"id":"/docs/topics/get-rich-quick-0","page_id":"/docs/topics/get-rich-quick","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/get-rich-quick","content":"Develop the chapter on financial shortcuts and the promises behind them."},"2204":{"id":"/docs/topics/get-rich-quick-1","page_id":"/docs/topics/get-rich-quick","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/topics/get-rich-quick#working-angle","content":"Working angle"},"2205":{"id":"/docs/topics/get-rich-quick-2","page_id":"/docs/topics/get-rich-quick","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/topics/get-rich-quick#connection-to-the-book","content":"Connection to the book"},"2206":{"id":"/docs/topics/get-rich-quick-3","page_id":"/docs/topics/get-rich-quick","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/topics/get-rich-quick#exercise-to-build","content":"Exercise to build"},"2207":{"id":"/docs/topics/get-rich-quick-4","page_id":"/docs/topics/get-rich-quick","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/get-rich-quick","content":"**Chapter destination:** 10. The promise of fast money. These are supporting notes for the manuscript."},"2208":{"id":"/docs/topics/get-rich-quick-5","page_id":"/docs/topics/get-rich-quick","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/get-rich-quick#working-angle","content":"The general plan calls for explaining why get-rich-quick schemes do not work."},"2209":{"id":"/docs/topics/get-rich-quick-6","page_id":"/docs/topics/get-rich-quick","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/get-rich-quick#working-angle","content":"Choose specific examples and explain what is being promised, where the money comes from, and what evidence supports the outcome being advertised. A broad claim needs a clear definition of the schemes covered."},"2210":{"id":"/docs/topics/get-rich-quick-7","page_id":"/docs/topics/get-rich-quick","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/get-rich-quick#connection-to-the-book","content":"Notes 1 and 2 explain that the author is not promising to make every reader a millionaire. This section can connect that stance to the reader's own goals and the work needed to reach them."},"2211":{"id":"/docs/topics/get-rich-quick-8","page_id":"/docs/topics/get-rich-quick","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/get-rich-quick#exercise-to-build","content":"Give the reader an example offer to examine. Ask them to identify the promise, cost, assumptions, and evidence. Use sourced cases before writing the final explanation."},"2212":{"id":"/docs/topics/getting-paid","page_id":"/docs/topics/getting-paid","type":"page","content":"When you get paid","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Topic notes"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/topics/getting-paid"},"2213":{"id":"/docs/topics/getting-paid-0","page_id":"/docs/topics/getting-paid","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/getting-paid","content":"Plan the steps for deciding where a pay cheque goes."},"2214":{"id":"/docs/topics/getting-paid-1","page_id":"/docs/topics/getting-paid","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/topics/getting-paid#the-readers-question","content":"The reader's question"},"2215":{"id":"/docs/topics/getting-paid-2","page_id":"/docs/topics/getting-paid","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/topics/getting-paid#what-this-section-needs","content":"What this section needs"},"2216":{"id":"/docs/topics/getting-paid-3","page_id":"/docs/topics/getting-paid","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/topics/getting-paid#source-material","content":"Source material"},"2217":{"id":"/docs/topics/getting-paid-4","page_id":"/docs/topics/getting-paid","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/getting-paid","content":"**Chapter destination:** 4. When you get paid. The chapter now has a first draft, a spoken adaptation, and workbook pages 10–12. These remain supporting source notes."},"2218":{"id":"/docs/topics/getting-paid-5","page_id":"/docs/topics/getting-paid","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/getting-paid#the-readers-question","content":"What should I do when I get paid?"},"2219":{"id":"/docs/topics/getting-paid-6","page_id":"/docs/topics/getting-paid","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/getting-paid#the-readers-question","content":"The written plan calls for a practical way to split a pay cheque. Note 3 also asks how much to save and how giving to church fits."},"2220":{"id":"/docs/topics/getting-paid-7","page_id":"/docs/topics/getting-paid","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/getting-paid#what-this-section-needs","content":"A worked example should show the decisions in order. It should explain which steps change when the reader has debt, earns uneven amounts, or runs a business. Avoid giving everyone the same split before those differences have been researched."},"2221":{"id":"/docs/topics/getting-paid-8","page_id":"/docs/topics/getting-paid","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/getting-paid#what-this-section-needs","content":"The developed exercise ends with a plan for the next payment, a dated calendar, and a shortfall sheet. Chapter 4 research records the verified guidance and settled example assumptions."},"2222":{"id":"/docs/topics/getting-paid-9","page_id":"/docs/topics/getting-paid","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/getting-paid#source-material","content":"Note 9, 00:00–18:42, now supplies the main payday prose: plan mandatory bills first, choose affordable saving, preserve the author's giving practice, and face shortfalls."},"2223":{"id":"/docs/topics/getting-paid-10","page_id":"/docs/topics/getting-paid","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/getting-paid#source-material","content":"Note 3 supplies the pay-cheque, saving, and giving questions. General plan makes this a core topic."},"2224":{"id":"/docs/topics/getting-paid-11","page_id":"/docs/topics/getting-paid","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/getting-paid#source-material","content":"The main reader paths here are I-D and I-ND. Employer, Own work, Assets, and Support / retirement labels can explain source differences. Record payment timing separately."},"2225":{"id":"/docs/topics/income","page_id":"/docs/topics/income","type":"page","content":"Growing your income","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Topic notes"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/topics/income"},"2226":{"id":"/docs/topics/income-0","page_id":"/docs/topics/income","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/income","content":"Put income growth in the context of the reader's starting point and goals."},"2227":{"id":"/docs/topics/income-1","page_id":"/docs/topics/income","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/topics/income#the-point-from-note-3","content":"The point from Note 3"},"2228":{"id":"/docs/topics/income-2","page_id":"/docs/topics/income","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/topics/income#what-to-cover","content":"What to cover"},"2229":{"id":"/docs/topics/income-3","page_id":"/docs/topics/income","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/topics/income#story-to-develop","content":"Story to develop"},"2230":{"id":"/docs/topics/income-4","page_id":"/docs/topics/income","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/income","content":"**Chapter destination:** 3. Find and grow your income. The chapter now has a first draft, a spoken adaptation, and workbook pages 8 and 9. These remain supporting source notes."},"2231":{"id":"/docs/topics/income-5","page_id":"/docs/topics/income","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/income#the-point-from-note-3","content":"The written plan says increasing monthly income is the number-one priority. In the recording, that priority depends on where the reader starts."},"2232":{"id":"/docs/topics/income-6","page_id":"/docs/topics/income","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/income#the-point-from-note-3","content":"For someone with no income, the author puts getting income first. For someone who already has income, he connects earning more to the life they want and the skills they are willing to learn."},"2233":{"id":"/docs/topics/income-7","page_id":"/docs/topics/income","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/income#the-point-from-note-3","content":"The author then corrects the larger goal: work out the life you want and what it costs. That gives the income discussion a purpose."},"2234":{"id":"/docs/topics/income-8","page_id":"/docs/topics/income","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/income#what-to-cover","content":"Finding income when the reader has none."},"2235":{"id":"/docs/topics/income-9","page_id":"/docs/topics/income","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/income#what-to-cover","content":"Ways to grow income from hourly work, a salary, or a business."},"2236":{"id":"/docs/topics/income-10","page_id":"/docs/topics/income","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/income#what-to-cover","content":"The skills, time, and tradeoffs involved in each option."},"2237":{"id":"/docs/topics/income-11","page_id":"/docs/topics/income","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/income#what-to-cover","content":"Why a business or a full-time job will not suit everyone."},"2238":{"id":"/docs/topics/income-12","page_id":"/docs/topics/income","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/income#what-to-cover","content":"How this connects to the reader's own lifestyle calculation."},"2239":{"id":"/docs/topics/income-13","page_id":"/docs/topics/income","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/income#what-to-cover","content":"The first chapter draft develops these steps, including a fictional editing test and the limits around work, costs, support, and payment timing. Research notes record the primary-source checks. Actual pay, hiring, business results, and support eligibility remain specific to the reader."},"2240":{"id":"/docs/topics/income-14","page_id":"/docs/topics/income","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/income#story-to-develop","content":"Note 3 describes losing a job during a honeymoon, moving to New York, and facing higher bills while carrying debt. It can ground the no-income path. Confirm the timeline and figures before using the full story."},"2241":{"id":"/docs/topics/income-15","page_id":"/docs/topics/income","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics/income#story-to-develop","content":"Sources: Note 3 and General plan."},"2242":{"id":"/docs/topics","page_id":"/docs/topics","type":"page","content":"Topic notes","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Topic notes"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/topics"},"2243":{"id":"/docs/topics-0","page_id":"/docs/topics","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics","content":"Supporting ideas and exercises that feed the numbered chapters."},"2244":{"id":"/docs/topics-1","page_id":"/docs/topics","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics","content":"These are working notes behind the 12 chapters. Each page links to its chapter destination. The book plan places smaller questions inside those chapters."},"2245":{"id":"/docs/topics-2","page_id":"/docs/topics","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics","content":"Most pages are writing briefs. The life-you-want page includes a draft exercise drawn from Note 3. Factual chapters need sources and worked examples before they are ready for readers."},"2246":{"id":"/docs/topics-3","page_id":"/docs/topics","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics","content":"<Card title=\"The life you want\" href=\"/docs/topics/dream-lifestyle\" description=\"Work out what it costs.\" />"},"2247":{"id":"/docs/topics-4","page_id":"/docs/topics","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics","content":"<Card title=\"Growing your income\" href=\"/docs/topics/income\" description=\"Find the next income step for your situation.\" />"},"2248":{"id":"/docs/topics-5","page_id":"/docs/topics","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics","content":"<Card title=\"When you get paid\" href=\"/docs/topics/getting-paid\" description=\"Decide where your pay cheque goes.\" />"},"2249":{"id":"/docs/topics-6","page_id":"/docs/topics","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics","content":"<Card title=\"Debt 101\" href=\"/docs/topics/debt\" description=\"Understand and manage what you owe.\" />"},"2250":{"id":"/docs/topics-7","page_id":"/docs/topics","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics","content":"<Card title=\"Credit cards 101\" href=\"/docs/topics/credit-cards\" description=\"Interest, minimum payments, benefits, and risks.\" />"},"2251":{"id":"/docs/topics-8","page_id":"/docs/topics","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics","content":"<Card title=\"Credit scores 101\" href=\"/docs/topics/credit-scores\" description=\"What they mean and when they matter.\" />"},"2252":{"id":"/docs/topics-9","page_id":"/docs/topics","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/topics","content":"<Card title=\"Buying some