"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#where-each-recording-goes","content":"Chapter 6; borrowing comparisons in Chapter 5; payment dates link to Chapter 4."},"3132":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-34","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#where-each-recording-goes","content":"Note 7, 06:47–07:45 and 09:20–10:33"},"3133":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-35","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#where-each-recording-goes","content":"Credit scores, reports, applications, balances, and the correction from Vanguard to Equifax."},"3134":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-36","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#where-each-recording-goes","content":"Chapter 7. Research the terms before drafting the explanation."},"3135":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-37","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#where-each-recording-goes","content":"Note 8, plus Note 4 at 21:15–end and Note 7 at 09:42–10:13"},"3136":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-38","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#where-each-recording-goes","content":"Possible online exercises, reminders, QR links, and a page-specific email question service."},"3137":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-39","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#where-each-recording-goes","content":"Open companion/support ideas below. These are tentative, not existing services."},"3138":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-40","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#where-each-recording-goes","content":"Note 9, 00:00–18:42"},"3139":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-41","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#where-each-recording-goes","content":"Plan bills before payday; build a saving habit where affordable; the author's tithing practice; prioritize when money falls short."},"3140":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-42","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#where-each-recording-goes","content":"Chapter 4, with links to Chapters 5 and 9 for debt and changes in income."},"3141":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-43","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#where-each-recording-goes","content":"Note 9, 18:42–end"},"3142":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-44","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#where-each-recording-goes","content":"The author's “afford it twice” rule, spending expected income, BNPL, and cars or other purchases that affect quality of life."},"3143":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-45","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#where-each-recording-goes","content":"Chapter 8; link back to Chapter 4's payment plan and Chapter 6's card explanation."},"3144":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-46","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#draft-sequence-and-completion","content":"**Progress, October 1:** All five steps below have first drafts. Raw Notes 4–9 remain intact. Each developed chapter has a spoken adaptation, a plain-paper exercise, companion pages, and scoped primary-source checks in Research notes."},"3145":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-47","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#draft-sequence-and-completion","content":"Chapter"},"3146":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-48","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#draft-sequence-and-completion","content":"Draft and script"},"3147":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-49","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#draft-sequence-and-completion","content":"Workbook pages"},"3148":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-50","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#draft-sequence-and-completion","content":"2"},"3149":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-51","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#draft-sequence-and-completion","content":"The life you want · script"},"3150":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-52","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#draft-sequence-and-completion","content":"5–7"},"3151":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-53","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#draft-sequence-and-completion","content":"3"},"3152":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-54","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#draft-sequence-and-completion","content":"Find and grow your income · script"},"3153":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-55","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#draft-sequence-and-completion","content":"8–9"},"3154":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-56","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#draft-sequence-and-completion","content":"4"},"3155":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-57","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#draft-sequence-and-completion","content":"When you get paid · script"},"3156":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-58","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#draft-sequence-and-completion","content":"10–12"},"3157":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-59","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#draft-sequence-and-completion","content":"5"},"3158":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-60","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#draft-sequence-and-completion","content":"Make a plan for debt · script"},"3159":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-61","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#draft-sequence-and-completion","content":"13–14"},"3160":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-62","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#draft-sequence-and-completion","content":"6"},"3161":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-63","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#draft-sequence-and-completion","content":"Credit cards 101 · script"},"3162":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-64","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#draft-sequence-and-completion","content":"15–16"},"3163":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-65","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#draft-sequence-and-completion","content":"7"},"3164":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-66","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#draft-sequence-and-completion","content":"Credit scores 101 · script"},"3165":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-67","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#draft-sequence-and-completion","content":"17"},"3166":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-68","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#draft-sequence-and-completion","content":"8"},"3167":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-69","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#draft-sequence-and-completion","content":"Before you buy · script"},"3168":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-70","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#draft-sequence-and-completion","content":"18"},"3169":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-71","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#draft-sequence-and-completion","content":"The October 1 drafting pass developed Chapters 4–8 and checked their sources, examples, scripts, and workbook instructions together. This completes the recording-incorporation draft plan, not the entire 12-chapter manuscript. Chapters 9–12 remain planned. Author review, unresolved source-wording checks if those passages are later used, and final print layout remain. The site stays local."},"3170":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-72","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#draft-sequence-and-completion","content":"Validation: the integrated local build passed. All 550 internal links and anchors, workbook numbering 1–18, and the new worked calculations passed checks. No publication or CI run was triggered; the existing print PDFs still cover only Chapter 1 and workbook pages 1–4."},"3171":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-73","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#draft-sequence-and-completion","content":"**Chapter 2 — Know what you want.** Start with “What was your plan?” Connect the current money picture to a detailed future day. Develop one settled version of Michelle as an explicitly fictional example. Price the whole household plan, state what a partner covers, and separate monthly costs from one-time purchases. End with the gap between today's situation and the target. Exercise: desired day, costs, assumptions, and target date."},"3172":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-74","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#draft-sequence-and-completion","content":"**Chapter 3 — Get income now; choose a route for later.** Keep Notes 5–6's urgency and Note 5's care for students and stay-at-home parents. Fit the steps to the existing no-income paths and support sources; account for ability, care duties, work costs, and access. Then use the vehicle analogy and the author's founder-versus-product-work reflection. Compare possible routes without promising that writing, posting, or building an app guarantees earnings. End with one action, its cost, and a review date. Exercise: current options, required skills, constraints, and a next step."},"3173":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-75","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#draft-sequence-and-completion","content":"**Chapter 4 — Make a plan before the money arrives.** Follow Note 9's own correction at 03:12–03:26: list mandatory bills before choosing a saving amount. Build a payment calendar, then show an affordable saving choice and room for chosen spending. Keep the tithing passage in the author's first-person faith perspective, with its own example. Add a separate shortfall case so the saving percentages never become an instruction to miss essential payments. Exercise: take-home income, commitments, due dates, chosen saving/giving, and the remainder."},"3174":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-76","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#draft-sequence-and-completion","content":"**Chapters 5–7 — Understand the bill and the borrowing.** Explain the terms before using the author's rewards and spending examples. Chapter 5 owns borrowing comparisons, including overdrafts and lines of credit. Chapter 6 owns card costs, statements, minimum payments, and the payment decision. Chapter 7 owns scores and reports. Proposed script sequence: what you borrowed → what it costs → what is due → what gets reported. Exercise: a card/borrowing record with balance labels, rate, fees, minimum due, and dates."},"3175":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-77","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#draft-sequence-and-completion","content":"**Chapter 8 — Decide before you buy.** Present “afford it twice” as the author's personal rule for unplanned purchases, using his corrected $250 threshold. Keep the distinction between saved-for purchases and impulse buys. Use the $350 headphones / $700 example, then the expected freelance-payment story, BNPL obligations, and the car discussion. Label illustrative details in the freelance story rather than claiming every number and purchase was a documented event. End with a purchase decision sheet that includes existing commitments and the full cost."},"3176":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-78","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#draft-sequence-and-completion","content":"For every chapter, write the prose first, then adapt the same sourced material into a spoken script with an opening question, one example, a practical step, and an exercise. Keep the author's candor and faith. Remove filler in developed prose while retaining the full source notes. Link each developed passage to its note and relevant time range."},"3177":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-79","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#draft-sequence-and-completion","content":"Add companion-workbook pages only when these exercises are drafted. Choose the next unused page numbers then, and give the same instructions for plain paper. Pages 1–4 stay attached to Chapter 1. Pages 5–7 accompany Chapter 2; pages 8–9 accompany Chapter 3; pages 10–18 accompany Chapters 4–8."},"3178":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-80","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#example-and-claim-checks","content":"**Michelle and the income examples:** settle her fictional background; recheck the cleaning amount ($20 or $200 a week); resolve the $6,500/$600 target change; rebuild the tax, sales, and app-subscription examples. Keep gross revenue, business costs, taxes, and personal income separate. Do not carry over guaranteed audience or earnings claims."},"3179":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-81","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#example-and-claim-checks","content":"**Payday examples:** the first settled illustration is $5,000 take-home minus $2,500 bills minus $1,250 saving, leaving $1,250. The smaller-surplus example changes from $500 to $300 to $400 in speech; choose and label one set of assumptions. Specify whether a percentage applies to take-home income, income after giving, or the remainder after bills. For the tithing example, $5,000 minus $500 leaves $4,500 before bills and saving; rebuild the rest explicitly rather than reusing the original remainder."},"3180":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-82","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#example-and-claim-checks","content":"**Card and score explanations:** recheck the garbled spending rule and the soft-check/report passages. Research minimum payments versus paying the statement balance, interest and grace periods, product terms, score models, reporting, and country-specific rules. The author's Amex offer and remembered book passage are source leads, not verified current advice."},"3181":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-83","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#example-and-claim-checks","content":"**Purchases and shortfalls:** define the limits of the author's personal rules. Research full ownership costs, BNPL terms, payment-date changes, and consequences of missed payments before providing step-by-step guidance."},"3182":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-84","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#example-and-claim-checks","content":"The detailed evidence queue is in Research notes. Unclear wording remains flagged in each raw note; a second automated pass did not settle every passage."},"3183":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-85","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#open-companion-and-support-ideas","content":"Notes 4, 7, 8, and 9 explore online exercises, QR links, reminders, and an email/AI question service. Keep these in the backlog until the author chooses a format. No domain, contact address, app, or response service is promised. Keep the current optional workbook and plain-paper path. Spoken suggestions are source material, not authorization to build, publish, or send anything."},"3184":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-86","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#open-companion-and-support-ideas","content":"Note 7's opening comment about leaving debt until the end is ambiguous and may concern recording order. Keep Chapter 5 in its current place. Note 4's abandoned wife anecdote stays in the raw transcript but should not lead the developed chapter. These choices preserve the settled plan while recording the unresolved ideas."},"3185":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-87","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#checks-to-keep-for-revisions","content":"Every new passage has a source note; uncertain speech is checked before it becomes a quote."},"3186":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-88","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#checks-to-keep-for-revisions","content":"Examples reconcile, state their assumptions, and distinguish hypothetical figures from personal experience."},"3187":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-89","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#checks-to-keep-for-revisions","content":"Financial claims have current primary sources and a stated country or product scope where needed."},"3188":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-90","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#checks-to-keep-for-revisions","content":"Chapters, spoken scripts, workbook references, and plain-paper exercises agree."},"3189":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-91","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#checks-to-keep-for-revisions","content":"The author can review prose separately from the preserved raw recordings."},"3190":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-92","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#bring-the-next-recording-here","content":"Attach an audio file in the Codex chat for this project. You can talk through one topic or several. Add context if you want a specific focus."},"3191":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-93","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#bring-the-next-recording-here","content":"The assistant will transcribe the recording, save it as the next numbered note, and use the book plan to place its ideas. You do not have to name a destination for every thought."},"3192":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-94","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#what-happens-to-a-recording","content":"Save the full transcript in Raw thoughts as the next numbered note. Notes 1–9 are now saved; the next is Note 10."},"3193":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-95","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#what-happens-to-a-recording","content":"Read it for ideas, examples, stories, and claims."},"3194":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-96","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#what-happens-to-a-recording","content":"Update the relevant topic or draft chapter in the author's voice. Add or revise its companion-workbook page when an exercise needs answer space. Keep plain-paper instructions in the book."},"3195":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-97","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#what-happens-to-a-recording","content":"Link the new prose back to the source note."},"3196":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-98","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#what-happens-to-a-recording","content":"Put factual questions in Research notes."},"3197":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-99","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#what-happens-to-a-recording","content":"Report which book pages changed and what still needs clarification."},"3198":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-100","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#what-happens-to-a-recording","content":"If one recording covers several topics, its ideas can go in several pages. The full transcript stays together as the source."},"3199":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-101","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#keep-the-voice","content":"Follow the writing guide and compare new prose with Notes 1–9. The October 1 voice update applies to both chapters and spoken scripts: keep the author's questions, everyday examples, directness, admissions, and meaningful qualifications. Clean up speech while preserving its natural rhythm. Give existing drafts the same voice pass as they are revised."},"3200":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-102","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#keep-the-voice","content":"When an unclear word or a conflict could change the meaning, flag it for review. Do not silently decide what the author meant."},"3201":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-103","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#where-the-book-stands","content":"The opening and how-to-use section have first drafts. Chapter 1: Start where you are has a full first draft with money maps, examples, and exercise instructions. Its four fill-in pages are in the companion workbook. Chapter 2: The life you want now has a first draft, workbook pages 5–7, and a spoken adaptation. Chapter 3: Find and grow your income has a first draft, workbook pages 8–9, and a spoken adaptation. Chapters 4–8 now have first drafts, spoken adaptations, and workbook pages 10–18. Chapters 9–12 have placeholder pages with planned sections."},"3202":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-104","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#where-the-book-stands","content":"The topic notes hold supporting material, including the early lifestyle worksheet. Add new ideas to the relevant chapter or section and keep their source links. Smaller questions do not need a new page by default."},"3203":{"id":"/docs/writing-plan-105","page_id":"/docs/writing-plan","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-plan#where-the-book-stands","content":"The title and final promise are still open. The site stays local."},"3204":{"id":"/docs/writing-style","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","type":"page","content":"Writing guide","breadcrumbs":["Docs"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/writing-style"},"3205":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-0","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-style","content":"The author's speaking voice, drawn from Notes 1–9, adapted for the book and scripts."},"3206":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-1","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/writing-style#the-voice","content":"The voice"},"3207":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-2","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/writing-style#what-sounds-like-the-author","content":"What sounds like the author"},"3208":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-3","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/writing-style#make-it-easy-to-read","content":"Make it easy to read"},"3209":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-4","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/writing-style#keep-the-reasoning-and-personality-together","content":"Keep the reasoning and personality together"},"3210":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-5","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/writing-style#what-to-preserve-from-the-recordings","content":"What to preserve from the recordings"},"3211":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-6","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/writing-style#how-to-turn-speech-into-prose","content":"How to turn speech into prose"},"3212":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-7","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/writing-style#a-voice-example","content":"A voice example"},"3213":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-8","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/writing-style#more-examples-from-the-recordings","content":"More examples from the recordings"},"3214":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-9","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/writing-style#book-prose-and-spoken-scripts","content":"Book prose and spoken scripts"},"3215":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-10","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/writing-style#supplied-style-references","content":"Supplied style references"},"3216":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-11","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-style#the-voice","content":"Write like the author is talking to one person. Use “you” when speaking to the reader and “I” for the author's own experience. Contractions fit. Plain words fit. The tone is direct and candid, with room for warmth and a short aside."},"3217":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-12","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-style#the-voice","content":"Use Notes 1–9 as the voice reference. The author's October 1, 2026 revision direction is to make the book easy to understand and follow while sounding more like his speech. There is no school-grade reading target. Preserve his way of explaining, questioning, and reacting when cleaning up the words. The reader should hear someone working through a decision with them."},"3218":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-13","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-style#the-voice","content":"Note 1 gives the stance: “I'm also still trying to figure it out as well.” Keep that openness. Do not turn the author into someone who has solved every money problem."},"3219":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-14","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-style#the-voice","content":"Note 2 says, “We have ups and we have downs.” The author talks about money as something that changes over a person's life. He gives the reader room to start again."},"3220":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-15","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-style#the-voice","content":"Note 3 moves from an idea to a specific life: getting up, going to the gym, taking the kids to school, working, and eating with family. Keep that habit of making an idea concrete."},"3221":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-16","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-style#what-sounds-like-the-author","content":"**Start with the question someone would ask.** Note 4 asks what the plan was. Note 9 asks what happens when there isn't enough to pay the bills. Use a question to open the actual problem, then spend time answering it. Avoid adding a question to every paragraph."},"3222":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-17","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-style#what-sounds-like-the-author","content":"**Walk through it together.** “Okay, so,” “let's say,” “right?” and “now” are natural parts of the recordings. Keep a few where they move the explanation along or invite the reader to check the reasoning. Do not sprinkle them into every sentence."},"3223":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-18","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-style#what-sounds-like-the-author","content":"**Make the situation visible.** Note 4 gives Michelle a workday, children to pick up, and things she wants to afford. Note 9 uses bills, headphones, a camera, and a payment that hasn't arrived. Show the choice in someone's day before naming an abstract principle."},"3224":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-19","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-style#what-sounds-like-the-author","content":"**Say what you think.** Keep “for me,” “I think,” and “what I would do” when they distinguish personal judgment from a fact. Note 9 explicitly calls the tithing passage a personal belief. Do not edit those qualifications into universal instructions."},"3225":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-20","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-style#what-sounds-like-the-author","content":"**Keep the push and the admission.** Notes 6 and 9 can be blunt. Note 9 also admits that the author doesn't get his own purchase rule right every time. Preserve both sides of that voice. A warning should explain the decision and its consequence; do not invent insults or make every section a telling-off."},"3226":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-21","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-style#what-sounds-like-the-author","content":"**Give people room.** Note 5 respects stay-at-home parents. Note 9 acknowledges that sometimes the proposed saving amount isn't possible. Keep those conditions beside the advice they qualify, with the same weight as the forceful line."},"3227":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-22","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-style#what-sounds-like-the-author","content":"These are patterns to listen for, not a template every section must follow. Notes 4–9 are automated transcripts with flagged uncertainties. Use clear passages for voice; do not copy a transcription error as a personal mannerism."},"3228":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-23","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-style#make-it-easy-to-read","content":"Write for an adult who may be new to money topics. Assume intelligence without assuming financial knowledge. Use familiar language, explain unfamiliar terms, and give the reader enough reasoning to follow the decision. A grade score, sentence length, or comma count does not decide whether a passage is clear."},"3229":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-24","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-style#make-it-easy-to-read","content":"Use active voice and simple tenses."},"3230":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-25","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-style#make-it-easy-to-read","content":"Prefer present tense for guidance. Use past tense when a real story needs it."},"3231":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-26","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-style#make-it-easy-to-read","content":"Vary sentence length with the thought. Keep a cause, its consequence, and a useful qualification together when that makes the explanation easier to follow. Use a short sentence when it deserves emphasis. Do not break ordinary lists or connected ideas into fragments."},"3232":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-27","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-style#make-it-easy-to-read","content":"Choose everyday words. Explain financial terms when the reader first meets them."},"3233":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-28","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-style#make-it-easy-to-read","content":"Cut empty intensifiers and repeated filler. Keep a word such as “honestly” or “actually” when it carries the author's reaction or a meaningful correction."},"3234":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-29","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-style#make-it-easy-to-read","content":"Give each paragraph one main idea and enough development to make it useful. Connect it to the paragraph before it; avoid a sequence of detached tips."},"3235":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-30","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-style#make-it-easy-to-read","content":"Explain why a step matters before asking the reader to do it. Keep numbered steps for exercises and sequences, and tables for comparisons and calculations."},"3236":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-31","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-style#make-it-easy-to-read","content":"Carry the same voice through technical sections and exercises. Preserve the required action and its conditions, but explain how to do it in connected prose. Accuracy protects the meaning; it does not require keeping a stiff sentence or a cluster of commands unchanged."},"3237":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-32","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-style#make-it-easy-to-read","content":"Keep the rhythm natural. A paragraph can follow the author through a situation, his reaction, and the reason for his advice. It does not have to end with a slogan or a question. Leave room for a short aside or meaningful repetition when the source earns it."},"3238":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-33","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-style#make-it-easy-to-read","content":"Keep the book's compact pace. Expand a passage when the reasoning is missing, and cut repeated instructions or summaries that add nothing. Do not pad chapters to reach a page count, and do not compress a useful explanation just to keep it short."},"3239":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-34","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-style#keep-the-reasoning-and-personality-together","content":"The recordings often move from a concrete situation to the author's reaction, then to advice and a qualification or admission. Preserve that movement where it exists. His admission that he has spent money before a freelance payment arrived gives weight to his warning about doing it. Extracting only the warning loses part of the explanation."},"3240":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-35","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-style#keep-the-reasoning-and-personality-together","content":"Keep specific details that do useful work: school pickup, the car someone wants, a sale that tempts him to break his own rule. Keep his humor and firm opinions when supported by the notes. Do not soften every reaction into neutral advice, and do not manufacture a stronger opinion than he expressed. “Bro,” questions, and profanity cannot substitute for his way of thinking through the problem."},"3241":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-36","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-style#keep-the-reasoning-and-personality-together","content":"When the source only asks a question and leaves the answer for research, explain the verified answer through that practical question. Do not invent a personal experience to make a researched section feel conversational. Necessary conditions, risks, and country-specific limits remain beside the claims they qualify."},"3242":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-37","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-style#what-to-preserve-from-the-recordings","content":"Keep the author's point of view, faith, personal experience, and respect for different goals. Keep a blunt phrase or a casual word when it carries his voice. Use profanity only when it comes from the source and belongs in the passage; do not add it to manufacture personality."},"3243":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-38","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-style#what-to-preserve-from-the-recordings","content":"Words such as “stuff,” “things,” “bro,” and “that's okay” can stay when they fit the passage. Do not replace every everyday word with formal language. Use a precise financial term where the distinction matters, and explain it. Slang and profanity are occasional choices, not a quota or a substitute for an explanation."},"3244":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-39","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-style#what-to-preserve-from-the-recordings","content":"Keep God, church, and the author's Christian perspective where he brings them into the material. Preserve his stated belief and its scope. Keep his admissions about debt and mistakes too. Do not give him a polished success story or authority that he hasn't claimed."},"3245":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-40","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-style#what-to-preserve-from-the-recordings","content":"Use stories and numbers the author supplied. Label invented worked examples as examples and use generic $ amounts, as requested in Plan decisions. Keep real source figures intact with their original context. Use one currency within a calculation; generic dollars do not make legal or tax rules universal."},"3246":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-41","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-style#how-to-turn-speech-into-prose","content":"Keep the full transcript in Raw thoughts. In the book draft, remove false starts and repeated filler. Add punctuation and paragraph breaks. Keep repetition when it adds emphasis or explains an idea."},"3247":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-42","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-style#how-to-turn-speech-into-prose","content":"Keep a repeated phrase when the second use earns its place. Note 9's “they stack up” makes accumulated payments feel concrete; several repeats of “super” can usually become one. Remove recording logistics such as “as this is being transcribed” from reader-facing prose. Use a settled correction in the draft, while keeping its original wording and correction together in the raw note."},"3248":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-43","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-style#how-to-turn-speech-into-prose","content":"Keep production commentary out of the chapter's explanation. Put transcript timestamps, uncertain source wording, reconstruction history, and reminders to the editor in the end source notes. Keep source links there so passages remain traceable. A brief label such as “a fictional example in generic dollars” belongs beside a worked example; a sentence about what was or was not checked in the recording does not. Factual citations can stay beside the claims they support without interrupting the author to describe the research process."},"3249":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-44","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-style#how-to-turn-speech-into-prose","content":"Do not guess at unclear transcription. Mark it for review. Do not invent a personal story, a result, a belief, or a fact to make a paragraph stronger."},"3250":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-45","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-style#how-to-turn-speech-into-prose","content":"The author's claims still need evidence. If a new recording disagrees with a drafted section, record the conflict and resolve it from the source or ask one focused question."},"3251":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-46","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-style#how-to-turn-speech-into-prose","content":"Confidence of delivery does not settle a claim. Keep personal rules labeled as personal rules. Check numbers and financial explanations through the research record. Source an anecdote before writing it in the first person; never add an experience just to make the prose sound more personal."},"3252":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-47","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-style#a-voice-example","content":"Edited from Note 2:"},"3253":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-48","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-style#a-voice-example","content":"> We have ups and we have downs. You can earn a good income and still struggle to get to the next payday, so I want you to have something you can use wherever you are right now. Work through the part that fits your situation, put it into practice, and come back when you need the next part. You're probably not going to remember every single thing in here anyway."},"3254":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-49","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-style#a-voice-example","content":"This is a draft example of the author's voice, not a quote from the recording."},"3255":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-50","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-style#more-examples-from-the-recordings","content":"These are short edited voice samples, not verbatim quotations or finished chapter passages. They keep the source's meaning while removing restarts."},"3256":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-51","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-style#more-examples-from-the-recordings","content":"From Note 4, around 03:08–03:47:"},"3257":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-52","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-style#more-examples-from-the-recordings","content":"> Okay, so let's work backwards. Where would you love to be five or ten years from now? What does that life look like? Then we can figure out what you need to do to get there."},"3258":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-53","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-style#more-examples-from-the-recordings","content":"From Note 9, around 14:01–14:36:"},"3259":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-54","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-style#more-examples-from-the-recordings","content":"> Sometimes you don't have enough money to pay all your bills. What happens then? I'm not going to sit here and pretend that never happens. When I'm in that situation, I look at what I absolutely need to pay."},"3260":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-55","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-style#more-examples-from-the-recordings","content":"From Note 9, around 25:04–end:"},"3261":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-56","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-style#more-examples-from-the-recordings","content":"> I love cars, and I'm not going to pretend I don't care which one I drive. They make me happy. You might care about something completely different, and that's fine, because your quality of life matters too. But if getting the car means you can't enjoy driving it because you're worried about the payments, we need to look at that before you buy it."},"3262":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-57","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-style#more-examples-from-the-recordings","content":"The first sample thinks through a goal with the reader. The second keeps the author's experience beside the difficult situation. The third lets him have a preference without turning it into a rule for everyone."},"3263":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-58","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-style#book-prose-and-spoken-scripts","content":"Use the same voice in both. In the book, trim enough repetition that the reader can follow the thought on the first pass. In a spoken script, leave room for a pause, an aside, or a repeated line that helps someone listening. Do not turn the book into formal finance copy or the scripts into an exaggerated version of the author."},"3264":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-59","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-style#book-prose-and-spoken-scripts","content":"Before finishing a passage, read it aloud and check:"},"3265":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-60","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-style#book-prose-and-spoken-scripts","content":"Does this sound like something the author could say in Notes 1–9?"},"3266":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-61","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-style#book-prose-and-spoken-scripts","content":"Did the edit keep his opinion, faith, admissions, and meaningful conditions?"},"3267":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-62","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-style#book-prose-and-spoken-scripts","content":"Is the example concrete, with its source and any uncertainty accounted for?"},"3268":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-63","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-style#book-prose-and-spoken-scripts","content":"Did the edit remove filler without removing all the conversational rhythm?"},"3269":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-64","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-style#book-prose-and-spoken-scripts","content":"Is each personal claim supported by his source material, and each factual claim checked?"},"3270":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-65","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-style#book-prose-and-spoken-scripts","content":"Can a reader new to this subject follow why one thought leads to the next?"},"3271":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-66","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-style#book-prose-and-spoken-scripts","content":"Have production notes stayed out of the explanation, with necessary factual conditions still visible?"},"3272":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-67","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-style#book-prose-and-spoken-scripts","content":"Apply this guide when drafting and revising the chapters and scripts in the writing plan. Track completed revisions there; a guide change alone does not establish that a chapter or script has been revised."},"3273":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-68","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-style#supplied-style-references","content":"The author supplied these links with the reading-level and sentence rules: YouTube short, TikTok video, and writing-guidelines post."},"3274":{"id":"/docs/writing-style-69","page_id":"/docs/writing-style","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/writing-style#supplied-style-references","content":"Those references record the earlier approach. The author's later direction replaces the grade target and sentence/comma rules. Use his recordings and the clarity checks above to govern the prose; do not imitate the public figure in the references."}},"count":3274},"sorting":{"languag