ain supporting source notes; the chapter holds the developed exercise and scoped research."],[3174,"**Chapters 5–7 — Understand the bill and the borrowing.** Explain the terms before using the author's rewards and spending examples. Chapter 5 owns borrowing comparisons, including overdrafts and lines of credit. Chapter 6 owns card costs, statements, minimum payments, and the payment decision. Chapter 7 owns scores and reports. Proposed script sequence: what you borrowed → what it costs → what is due → what gets reported. Exercise: a card/borrowing record with balance labels, rate, fees, minimum due, and dates."],[679,"**Check each amount.** Your starting point is page 3, “What I owe.” Add the details that will help you plan: the creditor, balance date, purpose, rate, fees, required payment, due date, and any overdue or disputed amounts. Include collateral, rate changes, and early-payment terms so those conditions stay beside the balance. List anything you still need to find out."],[1091,"**Check the plan when you're paid.** Compare what actually arrived with what you expected, and confirm the bills before making payments or transfers. Make only those the available funds can support, then record what you did. Review the plan before the next payment comes in, or sooner if something changes."],[346,"**Check the records.** Put the report beside your own records and compare the account names, balances, limits, payment history, and any unfamiliar inquiries. If something appears wrong, write down the exact item and the evidence that makes you question it. That will help you explain the possible error when you follow up."],[268,"**Check the result.** When the payment should have posted, check whether it did and record the result. Update your debt record with the amount now owed and set the next review date, so the plan follows what happened in the account."],[683,"**Check what changed.** Set a date to review the plan after payments. When you come back to it, record new charges, the payments you actually made, and the closing balances. If the debt grew, work out why before repeating the same plan."],[266,"**Choose a funded payment.** Work out what's available for this card after your other commitments, then record that amount alongside the payment you choose, the bank account it will come from, and the date. Check your payday plan as you do this: if it already includes the payment, don't add it again."],[682,"**Choose a next step.** If you have affordable extra money, name the debt it will go toward and why. If you don't, write the next contact or income action instead. Give the action a date and record any agreement that has actually been confirmed, so you know what you can rely on."],[347,"**Choose one action.** Based on what you found, decide whether the next step is to check with the provider, submit a supported dispute, or adjust an existing payment plan. Give that action a date and keep the response, so you can see whether it resolved the issue."],[936,"**Choose one next step.** Look over what you've written and choose one number to check or one change to explore. Give that action a date, then set a date to review the whole page too. Prices and your circumstances can change, so you'll need to come back to the estimate as you learn more. We aren't fixing the next ten years of your life on this sheet."],[1087,"**Choose the period you're planning for.** Put the start and end dates at the top, along with the currency and whose money you're including. Write the cash available at the start, showing what you've left out because it's reserved elsewhere. List future receipts separately with their dates and how certain they are. Account for any business costs or tax still to cover once, so you can see what you're starting with and what still has to arrive."],[935,"**Compare like with like.** Add your monthly costs, payments, and saving to get the target. Beside it, put income and support for the same person or household and the same period, allowing for known costs and tax still to cover. If your income is uneven, use a range from your records so you're looking at more than your best month. Now subtract that income from the target. A positive result shows the gap; a negative result means the listed income is more than the estimated target. Keep any missing costs and payment dates beside the comparison, because that result doesn't settle them."],[2874,"**Current monthly income after those remaining costs/tax: $\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_**"],[1677,"**Debt:** money the reader owes. This includes amounts people might call good debt."],[932,"**Describe the day.** Start with your name or household, today's date, and the date you're working towards. Then write about your morning, your work or other responsibilities, your evening, and your time off. Give yourself enough detail to see what you want your days to look like. As you read it back, mark what matters most and what you'd be willing to change, because those choices will help when you see the cost."],[1676,"**Dollar amounts:** use generic $ amounts in examples. There is no need to choose between USD and CAD for a made-up calculation. Real source amounts keep their context."],[2296,"**Don't have the companion workbook?** A notebook or plain paper works too. The book tells you what to write. You can do every exercise without the workbook. You can also use a document if writing by hand doesn't work for you."],[2019,"**Draft adaptation of Chapter 5.** This is a speaking script, not a transcript. The chapter holds full product explanations and country-specific sources."],[1946,"**Draft spoken adaptation.** Based on Chapter 6 and Note 7, especially 01:05–04:25 and 08:04–11:09. This is edited prose, not a transcript. The example is invented and uses generic dollars; the linked financial guidance is US guidance."],[1964,"**Draft spoken adaptation.** Based on Chapter 7 and the questions in Note 7, around 06:47–07:45 and 09:20–10:33. This is edited prose, not a transcript. The system described is the United States; the worked figures are invented."],[1679,"**Extra topics:** log each question and place it within a chapter as a section. It does not need a page of its own."],[265,"**Find the cost.** Look through the terms for the purchase rate, any other rates that apply, fees, and any promotion end date, and copy them onto the page. Find out whether you have a purchase grace period and what preserves or restores it, then write that down too. For anything unclear, leave yourself the note “ask issuer.” You'll know what still needs an answer before you rely on it."],[681,"**Find the real payment amount.** Bring over Chapter 4's plan for the same period and show your non-debt needs, any reserve, required debt payments, and money left. Check that you've counted each payment once. If the result is negative, write down the shortfall and what you'll do about it; there isn't extra money to allocate yet."],[296,"**First draft · All readers.** This chapter uses the United States as its worked system. Reports, scores, access, and rules differ by country. The exercise starts by finding the system that applies to you."],[212,"**First draft · All readers.** Use your own card terms beside this chapter. The official guidance linked here is for the United States. Dollar examples are invented teaching examples, not current offers."],[393,"**First draft · Chapter 3.**"],[1007,"**First draft · Chapter 4.**"],[569,"**First draft · Chapter 5.**"],[55,"**First draft · Chapter 8.**"],[1089,"**Follow it through the calendar.** Put each receipt and payment on its date, including automatic payments already set up. Allow enough time for a payment to arrive where it's due. Add money in, subtract money out, and carry the balance to the next date, marking whether incoming money has arrived or is still forecast. Now you can see whether the plan works all the way through the period."],[2883,"**Gap: monthly target minus current monthly income after costs/tax = $\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_**"],[3226,"**Give people room.** Note 5 respects stay-at-home parents. Note 9 acknowledges that sometimes the proposed saving amount isn't possible. Keep those conditions beside the advice they qualify, with the same weight as the forceful line."],[1088,"**Give that money somewhere to go.** Bring your required costs, debt payments, and amounts reserved for later bills onto the page. Then include any giving, saving, and other spending you've chosen. If you use a percentage, write what you're taking that percentage of. An unknown amount means the plan is provisional; keep it visible so you know what to check before relying on the remainder."],[615,"**Highest rate first:** you keep up the required payments on your debts and put the extra toward the one with the highest rate, so you're targeting the interest cost."],[688,"**I-D**"],[776,"**I-D** · Income with debt"],[690,"**I-ND**"],[777,"**I-ND** · Income without debt"],[1678,"**Income and debt categories:** give a compact map, concrete examples, and a way to handle several sources. Do not use an occupation as an income type."],[2708,"**Income and support received during \\_\\_\\_\\_\\_: $\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_**"],[2916,"**Income and support received during \\_\\_\\_\\_\\_: $\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_**"],[3225,"**Keep the push and the admission.** Notes 6 and 9 can be blunt. Note 9 also admits that the author doesn't get his own purchase rule right every time. Preserve both sides of that voice. A warning should explain the decision and its consequence; do not invent insults or make every section a telling-off."],[933,"**List the costs.** Beside each part of the life you want, write an amount, how often you'd pay it, its monthly share, and where the estimate came from. Use one currency throughout so you can add the figures. Include saving and payments you expect to owe, and note who would cover any shared costs. If you don't know an amount yet, leave that gap visible rather than letting it disappear from the plan."],[934,"**List the one-time goals.** For each goal, write the full cost, the money already set aside for it, what you still need, and how many months are left. If you've already set aside enough, you have zero left to find. Otherwise, divide what you still need by the months left and include that monthly amount in saving once. Check which money you've assigned to each goal; the same savings can't fund two things at once. If you don't know the cost or timing, mark the result incomplete. And if the goal is due now, write down the immediate shortfall. There are no months left to spread it across, so dividing by zero won't help you plan for it."],[3223,"**Make the situation visible.** Note 4 gives Michelle a workday, children to pick up, and things she wants to afford. Note 9 uses bills, headphones, a camera, and a payment that hasn't arrived. Show the choice in someone's day before naming an abstract principle."],[680,"**Mark urgent consequences.** For each payment, write what could happen if you miss it. Add any notices and deadlines, then decide whom you need to contact and by when. This is how you'll see what needs attention before choosing where extra money goes."],[3178,"**Michelle and the income examples:** settle her fictional background; recheck the cleaning amount ($20 or $200 a week); resolve the $6,500/$600 target change; rebuild the tax, sales, and app-subscription examples. Keep gross revenue, business costs, taxes, and personal income separate. Do not carry over guaranteed audience or earnings claims."],[143,"**Money available today.** Start with cash you can use, then subtract money already assigned to other commitments once. Keep expected payments separate until they arrive. If you've saved specifically for this purchase, include that fund in the available total; don't subtract it with savings for other purposes or add it a second time. You want to see the money this decision can use."],[2704,"**Money I have now: $\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_**"],[2961,"**Money I have now: $\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_**"],[2712,"**Money I owe: $\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_**"],[2992,"**Money I owe: $\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_**"],[3055,"**Monthly target: A + B = $\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_**"],[458,"**My limits.** Think through what it would take to fit this step into your life. Write down the hours you have, care needs, travel, work requirements, and how much money you can afford to put into it. If there's no money available, write “none.” That belongs in the plan because it affects which options you can try."],[457,"**My starting point.** Write your reader path and the sources you already have, then say whether you need money for now or are working towards a change for later. If you've done Chapter 2, bring over your target and gap so you can refer to them as you compare choices. If you haven't, you can still start here with the need you have today."],[692,"**N-D**"],[779,"**N-D** · No income with debt"],[694,"**N-ND**"],[780,"**N-ND** · No income without debt"],[344,"**Name the purpose.** Start with why you're checking: you might be looking for errors, preparing for an application, or simply learning what's on file. Write that purpose along with the country and the official source you'll use, so the check fits the decision you need to make."],[460,"**One action.** Choose something you can do by a date, such as finishing an application, having a pay or customer conversation, or checking with a support provider. Give it a time limit and a spending limit, so you've decided how much you can put into this step before you start."],[1226,"**Open the Chapter 1 PDF**"],[1228,"**Open the four-page workbook PDF**"],[3179,"**Payday examples:** the first settled illustration is $5,000 take-home minus $2,500 bills minus $1,250 saving, leaving $1,250. The smaller-surplus example changes from $500 to $300 to $400 in speech; choose and label one set of assumptions. Specify whether a percentage applies to take-home income, income after giving, or the remainder after bills. For the tithing example, $5,000 minus $500 leaves $4,500 before bills and saving; rebuild the rest explicitly rather than reusing the original remainder."],[2981,"**Payment dates, interest, fees, and terms are optional for now.** Write them down if you know them. If you don't, leave the box blank or write “not sure yet.” We'll go through the details later in the book."],[369,"**Placeholder · Chapter 10.** The full draft is still to come."],[169,"**Placeholder · Chapter 11.** The full draft is still to come."],[962,"**Placeholder · Chapter 12.** The full draft is still to come."],[982,"**Placeholder · Chapter 9.** The full draft is still to come."],[3144,"**Progress, October 1:** All five steps below have first drafts. Raw Notes 4–9 remain intact. Each developed chapter has a spoken adaptation, a plain-paper exercise, companion pages, and scoped primary-source checks in Research notes."],[1680,"**Promise and title:** leave them open."],[3181,"**Purchases and shortfalls:** define the limits of the author's personal rules. Research full ownership costs, BNPL terms, payment-date changes, and consequences of missed payments before providing step-by-step guidance."],[264,"**Read the record.** Give the card a nickname and write its issuer and country, leaving full account numbers off the worksheet. Then bring over the statement date, current-balance date, balances, pending activity, minimum, and due date. Keeping those dates beside the amounts will help you tell what the bill covers and what has happened since."],[345,"**Record what you got.** Write the bureau, the report date, and the date shown for the item you're reviewing. If there's a score too, record its model, provider, data source, date, and scale where shown. Use “not shown” for anything you can't see. You can still do useful work with the report alone."],[3224,"**Say what you think.** Keep “for me,” “I think,” and “what I would do” when they distinguish personal judgment from a fact. Note 9 explicitly calls the tithing passage a personal belief. Do not edit those qualifications into universal instructions."],[267,"**See what remains.** Estimate what you'll owe after the payment, marking any interest you don't yet know and any pending charges. Use that picture to decide how you'll handle new spending. If you can't meet the required amount, your next step is the contact action discussed above; write what you'll do and when."],[348,"**Set a review point.** Write what you're waiting for and when you'll follow up. If you're doing this before an application, bring its estimated costs and payments into your money plan too; checking the report is one part of preparing for that decision."],[616,"**Smallest balance first:** you keep up the required payments and put the extra toward clearing the smallest balance. Finishing that one may help you keep going, though this approach can cost more interest."],[1367,"**Source recording:** `DJI_20260929090354_0054_D.MP4`\\\n**Recording duration:** 22:17.436 (minutes:seconds)."],[1417,"**Source recording:** `DJI_20260929092656_0056_D.MP4`\\\n**Recording duration:** 3:17.197 (minutes:seconds)."],[1436,"**Source recording:** `DJI_20260929093028_0057_D.MP4`\\\n**Recording duration:** 2:12.332 (minutes:seconds)."],[1462,"**Source recording:** `DJI_20260930081334_0060_D.MP4`\\\n**Recording duration:** 11:28.832 (minutes:seconds)."],[1496,"**Source recording:** `DJI_20260930082509_0061_D.MP4`\\\n**Recording duration:** 0:44.651 (minutes:seconds)."],[1535,"**Source recording:** `Tennis Court.m4a`\\\n**Recording duration:** 26:46.165 (minutes:seconds)."],[1981,"**Spoken adaptation · First draft.** Based on Chapter 3. This is developed prose, not a transcript of a recording."],[2072,"**Spoken adaptation · First draft.** Based on Chapter 4. This is developed prose, not a raw transcript."],[1913,"**Spoken adaptation · First draft.** Based on Chapter 8. This is developed prose, not a raw transcript."],[3221,"**Start with the question someone would ask.** Note 4 asks what the plan was. Note 9 asks what happens when there isn't enough to pay the bills. Use a question to open the actual problem, then spend time answering it. Avoid adding a question to every paragraph."],[144,"**The choices and full cost.** Put buying this option beside a suitable alternative and, where possible, waiting. For each, include purchase charges, financing costs, and the costs of using it over the same period. Mark any estimates and details you still need to check, so the comparison doesn't look settled before it is."],[146,"**The decision.** Now choose: buy, save first, choose another option, wait, or get more information. Write your reason, what money would remain after the purchase, and your next action or review date. If you've used my twice-the-price rule, label it as a personal check for an unplanned purchase. You still need the rest of the picture you've just worked through."],[924,"**The household amount needed for these assumptions**"],[145,"**The payment dates.** Place the upfront amount and every new instalment beside your existing commitments. Keep the full obligation on a separate line, as we did with the headphones. If a later payment depends on income that hasn't arrived, write down that risk and what you'd do if the income were late."],[142,"**The purchase and the reason.** Write what you want, what it would add to your life, and whether you've planned for it or only just started thinking about it. Then describe what would happen if you waited. That will help you separate something you want today from a need you have to deal with now."],[922,"**Total monthly target**"],[637,"**Total**"],[662,"**Total**"],[459,"**Two options.** For each option, write who would pay you, what you'd do, and the costs and time involved. Beside that, explain what evidence you have and what you still don't know, so you can see what needs checking. Keep expected earnings separate from your current income. If only one option is feasible, record why; you don't need to invent a second one to fill the page."],[3222,"**Walk through it together.** “Okay, so,” “let's say,” “right?” and “now” are natural parts of the recordings. Keep a few where they move the explanation along or invite the reader to check the reasoning. Do not sprinkle them into every sentence."],[1090,"**Work through any gap.** If the balance goes below zero or a need remains uncovered, take it to page 12. Write what could happen, whom you'll contact, what changes they've confirmed, and what remains unpaid. Go back through your choices before treating any remainder as money you can spend."],[2934,"**Workbook page 1 · Chapter 1, Step 1**"],[2759,"**Workbook page 10 · Chapter 4, Steps 1–2**"],[2365,"**Workbook page 11 · Chapter 4, Steps 3 and 5**"],[3063,"**Workbook page 12 · Chapter 4, Step 4**"],[2570,"**Workbook page 13 · Chapter 5, Steps 1–2**"],[2611,"**Workbook page 14 · Chapter 5, Steps 3–5**"],[2452,"**Workbook page 15 · Chapter 6, Steps 1–2**"],[2414,"**Workbook page 16 · Chapter 6, Steps 3–5**"],[2520,"**Workbook page 17 · Chapter 7**"],[2812,"**Workbook page 18 · Chapter 8**"],[2900,"**Workbook page 2 · Chapter 1, Step 2**"],[2977,"**Workbook page 3 · Chapter 1, Step 3**"],[2700,"**Workbook page 4 · Chapter 1, Step 4**"],[2493,"**Workbook page 5 · Chapter 2, Step 1**"],[3004,"**Workbook page 6 · Chapter 2, Steps 2–3**"],[2865,"**Workbook page 7 · Chapter 2, Steps 4–5**"],[2659,"**Workbook page 8 · Chapter 3, Steps 1–3**"],[2727,"**Workbook page 9 · Chapter 3, Steps 4–5**"],[775,"**You have income or support coming in**"],[778,"**You have no current income or support source**"],[865,"*First draft. Based on Note 3 and Note 4. Sources and example assumptions are at the end.*"],[721,"*First draft. The source notes are at the end.*"],[1109,"*Source direction: Note 3 and General plan. This section develops a practical tool check; it does not claim budgeting apps generally fail or recommend a specific product.*"],[1104,"*Source: Note 9, 00:00–05:06, including the author's correction to plan bills before choosing a saving amount.*"],[1105,"*Source: Note 9, 03:02–06:04 and 17:00–18:42. The calendar method is supported by the CFPB cash-flow tool, a US consumer-education resource.*"],[1106,"*Source: Note 9, 05:06–09:10. The smaller example settles one set of assumptions from figures that change in the recording.*"],[1107,"*Source: Note 9, 10:03–13:00. The belief and practice are the author's. The completed calculation is illustrative; it makes no promise of a financial return for giving.*"],[1108,"*Source: Note 9, 14:01–18:42. The shortfall arithmetic is a new fictional illustration. The unclear passage around 16:29 is not quoted.*"],[159,"*Source: Note 9, 19:02–end, especially the quality-of-life discussion at 25:04. The urgency questions are editorial steps for applying the idea.*"],[160,"*Source: Note 9, 20:08–21:06. The recording corrects $500 to $250 and separates unplanned from saved-for purchases. Checking already assigned money is an explicit addition to make the rule usable with the payday plan.*"],[161,"*Source: Note 9, 21:06–23:02. The personal pattern comes from the recording; the amounts and computer example retain their illustrative status.*"],[162,"*Source: Note 9, 23:02–25:04. The recording's unnamed or unclear brand reference is omitted. The payment schedule is newly invented; it does not promise zero-cost borrowing.*"],[163,"*Source: Note 9, 25:04–end. The full-cost comparison is an editorial extension supported by the cited guidance; the author's doubling and saving figures remain personal checks.*"],[2046,"*Spoken draft adapted from Chapter 2. Read with the chapter's worked table and workbook pages 5–7 available. This is edited prose, not a transcript or a recording of the author.*"],[2506,"\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_"],[2508,"\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_"],[2510,"\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_"],[1115,"<Card title=\"Book plan\" href=\"/docs/outline\" description=\"Twelve chapters and the