port checks. Keep the author's urgency and his student/stay-at-home-parent qualifications; do not turn them into an exhaustive list of who can be unable to work. No earnings or hiring outcome is established by these recordings."],[696,"First draft, October 1, 2026. Note 3 supplies the debt questions, starting paths, and personal admission. Note 7, 04:33–06:37 and 10:11–11:19, supplies borrowing comparisons to develop. Note 9, 14:01–18:42, supplies the shortfall discussion. Researched additions are linked where used; Canadian product examples and U.S. legal processes keep their scope."],[301,"First, let's separate the report from the score, because they're easy to mix up. A **credit report** is a record that can contain your accounts, balances, payment history, and requests to check your credit. Credit reporting companies collect that information from lenders and other sources. In the US, the three nationwide bureaus are Equifax, Experian, and TransUnion, and their records can differ because a lender doesn't have to report to all three. CFPB: credit reports, Consumer.gov: checking your report."],[2689,"Fit with my schedule and responsibilities"],[1691,"Five groups in that research tool, not the workbook's four. No population statistics are copied into the chapter."],[2591,"Fixed or variable; promotional end date, if any"],[762,"Follow how the debt came about when choosing its group. A student loan from the government is still borrowed money. If a court order concerns an existing debt, the order doesn't give you a second copy of that debt to count. The same goes for a loan you've used to pay off a bill: record the loan that's left, and take the paid bill out of your total. If you're disputing an amount, keep it on a separate line while you check it so it stays visible without getting mixed into the confirmed amounts."],[3199,"Follow the writing guide and compare new prose with Notes 1–9. The October 1 voice update applies to both chapters and spoken scripts: keep the 