nt I'd consider. But I mean if you can afford it. If that leaves you short for food or rent, the percentage doesn't work for you. You might start much smaller, and there will be periods when you can't put anything away. That's why we looked at the bills first."],[2079,"If you can afford to save twenty-five percent of the full five thousand, that's one thousand two hundred and fifty. You have one thousand two hundred and fifty left to allocate after those costs and saving."],[133,"If you can comfortably keep up with the payments, you can consider the option after comparing the total cost and reading the terms. The important part is looking at all your commitments together, instead of deciding from the size of this one instalment."],[817,"If you can't find a number, “to check” is enough for now. Leave that note where you can see it until you find the record; writing zero would tell us something different."],[261,"If you can't make the minimum, contact the issuer promptly, explain what you can afford, and ask what help is available. If they propose an arrangement, you need to understand what happens to interest, fees, future payments, card use, and reporting. Get the terms in writing before relying on them, because making the call by itself doesn't mean a change has been approved. CFPB: act fast if you can't pay."],[1956,"If you can't make the minimum, write that down too. Contact the issuer and explain what you can pay. Ask what help is available and what the terms would be. Don't assume help has been agreed just because you've asked. CFPB: payment difficulty."],[1058,"If you divide the monthly saving amount in half and take $625 out of that first payment, you'll have $1,875 left for $2,000 of bills. You're $125 short before the next payday, even though the monthly plan looked fine. This is why knowing the total isn't enough: we need to know when the money arrives and when it has to leave."],[489,"If you don't have the companion workbook, a notebook or plain paper is 