over are deducted once or marked unknown."],[1785,"No reader is assumed eligible, approved, or certain to receive a payment."],[1782,"No tax percentage, deductible-cost ruling, or universal worker classification is given. Generic dollars do not make US rules apply everywhere."],[1250,"Not everyone is meant to be a business owner or a full-time employee."],[1300,"Note 1"],[3218,"Note 1 gives the stance: “I'm also still trying to figure it out as well.” Keep that openness. Do not turn the author into someone who has solved every money problem."],[2267,"Note 1 mentions the author's investing experience and his mother's real estate experience. Those are leads for stories, not evidence for an investment claim."],[1308,"Note 2"],[2277,"Note 2 makes room for changes over time. Someone's current situation does not describe the rest of their life."],[3219,"Note 2 says, “We have ups and we have downs.” The author talks about money as something that changes over a person's life. He gives the reader room to start again."],[1322,"Note 3"],[177,"Note 3 — questions, examples, and starting-path logic."],[376,"Note 3 — questions, examples, and starting-path logic."],[972,"Note 3 — questions, examples, and starting-path logic."],[989,"Note 3 — questions, examples, and starting-path logic."],[2165,"Note 3 adds questions about collections, debt collectors, tax bills, loans, and lines of credit. These are planned sections of Chapter 5, alongside borrowing from and lending to friends."],[2265,"Note 3 also asks about index funds, bonds, Treasuries, private equity, and hedge funds. Chapter 12 includes these within its sections, with private equity and hedge funds as reference material."],[2127,"Note 3 asks when someone needs a budget and how to set one."],[2240,"Note 3 describes losing a job during a honeymoon, moving to New York, and facing higher bills while carrying debt. It can ground the no-income path. Confirm the timeline and figures before using the full story."],[3220,"Note 3 moves from an idea to a specific life: getting up, going to the gym, taking the kids to school, working, and eating with family. Keep that habit of making an idea concrete."],[1837,"Note 3 supplies the author's admission of debt after job loss and moving; no recovery is invented. Notes 7 and 9 supply the borrowing and shortfall questions. Uncertain exchange-rate amounts and the unfinished line-of-credit recommendation remain excluded."],[1789,"Note 3 supplies the goal: income serves the chosen life. Chapter 3 does not require every reader with existing income to increase it."],[2223,"Note 3 supplies the pay-cheque, saving, and giving questions. General plan makes this a core topic."],[1340,"Note 4"],[1791,"Note 4 at 04:11–08:22 supports the Starbucks conversation, vehicle analogy, and founder-versus-product reflection. The chapter retells it without a direct adviser quote, uncertain company name, or claim that a career change has already succeeded."],[1792,"Note 4 at 15:02–20:03 supplies the idea of exploring writing/business routes. Audience-growth guarantees, speculative conversions, guessed tax, and inconsistent subscription calculations are excluded. They remain raw source material."],[3120,"Note 4, 00:00–04:25 and 08:45–15:23"],[3123,"Note 4, 04:25–08:45 and 15:23–21:15"],[1887,"Note 4, Chapters 2–3"],[1409,"Note 5"],[3126,"Note 5 and Note 6"],[1429,"Note 6"],[1446,"Note 7"],[1853,"Note 7 supplies the author's admission and rewards concern. The uncertain personal offer, remembered book, generic rate range, garbled spending line, and universal 30-day language are excluded. New examples do not reproduce an actual card statement."],[3129,"Note 7, 00:55–06:37 and 07:50–11:19"],[3132,"Note 7, 06:47–07:45 and 09:20–10:33"],[1893,"Note 7, Chapter 7"],[1891,"Note 7, Chapters 5–6"],[1871,"Note 7's Equifax correction is retained. Its unclear report/application language and 720/750 assertions are not endorsed. Fictional calculations: $600 / $2,000 = 30% on one card; $600 / ($2,000 + $3,000) = 12% combined, not the average of two percentages. A $450 proposed payment against $200 available leaves a $250 gap. The paid-account $400 dispute example and follow-up date do not promise a correction or score change."],[3184,"Note 7's opening comment about leaving debt until the end is ambiguous and may concern recording order. Keep Chapter 5 in its current place. Note 4's abandoned wife anecdote stays in the raw transcript but should not lead the developed chapter. These choices preserve the settled plan while recording the unresolved ideas."],[1491,"Note 8"],[3135,"Note 8, plus Note 4 at 21:15–end and Note 7 at 09:42–10:13"],[1504,"Note 9"],[3138,"Note 9, 00:00–18:42"],[2222,"Note 9, 00:00–18:42, now supplies the main payday prose: plan mandatory bills first, choose affordable saving, preserve the author's giving practice, and face shortfalls."],[3141,"Note 9, 18:42–end"],[1895,"Note 9, Chapter 4"],[1897,"Note 9, Chapter 4 faith passage"],[1899,"Note 9, Chapter 8"],[1814,"Note 9's correction at 03:02 puts bill planning before choosing a saving percentage. The draft preserves the mother's advice, habit-building aim, conditional 25–30% suggestion, Christian tithing practice, and admission of shortfalls. It excludes unclear speech near 16:29, assumed universal pay frequency, guessed phone-plan prices, and claims about guaranteed returns from giving. No biblical text is quoted or interpreted."],[1882,"Note 9's threshold correction to $250, saved-for exception, admissions, expected freelance-payment pattern, and enjoyment of cars are retained. “Afford it twice” remains the author's personal check, not an established affordability formula. Editorial steps protect money already assigned elsewhere and distinguish urgent needs. The $10,000/$2,000 freelance telling is illustrative, not asserted as a checked transaction. The unclear BNPL brand is omitted."],[2210,"Notes 1 and 2 explain that the author is not promising to make every reader a millionaire. This section can connect that stance to the reader's own goals and the work needed to reach them."],[1159,"Notes 4–9 add source material for Chapters 2–8. The October 1 incorporation plan maps passages, proposed script outlines, exercises, drafting order, and checks still needed. Chapters 2–8 are developed as first drafts from this batch. The recording-based drafting sequence is complete; the drafts still need author review and final print layout."],[1298,"Notes 4–9 preserve the six recordings added in this batch, with source filenames, time markers and wording to check. The incorporation plan maps their material to the chapters and future scripts."],[3183,"Notes 4, 7, 8, and 9 explore online exercises, QR links, reminders, and an email/AI question service. Keep these in the backlog until the author chooses a format. No domain, contact address, app, or response service is promised. Keep the current optional workbook and plain-paper path. Spoken suggestions are source material, not authorization to build, publish, or send anything."],[1790,"Notes 5–6 supply the direct push to seek work and consider temporary roles. The student and stay-at-home-parent qualifications stay beside the advice. The chapter adds practical checks for health, access, care costs, and permission to work; these are editorial guidance, not new personal stories."],[1889,"Notes 5–6, Chapter 3"],[188,"Notes for each reader group"],[1243,"Notes:"],[1037,"Notice what changed: in the first example, we saved a percentage of the whole take-home amount. In the second, we saved a percentage of what remained after required costs. Saying “I save 25%” doesn't tell us which one you mean. Write the dollar amount beside the percentage so you can see what the decision leaves you with."],[126,"November 21"],[122,"November 7"],[2023,"Now add the details. Who gets paid? How much is due? When? What rate and fees apply? Is anything already overdue? Is there a car or home pledged as backup?"],[821,"Now add the income entries for your chosen month and write **“Income and support received during \\_\\_\\_\\_\\_: $\\_\\_\\_\\_\\_.”** Keep the costs and tax still to cover beside the result, with other cash receipts on their own line. Keep this total separate from today's account balance as well. If your pay is already in the account, it's already included in that balance; the monthly income total tells us how much came in during the period."],[585,"Now get the latest statement or agreement for each balance so we can add the details. The person or company you owe is your **creditor**. Write their name, the balance and the date you checked it, then the amount due and its due date. Look for the rate, fees, and anything already overdue. If you can't find something, write “unknown” and make a note to ask. Leaving it unknown is more useful than planning as though the cost is zero."],[2048,"Now I want you to think about the life you're trying to get to. What does that actually look like?"],[2032,"Now if his income drops, this plan changes. We can't just keep the same extra payment because it looked good on paper last month."],[1086,"Now let's do this with your money. Use workbook **10. My next payment plan**, **11. My bill calendar**, and **12. When money falls short**. If you're using plain paper, give three sheets those same headings and work through the steps below."],[2052,"Now let's look at Michelle. She's the fictional person in this chapter. She wants to spend her working day thinking, researching, and writing. Being available for her kids matters to her. She also wants a nicer car, cleaning help, and money going into savings."],[1954,"Now let's turn that bill into a plan. Where will the payment come from? What else does that money need to cover? Which date will you send it? And how will you check that it went through?"],[669,"Now suppose Sam's income fell to $2,700. With the same $2,400 costs and $100 reserve, he'd have $200 for $350 of required payments: a **$150 shortfall**, with nothing available for extra repayment. At that point, he needs to revisit the whole payment plan and contact the people he owes. Choosing between repayment methods won't cover the missing money."],[1036,"Now suppose the fictional numbers are tighter: you have $2,000 after tax, and required costs take $1,600 of it. With $400 left, you could choose to save 25% of **that $400 remainder**. That puts $100 into savings and leaves $300 for you to allocate. Measured against the original $2,000, you're saving 5%, even though you used 25% when you worked out what to keep from the remainder."],[90,"Now suppose you have only $350 free because you've been saving that amount for these headphones over time. Since this was a planned purchase, we're checking the plan you made. Has the price changed, or has something happened in your life that means you need the money elsewhere? If your other commitments are still protected and you still want to use the saved $350 this way, you can weigh that choice. Just recognize that buying them would leave no unassigned money in this example. Saving first doesn't remove the need to look at what comes afterward."],[259,"Now take the money available after essential needs and your other commitments, and use that to choose a payment amount. Write down when you'll pay and which account the money will come from, allowing for the issuer's processing time and cutoff. You don't want the due date to be the first time you try to work all of that out."],[1319,"Now the other thing is that this book is not meant to be read back to front, front to back sorry, it's not meant to be read in a linear fashion. You're meant to take the things that you learn and apply them in a certain stage of your life and then come back to this at a later time when you've advanced to the next stage and then you can continue moving."],[931,"Now try that with the life you described. Workbook pages 5: My chosen life, 6: What that life costs, and 7: My target and next step give you space to work through it. If you're using plain paper, use the same page names and follow these steps."],[822,"Now turn to workbook page 3: What I owe, or start a sheet headed **“What I owe.”** For each debt, give yourself a line for who you owe, what it's for, the amount still owed, and the date you checked it. Leave room beside that for any payment details you know."],[876,"Now we can put some amounts beside that life. First decide whether you're planning for your own money or for the household, and use the same choice throughout. If someone else will cover part of a shared cost, write down how much and whether you've agreed on it. We need to know whose money pays for which part."],[861,"Now we can see the gap"],[770,"Now we can use what you've learned to find your starting path. First, do you have income or support coming in? An ongoing source counts even if the payments arrive at uneven times, but make a note if it may stop. If you're living from savings with no current income source, use the no-income row and record the savings beside it. You have money to work with, but we're keeping that separate from having more coming in."],[678,"Now we're going to do this with your debt. You'll use workbook **page 13: My debt details** to understand the amounts, then **page 14: My debt plan** to decide what happens next. Two sheets of paper with the same headings will work just as well."],[1035,"Now you can see what the last $1,250 is available for. You might use some for dinners or other things you enjoy, put more toward debt, or keep more aside. Make that choice while you're looking at the whole plan, so the money doesn't disappear through purchases you never thought about together. You don't have to use it all up."],[1973,"Now you've got an action: check this account, send the record if needed, and follow up. That's something you can do. “Get a perfect score” doesn't tell you what to do today."],[398,"Now, being a stay-at-home parent is a full-time responsibility. If that's 