where you have no debt or you you don't have an income no problem the next level is still to get income that comes over paying off debt once you're earning income then the next level is to get to income with no debt but if you can if you can get income with no debt great that's the level that's the that's the lip that's what where we all want to fundamentally be at the end of the day and along that path of getting there is where you then have to is where you can then start crafting your lifestyle that"],[2558,"Official contact method and date to act: \\_\\_\\_\\_\\_"],[2486,"Official contact method and planned contact date: \\_\\_\\_\\_\\_"],[2525,"Official report-access service or provider for my country: \\_\\_\\_\\_\\_"],[1544,"OK, so let's talk about what to do when you get paid, right? When you get paid, there's a few different things that you have to worry about. Here's how I would sort of categorize these things, yeah? For me, it's about, at least this is what my mom used to always tell me, it's like, when you get paid, pay yourself first. I think that something that unfortunately is the consequence of the society that we live in, where credit is such a huge, huge, huge thing, we're sort of trained that when you get paid, the first thing to do is that you should go and pay off your debts and your bills and all these different things, and there's nothing, to be honest with you, there's nothing inherently wrong with that. But the issue with that is that what ends up happening is that you don't give yourself the opportunity to be able to build some sort of savings or some sort of buffer for your"],[1385,"okay so I'm gonna use this as an opportunity to talk about the part where you reverse engineer the life that you want yeah so essentially what I find is that one of the things that my wife always like not always but used to say to me all the time is that so for example what when my wife and I would get into a tussle because maybe she did something that I didn't understand I would always ask her like what was your plan and then it'd be a back and forth and the reason why I sort of asked that is because I just find that we're not even my wife I think like even with some other people or like sometimes when I"],[1951,"Okay, here's a fictional bill. It closes on April 30 with an $800 statement balance. The minimum is $30, due May 25. Then you make a new $100 purchase on May 3. Nothing else changes. Now the posted balance is $900."],[2027,"Okay, let's say the required payments fit and you have something extra. You could aim it at the highest rate. Or aim it at the smallest balance to get that one finished. Keep the other agreed payments in the plan."],[230,"Okay, let's walk through an invented card bill with ordinary purchases only. The person using it began with no carried balance and still qualifies for the purchase grace period, which we'll explain below. To keep the example focused, there are no fees, cash advances, special payment plans, refunds, or earlier payments."],[1998,"Okay, now do this for yourself. Use workbook pages eight and nine, or plain paper. Write down your starting point and what limits your options. Compare two possible routes if you have two. What do you know, and what do you need to check?"],[1937,"Okay, now pick one purchase and use workbook page eighteen, or plain paper. Name it and say why you want it. Work out the money available after commitments. Compare the choices and their full costs. Put any payments on the calendar."],[928,"Okay, now she has something to work with. She can look at ways to earn more, change part of the life she's priced, or give herself longer to get there. She may also check one of those estimates and find that it was wrong. That's a reason to work through the number again, not to throw away the whole plan."],[836,"Okay, put the pen down for a moment and look at the page. You can see what you have, what comes in, and what you owe, along with the things you still need to check. That's enough to give us somewhere to start. Keep adding to it as you find the missing amounts; a few boxes left blank don't stop you from taking the next step."],[1304,"Okay, so I just want to essentially start this off by saying that with this book that's coming out, you have to understand that as I'm writing this book, I am very much so in debt. I'm blessed because I have a job. I work as a software engineer professionally, so I'm blessed because I have a job that I'm able to earn a lot of money, but I've made a lot of student financial decisions and I have credit card debt, I got student loans debt, but I'm writing this book because even with all of that, at the end of the day, this is something that God has told me to do and I feel like I have a lot of useful tips that could truly, truly help. I have a lot of useful tips that could be helpful for a lot of people because I've learned a lot about finance. I've tried to start, I've worked at a few different fintechs so I know how these apps work. I have built fintech apps before. I've also spent,"],[1424,"Okay, so let's talk about creating a source of income for people that don't have a job. I think that before being able to go out of your way and do all these super ambitious things, I think the first thing that one needs to do is actually get a job. I think that it's hard for you to be able to plan your finances and think about the future if you're not earning money on a regular basis. And the truth is that, again, I feel like it's better for you to work at McDonald's or be a Uber driver or be a food delivery driver than to sit at home and not be making any money. If you're not a student full-time, if you're not like a student, sometimes you just can't"],[297,"Okay, so what is a credit score, and why does this number matter when you want to borrow money?"],[411,"Okay, so you want to write, edit, build an app, or sell a service. Who would pay you, and what would they get? Work backwards from that. If you're going to spend time making something, you need to figure out whether someone wants it enough to pay for it."],[1008,"Okay, so you've been paid. What happens next?"],[2073,"Okay, so you've been paid. What happens next?"],[866,"Okay, so you've put your money on paper. You can see what you have, what comes in, and what you owe. Now where are you trying to go?"],[2047,"Okay, so you've put your money on paper. You know what you have. You can see what comes in and what you owe."],[56,"Okay, so you've seen something you want to buy. Maybe it's a new pair of headphones, a camera, or a car you've wanted for a while. You like it, you can already picture yourself using it, and now you're trying to figure out whether you can afford it. What happens to the rest of your money if you go ahead?"],[684,"On Sam's page 14, you would see $500 available for debt, with $350 needed for required payments and $150 extra going to the card. At his review, he compares the next statements with the $4,970 closing total from our simplified example. You'll make the same comparison using your own terms and dates."],[1074,"On workbook page 11, you can also subtract money when you reserve it for a bill. If you use that option, the running amount tells you what's still available for this plan, which can differ from the balance your bank shows. When the reserved bill is paid, don't subtract it again. You've already accounted for that money."],[613,"Once you've covered current needs and required payments, we can look at whether there's money for extra repayment. Remember the costs you know are coming too. If you send away the money you need for food, you'll still have to figure out how to buy food afterward. We want a payment amount you can manage alongside the rest of your life."],[1021,"Once you've seen what the money needs to cover, you can decide what to put away and what you're saving for. What I care about here is getting into the habit of keeping some of what you earn, when your circumstances allow it