eed another conversation."],[310,"That's why the report and your money plan need to sit beside each other when you're considering an important application."],[1223,"The **book is 6 × 9 inches**. The **companion workbook is 8½ × 11 inches**, with more room to write. The image below shows them at the same scale."],[245,"The $100 purchase happened after April's bill closed, so it belongs to the next billing period. Under these assumptions, paying $800 by May 25 clears the April statement; paying $900 covers that later purchase too. If you pay only the $30 minimum, you still have $870 of the posted balance unpaid before any later interest or activity, and $770 of that is left from the April statement."],[930,"The $2,500 saving choice belongs to Michelle's example; it isn't an amount everyone needs to save. Even if she reaches her monthly income target, she still has to set the money aside over time before her moving fund is ready."],[1767,"The $200 monthly provision for less frequent costs represents $2,400 per year. Actual due dates remain separate."],[1765,"The $6,000 moving goal less $1,200 assigned savings leaves $4,800. Over 24 months that is $200 per month, already included in $2,500 saving. The remaining $2,300 is for other saving goals, including a cash reserve."],[1700,"The $800 / $1,000 example is invented arithmetic, excludes fees and tax, and promises no return."],[1084,"The account you use needs to work with that plan too. Look at how you can access your money and make payments, what support is available, and what fees you could pay. Read the conditions for avoiding monthly charges instead of assuming the account will always be free. As the CFPB explains, some US accounts require things such as a minimum balance or direct deposit to avoid a fee. CFPB account-fee guidance."],[3124,"The adviser conversation, Ferrari/Jeep analogy, work that fits the goal, and breaking an income target into possible actions."],[224,"The amount owed when that billing period clos