nce. The lender gives you a **credit limit**, which tells you how much borrowing it allows. So if the limit is $5,000, that's the borrowing limit on the card. You haven't suddenly made another $5,000 that you can add to your income. Consumer.gov: getting a credit card."],[97,"With buy now, pay later, or BNPL, you get the purchase and spread what you owe across repayments. You're still borrowing the money, even though you're seeing smaller payments at checkout. The US Consumer Financial Protection Bureau describes it as a type of instalment loan. The agreement in front of you tells you how that particular plan works, so check it before accepting. CFPB explanation of BNPL."],[605,"With overdraft protection, the question is where the money comes from when the payment goes through. A transfer from your own savings uses your money, while a transfer from a credit line adds borrowing. Both may involve fees, so check which one you have and what it costs. FCAC: overdraft protection."],[1342,"Wording and source details to check"],[1411,"Wording and source details to check"],[1431,"Wording and source details to check"],[1448,"Wording and source details to check"],[1493,"Wording and source details to check"],[1506,"Wording and source details to check"],[3243,"Words such as “stuff,” “things,” “bro,” and “that's okay” can stay when they fit the passage. Do not replace every everyday word with formal language. Use a precise financial term where the distinction matters, and explain it. Slang and profanity are occasional choices, not a quota or a substitute for an explanation."],[1425,"work and that's completely fine. Again of course, I think the only exceptions to this rule is that if you're a stay-at-home mom, because being a stay-at-home mom, that is your job, it's a full-time responsibility to be responsible for your children, or normally I'll say stay-at-home mom, stay-at-home parent. It's a full-time responsibility to be able to be responsible for your children while your partner is working all the time. And some people, this is what they want and that's completely fine. There's no negative aspect of being a stay-at-home parent because if that's what works for the dynamic of your relationship and if that's what you want for your life, that's completely fine. If that's not you, you need to have something that's getting you income every single week, month, something, something that's making you money."],[3121,"Work backward from a desired life; use the invented Michelle example to include family time, satisfying work, purchases, saving, and household costs."],[170,"Work out what the money is for and what risk you can take."],[548,"Work out what the money is for and what risk you can take."],[1886,"Work required before drafting"],[484,"Work toward your own life"],[2501,"Work, study, care, or other responsibilities"],[2299,"Workbook page"],[2310,"Workbook page"],[2319,"Workbook page"],[2326,"Workbook page"],[2335,"Workbook page"],[2342,"Workbook page"],[2349,"Workbook page"],[2354,"Workbook page"],[1839,"Workbook page 14 starts before bills and debt payments, then subtracts each commitment once. The primary integration review checked the source admission, calculations, worksheets, chapter routes, and the material line-of-credit and validation-notice claims against their primary pages."],[3147,"Workbook pages"],[2120,"Working angle"],[2204,"Working angle"],[15,"Working direction from Note 2 and Note 3: help people make sound money decisions at their current stage and work toward the life they want."],[1301,"Working transcript of the first recording."],[1309,"Working transcript of the second recording."],[1323,"Working transcript of the third recording."],[1420,"Working transcript reviewed as text only. No focused audio check has confirmed the wording."],[1936,"Would you still enjoy the car if its payments took away something else you care about?"],[412,"Write an answer a person could understand, then choose a way to test it with time and money you can afford to use. You might speak to possible customers, show them a sample, or offer one small paid job with a clear scope. You can learn from that before committing more of what you have."],[2091,"Write down the gap. If you have twelve hundred available and fifteen hundred in needs and required payments, you're three hundred short. There's no new saving or optional spending in that example. We can't make the gap disappear by putting it in a different column."],[2197,"Write down your current income and debt beside your estimate. Identify your reader path."],[1930,"Write every payment down. The amount today. The later amounts and dates. The full amount you owe. The terms and any costs. Then put those dates beside the payments you've already agreed to make."],[3228,"Write for an adult who may be new to money topics. Assume intelligence without assuming financial knowledge. Use familiar language, explain unfamiliar terms, and give the reader enough reasoning to follow the decision. A grade score, sentence length, or comma count does not decide whether a passage is clear."],[1278,"Write in active voice."],[2077,"Write it down. Look through the whole month and check for bigger bills coming later. Don't spend on a subscription just because its payment comes out before rent. That rent money is already needed, even while you can see it sitting in your account."],[3216,"Write like the author is talking to one person. Use “you” when speaking to the reader and “I” for the author's own experience. Contractions fit. Plain words fit. The tone is direct and candid, with room for warmth and a short aside."],[2182,"Write out your own day before you put a number on it."],[2931,"Write the money you have today and keep other things you own on a separate list."],[195,"Write the steps in order. Make each one specific enough to carry out."],[1580,"Write what you know for now"],[806,"Write what you know, and if you don't know a number or a word yet, that's okay. Put “not sure yet” 