ng it would mean for the rest of your life."],[1923,"You could buy a suitable two-hundred-and-fifty-dollar pair and keep four hundred and fifty free. You could keep what you have and leave all seven hundred alone. Those are choices. Write down which one you're making and why."],[1315,"You could have a career where you happen to earn a lot of money. Like being a doctor or being an engineer or some sort of lawyer. But then you can also be living paycheck to paycheck and being broken and the Amazon delivery driver simply because of the fact that his expenses might be less than yours."],[723,"You could have a job that pays a lot and still struggle to cover your bills. You could have no job and receive money from somewhere else. So when I ask where you are financially, telling me your job title only gives me part of the picture. We need to look at what happens to the money."],[1427,"You don't have the liberty of being picky. If you're not earning any income on a regular basis, like..."],[739,"You don't have to fit your whole working life into one box. Sort each payment by where it comes from, and use as many groups as you need."],[485,"You don't have to read this book from front to back before you can do anything with it. Start with the part that fits your life right now, whether you have income and debt, have no income coming in, or are trying to work out what to do with the money you have."],[2036,"You don't have to solve every balance today. But you do need to know what happens next."],[728,"You don't have to solve the whole thing while you make the list. For now, get used to looking at the number and saying, “This is where I am today.” If you need to take a break, take one and come back. Then we can work through what money you have, which payment is coming up, and what needs to change."],[2060,"You don't know a number yet? Write that down too. We can come back to it."],[2001,"You don't need to figure out your whole career today. You do need to know what you're going 