 belongs on the page. To decide whether borrowing helped you, we need to know more than the label:"],[617,"You may hear these called the avalanche and snowball methods; the CFPB compares both. We're choosing between them after dealing with urgent consequences, and we still need to check for special terms that could affect the decision, such as a promotional rate about to end or a fee for paying early."],[735,"You may receive interest on savings, dividends from shares, or rent from a property. You may also receive royalties when someone pays to use work or rights you own. The payment comes from something you own, which is why it has its own place on the page."],[590,"You might be offered a loan, a line of credit, or overdraft borrowing. Here's how those common structures generally work, along with what to look for in the actual offer before deciding what it would cost you."],[91,"You might decide the cheaper pair does everything you need, or that you'd rather keep what you have for now. You could also decide the more expensive pair is worth the difference. Write down your reason and the amount you'd have left, so you're deciding with the whole picture in front of you."],[2181,"You might want to get up at 5:30, go to the gym, and take your kids to school. You might want to do your job and get home in time for dinner. That is one of the lives described in the recording. Yours can look different."],[868,"You might want to travel, get home in time to eat with your family, or look at your bank account without worrying about whether the bills are covered. All of those are things we can work towards, but “I want more money” doesn't tell us which one matters to you. We need to get specific enough to picture the life, estimate what it costs, and choose something to work on next."],[2278,"You might want to travel. You might want to know your bills are covered. You might want to be home for dinner with your family."],[726,"You need to see the numbers before you can make a plan 